<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Ball packing stability</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a connected symplectic manifold <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> of finite volume, which may be open or have boundary. For <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-th ball packing number of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math></span>, denoted by <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, quantifies how much of the volume of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be covered by <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint, symplectically embedded balls of equal size. More precisely, the numbers <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> are defined by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M)\coloneqq\sup_{a}\frac{k\cdot\operatorname{vol}(B(a))}{\operatorname{vol}(M)}\in[0,1]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>a</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M)\coloneqq\sup_{a}\frac{k\cdot\operatorname{vol}(B(a))}{\operatorname{vol}(M)}\in[0,1],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the supremum is taken over all <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the disjoint union of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> copies of the closed <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional ball <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(a)=B^{2n}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(a)=B^{2n}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> of symplectic width <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> admits a symplectic embedding into the interior of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. The ball packing numbers serve as a fundamental quantitative measure of symplectic rigidity. Since Gromov’s seminal work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gro85</a>]</cite>, which revealed the first non-trivial obstructions to symplectic embeddings, these invariants have attracted considerable attention. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut11b</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHLS07</a>]</cite> for surveys.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">MP94</a>, Remark 1.5.G]</cite>, McDuff and Polterovich observed that <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}p_{k}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}p_{k}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for every symplectic manifold of finite volume. This implies that symplectic ball packings become asymptotically flexible as the number of balls increases. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir97</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir99</a>]</cite>, Biran proved a much stronger and deeper flexibility result for closed, connected symplectic <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> which are rational, meaning that the symplectic form <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> represents a rational cohomology class <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. He showed that there exists a finite threshold <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. This phenomenon is referred to as <span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symplectic ball packing stability</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A natural problem is to determine which connected symplectic manifolds of finite volume, beyond closed, rational symplectic <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds, exhibit packing stability. This question is explicitly raised in the survey article <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHLS07</a>, §3.2, Problem 4]</cite> by Cieliebak, Latschev, Hofer, and Schlenk, as well as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch</a>, Conjecture 13.2]</cite> by Schlenk. Several results have been established in this direction. It was proved by Buse and Hind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH11</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH13</a>]</cite> that packing stability holds for closed, rational symplectic manifolds of arbitrary dimension. In dimension four, Buse, Hind, and Opshtein <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>]</cite> eliminated the rationality assumption, showing that packing stability holds for all closed, connected symplectic <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds. In higher dimensions, however, the general case remains open. Moreover, packing stability has been verified for certain open manifolds with highly symmetric boundary, including balls and ellipsoids <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH13</a>]</cite>, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polydisks and pseudoballs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>]</cite>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional rational convex toric domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHMP25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the positive results described above, Cristofaro-Gardiner and Hind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHa</a>]</cite> recently identified the first examples of open symplectic manifolds of finite volume for which packing stability fails. They constructed open domains <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, diffeomorphic to the open <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-ball, such that <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(U)&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(U)&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The failure of packing stability in these examples is connected to the domains’ “wild” boundary behaviour; see Subsection <a href="#S1.SS3" title="1.3. Limits of packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> below for further discussion. This leaves a significant gap between these counterexamples and the known cases of packing stability, i.e. closed manifolds and special toric domains. It is an intriguing problem to more precisely identify and understand the transition between packing stability and its failure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">An important question is whether packing stability holds for compact symplectic manifolds with smooth boundary; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHa</a>, Question 7.1]</cite>. The known cases involving toric boundary provide limited insight into what one might expect in the general setting. For example, the symplectic <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-ball <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to the complement of a line in <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, equipped with a suitably scaled Fubini–Study symplectic form. More generally, any <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional convex toric domain can be approximated by toric domains which are symplectomorphic to the complements of nodal divisors in closed symplectic <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cri19</a>]</cite>. In other words, the known instances of packing stability in the presence of boundary essentially reduce to the case of nodal divisor complements and thus do not substantially extend beyond the closed case. In particular, no examples are known of packing stability for symplectic manifolds whose boundary carries a characteristic foliation with chaotic behaviour. In fact, general symplectic embedding results for manifolds with dynamically rich boundaries are rare; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edt24</a>]</cite> for an exception.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A major motivation for studying ball packings in symplectic manifolds with general smooth boundary arises from a close connection to the asymptotic behaviour of certain sequences of symplectic spectral invariants; see Subsection <a href="#S1.SS4" title="1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> below. These invariants encode important dynamical information, making manifolds with boundary—where the dynamics occurs—central to our investigation. Our first main result establishes that packing stability holds for all compact, connected symplectic <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds with smooth boundary.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A</span></span><span id="Thmintrothm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Ball packing stability)<span id="Thmintrothm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmintrothm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact, connected symplectic <math id="Thmintrothm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold, possibly with smooth boundary. Then symplectic ball packing stability holds for <math id="Thmintrothm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that there exists a positive integer <math id="Thmintrothm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmintrothm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmintrothm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We note that in Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A (Ball packing stability). ‣ 1.1. Ball packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> we only assume that the boundary is smooth and in particular do not impose any contact type assumptions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Ellipsoid embedding stability</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the <span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symplectic ellipsoid</span> <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> with widths <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\coloneqq\left\{z\in\mathbb{C}^{n}\kern 5.0pt\Big|\kern 5.0pt\sum_{j}\frac{\pi|z_{j}|^{2}}{a_{j}}\leq 1\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\coloneqq\left\{z\in\mathbb{C}^{n}\kern 5.0pt\Big|\kern 5.0pt\sum_{j}\frac{\pi|z_{j}|^{2}}{a_{j}}\leq 1\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2n}\cong\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2n}\cong\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with the standard symplectic form <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}=\sum_{i}dx_{i}\wedge dy_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}=\sum_{i}dx_{i}\wedge dy_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected symplectic manifold of finite volume. For every real number <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S1.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ellipsoid embedding number</span> <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}^{E}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}^{E}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies how much of the volume of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be filled by a single symplectic ellipsoid obtained from <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\dots,1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\dots,1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> by scaling. It is defined by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}^{E}(M)\coloneqq\sup_{\lambda}\frac{\operatorname{vol}(E(\lambda,\dots,\lambda,\lambda a))}{\operatorname{vol}(M)}\in[0,1]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>λ</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}^{E}(M)\coloneqq\sup_{\lambda}\frac{\operatorname{vol}(E(\lambda,\dots,\lambda,\lambda a))}{\operatorname{vol}(M)}\in[0,1],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the supremum is taken over all positive numbers <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the ellipsoid <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\lambda,\dots,\lambda,\lambda a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\lambda,\dots,\lambda,\lambda a)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a symplectic embedding into the interior of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch05b</a>, §1.3.2]</cite>. Since the ellipsoid <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,\dots,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,\dots,a)</annotation></semantics></math> is simply the ball <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}^{E}(M)=p_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}^{E}(M)=p_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well known that, for every positive integer <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the ellipsoid <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\dots,1,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\dots,1,k)</annotation></semantics></math> can be fully packed by <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> balls of equal size. This implies that <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}^{E}(M)\leq p_{k}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}^{E}(M)\leq p_{k}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Schlenk proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch05b</a>, §1.3.2, Theorem 3]</cite> that, for every connected symplectic manifold <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of finite volume, we have <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{a\rightarrow\infty}p_{a}^{E}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{a\rightarrow\infty}p_{a}^{E}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, symplectic embeddings of ellipsoids into <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> become asymptotically flexible as the ellipsoids become increasingly skinny. We say that <span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ellipsoid embedding stability</span> holds for <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists a finite threshold <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}^{E}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}^{E}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since ball packing stability holds for every ellipsoid, the condition <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}^{E}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}^{E}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for even a single value of <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> implies ball packing stability. In particular, ellipsoid embedding stability implies ball packing stability. Moreover, it was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH13</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite> that ellipsoid embedding stability holds for every ellipsoid, so knowing that <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}^{E}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}^{E}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for one value of <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> also implies ellipsoid embedding stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir01</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops13</a>]</cite>—see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH13</a>, Theorem 1.3]</cite>—that every closed, connected, rational symplectic manifold <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be fully filled by one single ellipsoid; that is <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}^{E}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}^{E}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that ellipsoid embedding stability holds for such manifolds. While ball packing stability is known to hold for all closed symplectic <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>]</cite>, the corresponding statement for ellipsoid embedding stability is open; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Conjecture 1]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch</a>, Conjecture 13.8]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that our ball packing stability result (Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A (Ball packing stability). ‣ 1.1. Ball packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) can, in fact, be strengthened to ellipsoid embedding stability. Although this result is new even in the closed case, the primary case of interest concerns compact symplectic manifolds whose boundary exhibits rich dynamical behaviour.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B</span></span><span id="Thmintrothm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Ellipsoid embedding stability)<span id="Thmintrothm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmintrothm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact, connected symplectic <math id="Thmintrothm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold, possibly with smooth boundary. Then symplectic ellipsoid embedding stability holds for <math id="Thmintrothm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that there exists a number <math id="Thmintrothm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmintrothm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}^{E}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}^{E}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmintrothm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact, connected symplectic <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold with smooth boundary. Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the threshold from Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm2" title="Theorem B (Ellipsoid embedding stability). ‣ 1.2. Ellipsoid embedding stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. Fix <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the symplectic ellipsoid <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=E(r,ra)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=E(r,ra)</annotation></semantics></math> where the parameter <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen so that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(M)=\operatorname{vol}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(M)=\operatorname{vol}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that the closed symplectic ellipsoid <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda E</annotation></semantics></math> symplectically embeds into the interior of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. This naturally raises the question of whether the interior of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> itself admits a symplectic embedding into the interior of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. In light of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx75" title="" class="ltx_ref">PV15</a>, Theorem 4.4]</cite>, this would follow if one could arrange the symplectic embeddings of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda E</annotation></semantics></math> into the interior of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> to depend smoothly on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> approaches <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. It is conceivable that, with additional care, such a smooth dependence can be achieved using the symplectic embedding constructions developed in this paper. However, we will not pursue this direction further here and leave it for future work.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In the literature, the ellipsoid embedding numbers <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}^{E}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}^{E}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> of a symplectic <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are often encoded in terms of the <span id="S1.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ellipsoid embedding function</span> <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{M}:[0,\infty)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{M}:[0,\infty)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>; see, for example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHMP25</a>]</cite>. This function is defined by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{M}(a)\coloneqq\inf\left\{r\mid E(1,a)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}(M,r\omega)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{M}(a)\coloneqq\inf\left\{r\mid E(1,a)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}(M,r\omega)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}Y</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the existence of a symplectic embedding from <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The function <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{M}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{M}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the same information as the ellipsoid embedding numbers <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}^{E}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}^{E}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The ellipsoid embedding function <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is notoriously difficult to compute. In a landmark paper, McDuff and Schlenk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx72" title="" class="ltx_ref">MS12</a>]</cite> succeeded in computing the ellipsoid embedding function <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{B(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{B(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional ball—arguably the simplest nontrivial target manifold. Their result is an intricate yet explicit function featuring the so-called infinite Fibonacci staircase. A further key feature of <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{B(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{B(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is that for sufficiently large <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, the function stabilizes to the volume curve:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{B(1)}(a)=\sqrt{\frac{a}{2\operatorname{vol}(B(1))}}=\sqrt{a}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{B(1)}(a)=\sqrt{\frac{a}{2\operatorname{vol}(B(1))}}=\sqrt{a}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">This stabilization is equivalent to the assertion that ellipsoid embedding stability holds for <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For a general compact, connected symplectic <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth boundary, fully computing the function <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> appears entirely out of reach. Nevertheless, Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm2" title="Theorem B (Ellipsoid embedding stability). ‣ 1.2. Ellipsoid embedding stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> shows that <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> always stabilizes to the volume curve.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Limits of packing stability</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned above, Cristofaro-Gardiner and Hind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHa</a>]</cite> constructed the first examples of symplectic manifolds of finite volume that do not exhibit ball packing stability. For a discussion of the failure of packing stability in the context of packings by certain generalized convex toric domains, rather than by balls, we refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">CMM</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The examples in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHa</a>]</cite> are unbounded concave toric domains of finite volume. More precisely, let <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:[0,\infty)\rightarrow(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:[0,\infty)\rightarrow(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex function such that <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{\infty}f(x)dx&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{\infty}f(x)dx&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the region in the first quadrant lying below the graph of <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, and set</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}\coloneqq\left\{(z_{1},z_{2})\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\mid\pi(|z_{1}|^{2},|z_{2}|^{2})\in\Omega\right\}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}\coloneqq\left\{(z_{1},z_{2})\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\mid\pi(|z_{1}|^{2},|z_{2}|^{2})\in\Omega\right\}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This domain has finite volume, and it is shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHa</a>]</cite> that packing stability fails for <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, for suitable choices of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHa</a>]</cite> proves that one can symplectically fold the infinite “tail” of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> into a bounded region of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby producing an open, bounded domain <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> that is diffeomorphic to the open ball and symplectomorphic to the interior of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. Packing stability must then also fail for <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the folding construction yields “wild” boundary behaviour: in particular <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial U</annotation></semantics></math> is not a topological submanifold of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. To more precisely locate the transition between packing stability and its failure, it is natural to seek counterexamples with more regular boundary. This is the content of the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem C</span></span><span id="Thmintrothm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmintrothm3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2-\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2-\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> failure of packing stability)<span id="Thmintrothm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arbitrarily <math id="Thmintrothm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> close to the unit ball in <math id="Thmintrothm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a star-shaped domain <math id="Thmintrothm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with the following properties:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The boundary of </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is in the regularity class </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcap_{\alpha\in(0,1)}C^{1,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcap_{\alpha\in(0,1)}C^{1,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, but not in </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Here </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> denotes the Hölder space of differentiable functions with </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{0,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{0,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Hölder continuous derivative.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The boundary of </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is smooth on the complement of a single point.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ball packing stability and ellipsoid embedding stability fail for </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. In fact, we have the following more general assertion: Let </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be an arbitrary compact domain with smooth boundary, possibly disconnected. Consider the number</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{W}(X)\coloneqq\sup_{r}\frac{\operatorname{vol}(rW)}{\operatorname{vol}(X)}\in[0,1]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>r</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{W}(X)\coloneqq\sup_{r}\frac{\operatorname{vol}(rW)}{\operatorname{vol}(X)}\in[0,1],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the supremum is taken over all </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rW</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> admits a symplectic embedding into </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{W}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{W}(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is strictly less than </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We do not currently know whether counterexamples to packing stability exist for symplectic manifolds with boundary regularity of class <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or higher. We expect that our methods for proving packing stability in the smooth case can be extended to manifolds whose boundary has finite differentiability. However, we do not expect that our present techniques can reach the <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> threshold; see Subsection <a href="#S1.SS6" title="1.6. Discussion and open problems ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> for further discussion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is a classical problem, dating back to work of Eliashberg and Hofer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH92</a>]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">CFHW96</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cie97</a>]</cite>), to understand when an open symplectic manifold can be given a boundary, and to what extent the interior of a symplectic manifold with boundary encodes information about the boundary. Questions of this nature have also been studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHa</a>]</cite>. For example, it is shown there that, under suitable decay conditions on the function <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, the interior of the domain <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in equation (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.3. Limits of packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) is not symplectomorphic to the interior of any compact symplectic manifold with smooth boundary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that if the interior of a symplectic <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to the interior of a compact symplectic manifold with smooth boundary, then packing stability must hold for each component of <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A (Ball packing stability). ‣ 1.1. Ball packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Thus Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm3" title="Theorem C (𝐶^{2-𝜀} failure of packing stability). ‣ 1.3. Limits of packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> has the following consequence:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollaryx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollaryx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary</span></span><span id="Thmcorollaryx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmcorollaryx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2-\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2-\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> domains with non-smoothable interior)<span id="Thmcorollaryx1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollaryx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollaryx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollaryx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a star-shaped domain <math id="Thmcorollaryx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="Thmcorollaryx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> close to the unit ball, whose boundary is in the regularity class <math id="Thmcorollaryx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcap_{\alpha\in(0,1)}C^{1,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcap_{\alpha\in(0,1)}C^{1,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and smooth on the complement of a single point, and whose interior is not symplectomorphic to the interior of a compact symplectic manifold with smooth boundary.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4. </span>Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a symplectic <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly open or with boundary, Hutchings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut11a</a>]</cite> defines a sequence of numbers</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=c_{0}(M)&lt;c_{1}(M)\leq c_{2}(M)\leq\cdots\leq\infty" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=c_{0}(M)&lt;c_{1}(M)\leq c_{2}(M)\leq\cdots\leq\infty</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">called <em id="S1.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">embedded contact homology (ECH) capacities</em>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut14</a>]</cite> for a survey. For every compact domain <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with piecewise smooth boundary, he proves the following remarkable asymptotic formula:</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(X)=2\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(X)k}+o(k^{1/2})\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(X)=2\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(X)k}+o(k^{1/2})\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This identity shows that ECH capacities asymptotically recover the symplectic volume of the domain. By analogy with the classical Weyl law for the spectrum of the Laplace operator, formula (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) is often referred to as the <span id="S1.SS4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ECH Weyl law</span>. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHR15</a>]</cite> for a version of the Weyl law for ECH spectral invariants associated with a contact form on a <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold. See also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iri15</a>]</cite> for a major application of the ECH Weyl law concerning the smooth closing lemma for <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Reeb flows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is a natural and compelling problem to study the error terms</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)\coloneqq c_{k}(X)-2\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(X)k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)\coloneqq c_{k}(X)-2\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(X)k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">in the ECH Weyl law.
In the case of the classical Weyl law for the Laplace spectrum, the corresponding error terms have been extensively investigated over the past century; see, for example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cou20</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lev52</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">DG75</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ivr80</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">CG23</a>]</cite>, among many others. In the symplectic setting, Sun <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun19</a>]</cite> proved that <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)=O(k^{125/252})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>125</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>252</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)=O(k^{125/252})</annotation></semantics></math> for every star-shaped domain <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth boundary. This was improved by Cristofaro-Gardiner and Savale <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">CS20</a>]</cite>, who established the bound <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)=O(k^{2/5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)=O(k^{2/5})</annotation></semantics></math>. Later Hutchings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut22a</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite> showed that in fact <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)=O(k^{1/4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)=O(k^{1/4})</annotation></semantics></math> for every compact domain <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth boundary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, in all known examples where the capacities <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> have been explicitly computed, one finds that <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>; see, for instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wor23</a>]</cite>. These examples comprise different classes of toric domains and are somewhat limited in scope, as the Reeb dynamics on the boundary of a toric domain is exceptionally simple and fails to reflect the complex, chaotic dynamics that can occur on the boundary of a general domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, Hutchings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut22a</a>, Conjecture 1.5]</cite> conjectured that, for a generic star-shaped domain <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth boundary, the error terms satisfy</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}e_{k}(X)=-\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{Ru}(X)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Ru</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}e_{k}(X)=-\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{Ru}(X),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ru}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ru</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ru}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <span id="S1.SS4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ruelle invariant</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rue85</a>]</cite> associated to the natural Reeb flow on the boundary of <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut22a</a>, §1.2]</cite> for more details. This conjecture can be viewed as a symplectic analogue of the well-known Weyl conjecture on the error terms in the classical Weyl law; see, for instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">LMP23</a>, Conjecture 3.3.7]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem is an application of our symplectic ball packing stability result, Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A (Ball packing stability). ‣ 1.1. Ball packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. It establishes that the error terms in the ECH Weyl law are bounded for all compact domains in <math id="S1.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth boundary. This significantly improves upon the best previously known bound of <math id="S1.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(k^{1/4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(k^{1/4})</annotation></semantics></math>, and can also be viewed as a step towards Hutchings’ conjecture (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem D</span></span><span id="Thmintrothm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Subleading asymptotics ECH)<span id="Thmintrothm4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmintrothm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact domain with smooth boundary. Then the ECH capacities satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(X)=2\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(X)k}+O(1)\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(X)=2\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(X)k}+O(1)\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmintrothm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In other words, <math id="Thmintrothm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The exponent <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in the bound <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> on the error terms <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is sharp. There are examples of smooth domains <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, for example the ball <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> does not converge to zero; see, for instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut22a</a>, Example 1.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, Hutchings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut22b</a>]</cite> defined an alternative sequence of capacities <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}^{\operatorname{alt}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>alt</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}^{\operatorname{alt}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> which retain most of the formal properties of the ECH capacities <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby recovering many key applications. Unlike the ECH capacities, however, their construction avoids the sophisticated machinery of embedded contact homology and is instead considerably more elementary. The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm4" title="Theorem D (Subleading asymptotics ECH). ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> carries over essentially unchanged to this setting and implies that the error terms in the Weyl law for these alternative capacities are also bounded. More precisely, for every compact domain <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth boundary, one has</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}^{\operatorname{alt}}(X)=2\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(X)k}+O(1)\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>alt</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}^{\operatorname{alt}}(X)=2\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(X)k}+O(1)\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">A variant of embedded contact homology, known as <em id="S1.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">periodic Floer homology (PFH)</em>, was introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut02</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx61" title="" class="ltx_ref">HS05</a>]</cite>. This theory allows one to extract numerical invariants—called <span id="S1.SS4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PFH spectral invariants</span>—from area-preserving surface diffeomorphisms. It was recently established independently by Cristofaro-Gardiner, Prasad, and Zhang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">CPZ</a>]</cite>, and by Hutchings and the author <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH</a>]</cite>, that these invariants satisfy a Weyl-type asymptotic formula, analogous to the ECH Weyl law. Important applications of the PFH Weyl law include smooth closing lemmas for area preserving surface maps established in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">CPZ</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH</a>]</cite> and the resolution of the long-standing simplicity conjecture in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHS24a</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">As in the ECH case, understanding the asymptotic behavior of the associated error terms is an interesting problem; see, for instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aim</a>, Problem 2.2]</cite>. Our ball packing stability result (Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A (Ball packing stability). ‣ 1.1. Ball packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) implies that the error terms in the PFH Weyl law are bounded. More precisely, we obtain the following result. For the relevant notation and terminology, we refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH</a>, Theorem 8.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E</span></span><span id="Thmintrothm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Subleading asymptotics PFH)<span id="Thmintrothm5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a closed surface <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with an area form <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> of total area <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a rational area preserving diffeomorphism <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\operatorname{Diff}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Diff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\operatorname{Diff}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> denote its mapping torus. Let <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}\subset Y_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}\subset Y_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of monotone reference cycles whose degrees <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> diverge to <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\in HP(\phi,\gamma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\in HP(\phi,\gamma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of non-zero PFH classes. Then, for every pair of Hamiltonians <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\pm}\in C^{\infty}(Y_{\phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\pm}\in C^{\infty}(Y_{\phi})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\sigma_{i}}(\phi,\gamma_{i},H_{+})-c_{\sigma_{i}}(\phi,\gamma_{i},H_{-})+\int_{\gamma_{i}}(H_{+}-H_{-})dt=d_{i}A^{-1}\int_{Y_{\phi}}(H_{+}-H_{-})dt\wedge\omega_{\phi}+O(1)\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\sigma_{i}}(\phi,\gamma_{i},H_{+})-c_{\sigma_{i}}(\phi,\gamma_{i},H_{-})+\int_{\gamma_{i}}(H_{+}-H_{-})dt=d_{i}A^{-1}\int_{Y_{\phi}}(H_{+}-H_{-})dt\wedge\omega_{\phi}+O(1)\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm5" title="Theorem E (Subleading asymptotics PFH). ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, we set the subgroups <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}\subset\operatorname{ker}(\omega_{\phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}\subset\operatorname{ker}(\omega_{\phi})</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH</a>, Theorem 8.1]</cite> all equal to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ker}(\omega_{\phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ker}(\omega_{\phi})</annotation></semantics></math>. As explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH</a>, Remark 8.2]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH</a>, Remark 7.3]</cite>, this choice, combined with the assumption that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is rational and the reference cycles <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are monotone, ensures that the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle assumption in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH</a>, Theorem 8.1]</cite> is automatically satisfied. This was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">CPPZ</a>]</cite>. We also emphasize that Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm5" title="Theorem E (Subleading asymptotics PFH). ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> is not vacuous: there exists a sequence of monotone reference cycles <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with diverging degrees, along with a sequence of non-zero classes <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\in HP(\phi,\gamma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\in HP(\phi,\gamma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>; see again <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH</a>, Remark 8.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edta</a>]</cite>, we introduced an alternative to PFH spectral invariants using more elementary methods; see also Remark <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Remark 1.2. ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.
These elementary PFH spectral invariants likewise satisfy a Weyl law, and again our methods show that the associated error terms are <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, in the notation of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edta</a>, Theorem 1.6]</cite>, we have</p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{d_{i}}(H_{+},H_{-})=d_{i}A^{-1}\int_{Y_{\phi}}(H_{+}-H_{-})dt\wedge\omega_{\phi}+O(1)\qquad(i\rightarrow\infty)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{d_{i}}(H_{+},H_{-})=d_{i}A^{-1}\int_{Y_{\phi}}(H_{+}-H_{-})dt\wedge\omega_{\phi}+O(1)\qquad(i\rightarrow\infty)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for every diverging sequence of positive integers <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite for each <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHMSS22</a>]</cite>, Cristofaro-Gardiner, Humilière, Mak, Seyfaddini, and Smith introduced another sequence of spectral invariants, called <em id="S1.SS4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">link spectral invariants</em>, which also satisfy a Weyl law. As in the previous cases, it is natural to investigate their subleading asymptotics; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aim</a>, Problem 2.1]</cite>. Let <math id="S1.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed surface equipped with an area form of total area <math id="S1.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="S1.SS4.p9.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lagrangian link</em> <math id="S1.SS4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{L}\subset\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{L}\subset\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of smoothly embedded circles in <math id="S1.SS4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. For links <math id="S1.SS4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{L}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying a certain monotonicity condition, the associated link spectral invariant is a function</p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\underline{L}}:C^{\infty}([0,1]\times\Sigma)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\underline{L}}:C^{\infty}([0,1]\times\Sigma)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p9.2" class="ltx_p">It is shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHMSS22</a>]</cite> that for an equidistributed sequence of <math id="S1.SS4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-component links <math id="S1.SS4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{L}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{L}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\underline{L}_{d}}(H)=A^{-1}\int_{[0,1]\times\Sigma}Hdt\wedge\omega+o(1)\qquad(d\rightarrow\infty)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\underline{L}_{d}}(H)=A^{-1}\int_{[0,1]\times\Sigma}Hdt\wedge\omega+o(1)\qquad(d\rightarrow\infty).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p9.3" class="ltx_p">In subsequent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHMSS</a>]</cite>, the same authors established the sharp bound <math id="S1.SS4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> on the subleading asymptotics of this Weyl law in the case where <math id="S1.SS4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the 2-sphere. For higher genus surfaces, Mak and Trifa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx68" title="" class="ltx_ref">MT</a>]</cite> obtained a weaker bound of <math id="S1.SS4.p9.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="O(d^{-1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d^{-1/2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Using Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm5" title="Theorem E (Subleading asymptotics PFH). ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> and results of Chen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chea</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheb</a>]</cite> relating PFH spectral invariants to link spectral invariants, we are able to obtain the sharp bound <math id="S1.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> for arbitrary genus. We emphasize, however, that the class of links considered in Chen’s work differs slightly from that in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHMSS22</a>]</cite>. Our result applies to the former class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a link <math id="S1.SS4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{L}\subset\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{L}\subset\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.SS4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">admissible</span> if the complement <math id="S1.SS4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\setminus\underline{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∖</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\setminus\underline{L}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of open disks and a single planar surface. We say that <math id="S1.SS4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.SS4.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monotone</span> if each connected component of <math id="S1.SS4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\setminus\underline{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∖</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\setminus\underline{L}</annotation></semantics></math> has the same area.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem F</span></span><span id="Thmintrothm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Subleading asymptotics link spectral invariants)<span id="Thmintrothm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmintrothm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed connected surface of total area <math id="Thmintrothm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and consider a sequence <math id="Thmintrothm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{L}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{L}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of admissible, monotone <math id="Thmintrothm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-component links. Then, for every <math id="Thmintrothm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in C^{\infty}([0,1]\times\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in C^{\infty}([0,1]\times\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\underline{L}_{d}}(H)=A^{-1}\int_{[0,1]\times\Sigma}Hdt\wedge\omega+O(d^{-1})\qquad(d\rightarrow\infty)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\underline{L}_{d}}(H)=A^{-1}\int_{[0,1]\times\Sigma}Hdt\wedge\omega+O(d^{-1})\qquad(d\rightarrow\infty).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.5. </span>Overview of the proofs and outline of the paper</h3>

<div id="S1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now highlight some key ideas underlying the proofs of our main results. We begin with Theorems <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A (Ball packing stability). ‣ 1.1. Ball packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> and <a href="#Thmintrothm2" title="Theorem B (Ellipsoid embedding stability). ‣ 1.2. Ellipsoid embedding stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, which concern ball packing and ellipsoid embedding stability, respectively. Let <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact, connected symplectic <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold with smooth boundary. As noted above, since both ball packing and ellipsoid embedding stability are known to hold when the target is a symplectic ellipsoid, it suffices to fill the volume of <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>—up to arbitrarily small error—by a single symplectic ellipsoid of fixed aspect ratio. In the case where <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is closed and rational, Opshtein <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops13</a>, Proposition 1.3]</cite> observed that such an ellipsoid filling can be deduced from the existence of a Donaldson divisor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Don96</a>]</cite>. This is closely related to the well-known Biran decomposition of a symplectic manifold <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir01</a>]</cite>. In contrast, our approach does not appeal to Donaldson’s theorem. Instead, we rely crucially on deep results of Banyaga concerning the simplicity and perfectness of the group of Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ban78</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ban97</a>]</cite>. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">BS</a>]</cite> for another recent flexibility result related to Banyaga’s theorems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate how the algebraic structure of Hamiltonian diffeomorphism groups enters into our symplectic embedding constructions, we consider the following simple toy example. Let <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be an area form on the <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of total area <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{T}\coloneqq\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{T}\coloneqq\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the circle, and define the open <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\coloneqq\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{T}\times S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\coloneqq\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{T}\times S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, equipped with the symplectic form <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\coloneqq ds\wedge dt+\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\coloneqq ds\wedge dt+\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> are coordinates on the cylinder <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:\mathbb{T}\times S^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:\mathbb{T}\times S^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic, mean-normalized Hamiltonian. We naturally view its graph as a codimension-<math id="S1.SS5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> hypersurface <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{graph}(H)\subset(W,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{graph}(H)\subset(W,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. The characteristic foliation on <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{graph}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{graph}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> lifts the Hamiltonian flow <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S^{2},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S^{2},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{graph}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{graph}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as the mapping torus of <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be a constant strictly less than the minimum of <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, and define <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> to be the compact region in <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> bounded from below by <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{C\}\times\mathbb{T}\times S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{C\}\times\mathbb{T}\times S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and from above by <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{graph}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{graph}(H)</annotation></semantics></math>. We view <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> as a toy model for a compact symplectic <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold with smooth boundary. Since <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrary, the dynamics on the boundary component <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{graph}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{graph}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> can be complicated. In what follows, we sketch how to decompose <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, for sufficiently negative values of <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, into much simpler pieces—namely finitely many balls and polydisks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In suitable polar coordinates <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z,\theta)\in(0,1)\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z,\theta)\in(0,1)\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the area form <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=dz\wedge d\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=dz\wedge d\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the autonomous Hamiltonian</p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:S^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad F(z,\theta)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2}z-\frac{1}{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:S^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad F(z,\theta)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2}z-\frac{1}{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This Hamiltonian is mean-normalized and its time-<math id="S1.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> flow <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{F}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{F}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a half-rotation of <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the normal closure of the subgroup <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\operatorname{id},\varphi_{F}^{1}\right\}\subset\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\operatorname{id},\varphi_{F}^{1}\right\}\subset\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{F}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{F}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is a simple group by Banyaga’s theorem, this normal closure must equal the entire group <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that every Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S^{2},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S^{2},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as a finite composition of conjugates of the half-rotation <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{F}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{F}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we can find finitely many Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1},\dots,\psi_{n}\in\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1},\dots,\psi_{n}\in\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{1}=(\psi_{n}^{-1}\circ\varphi_{F}^{1}\circ\psi_{n})\circ\cdots\circ(\psi_{1}^{-1}\circ\varphi_{F}^{1}\circ\psi_{1})=\varphi_{\psi_{n}^{*}F}^{1}\circ\cdots\circ\varphi_{\psi_{1}^{*}F}^{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{1}=(\psi_{n}^{-1}\circ\varphi_{F}^{1}\circ\psi_{n})\circ\cdots\circ(\psi_{1}^{-1}\circ\varphi_{F}^{1}\circ\psi_{1})=\varphi_{\psi_{n}^{*}F}^{1}\circ\cdots\circ\varphi_{\psi_{1}^{*}F}^{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Moreover, this identity continues to hold on the level of the universal cover of <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly after adding two additional factors of <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{F}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{F}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now use the factorization (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1.5. Overview of the proofs and outline of the paper ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>) of <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into half-rotations <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{\psi_{i}^{*}F}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{\psi_{i}^{*}F}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to construct a decomposition of <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> into balls and polydisks. To this end, we define a special Hamiltonian <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G:\mathbb{T}\times S^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G:\mathbb{T}\times S^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same time-<math id="S1.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> map as <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{G}^{1}=\varphi_{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{G}^{1}=\varphi_{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This Hamiltonian is constructed such that the path <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{G}^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{G}^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> is the concatenation of the <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> paths <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{\psi_{i}^{*}F}^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{\psi_{i}^{*}F}^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math>. We divide the circle <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> into intervals <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}\coloneqq[\frac{i-1}{n},\frac{i}{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}\coloneqq[\frac{i-1}{n},\frac{i}{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n}:\mathbb{T}\rightarrow[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n}:\mathbb{T}\rightarrow[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-negative function such that <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n}|_{I_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n}|_{I_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is compactly supported in the interior of <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{I_{i}}\rho_{n}dt=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{I_{i}}\rho_{n}dt=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We set</p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(t,p)\coloneqq\rho_{n}(t)\psi_{i}^{*}F(p)\qquad\text{for $p\in S^{2}$ and $t\in I_{i}$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(t,p)\coloneqq\rho_{n}(t)\psi_{i}^{*}F(p)\qquad\text{for $p\in S^{2}$ and $t\in I_{i}$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here the role of <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is to reparametrize the paths <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{\psi_{i}^{*}F}^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{\psi_{i}^{*}F}^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> in such a way that <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now assume that the constant <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is smaller than the minimum of <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We then consider the compact region <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> bounded below by <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{C\right\}\times\mathbb{T}\times S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{C\right\}\times\mathbb{T}\times S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and bounded above by <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{graph}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{graph}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is an elementary but important observation that <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are symplectomorphic, provided <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> can be connected to <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> through a smooth family <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> of mean-normalized Hamiltonians, strictly greater than <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and satisfying <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H^{\lambda}}^{1}=\varphi_{H}^{1}=\varphi_{G}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H^{\lambda}}^{1}=\varphi_{H}^{1}=\varphi_{G}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>; see Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>. Since the factorization (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1.5. Overview of the proofs and outline of the paper ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>) is assumed to hold on the level of the universal cover, such a connecting family of Hamiltonians exists whenever <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently negative. We have therefore reduced the task of decomposing <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> into balls and polydisks to the analogous task for <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now cut <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> pieces of the form <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\cap(\mathbb{R}\times I_{i}\times S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\cap(\mathbb{R}\times I_{i}\times S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. It is straightforward to verify that each of these <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> pieces is symplectomorphic to the domain <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> bounded below by <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\frac{C}{n}\right\}\times[0,1]\times S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\frac{C}{n}\right\}\times[0,1]\times S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and above by the graph of <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, we equip <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with the symplectic form <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ds\wedge dt+\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ds\wedge dt+\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and regard <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> as a Hamiltonian <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:[0,1]\times S^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:[0,1]\times S^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence it suffices to decompose <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> into balls and polydisks. This can be done explicitly, since <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a simple height function on <math id="S1.SS5.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We remove the poles of <math id="S1.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and cut open the resulting annulus <math id="S1.SS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> along the line segment <math id="S1.SS5.p7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\theta=0\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\theta=0\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This reveals that the open domain</p>
<table id="S1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\coloneqq\left\{(s,t,z,\theta)\in\mathbb{R}\times(0,1)^{3}\mid\frac{C}{n}&lt;s&lt;\frac{1}{2}z-\frac{1}{4}\right\}\subset(\mathbb{R}^{4},ds\wedge dt+dz\wedge\theta)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\coloneqq\left\{(s,t,z,\theta)\in\mathbb{R}\times(0,1)^{3}\mid\frac{C}{n}&lt;s&lt;\frac{1}{2}z-\frac{1}{4}\right\}\subset(\mathbb{R}^{4},ds\wedge dt+dz\wedge\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.p7.2" class="ltx_p">can be naturally regarded as an open subset of <math id="S1.SS5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of full volume. The domain <math id="S1.SS5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as the Lagrangian product of the right trapezoid</p>
<table id="S1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{(s,z)\in\mathbb{R}\times(0,1)\mid\frac{C}{n}&lt;s&lt;\frac{1}{2}z-\frac{1}{4}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{(s,z)\in\mathbb{R}\times(0,1)\mid\frac{C}{n}&lt;s&lt;\frac{1}{2}z-\frac{1}{4}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.p7.3" class="ltx_p">and the square <math id="S1.SS5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can cut the right trapezoid into a rectangle and a triangle. This yields a decomposition of <math id="S1.SS5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> into two pieces: a Lagrangian product of a rectangle and a square, which is symplectomorphic to a polydisk, and a Lagrangian product of a right triangle and a square, which is symplectomorphic to an ellipsoid. In our case, this ellipsoid is <math id="S1.SS5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\frac{1}{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\frac{1}{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, which can in turn be decomposed into two disjoint copies of the ball <math id="S1.SS5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(\frac{1}{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(\frac{1}{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. This concludes our construction of a decomposition of <math id="S1.SS5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> into balls and polydisks. We carry out a variation of this construction in more detail in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A significant amount of work is required to extend this simple toy example to obtain a filling of a general compact, connected symplectic <math id="S1.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold by a single ellipsoid. One immediate challenge is to upgrade a decomposition into finitely many balls and polydisks to an ellipsoid filling. Very roughly speaking, our strategy is as follows. Since ellipsoid embedding stability is already known to hold for balls and polydisks, we can fill each of the balls and polydisks in our decomposition with a skinny ellipsoid. We assume that the smaller of the two widths of each skinny ellipsoid is the same across all the ellipsoids. We then connect these skinny ellipsoids through tunnels at the interfaces between the balls and polydisks in the decomposition, ultimately forming a single ellipsoid that fills the entire volume. A detailed implementation of this strategy is provided in Sections <a href="#S5" title="5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S6" title="6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. It is interesting to point out that these arguments, combined with the proof of ball packing stability for general, possibly irrational, closed symplectic <math id="S1.SS5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>]</cite>, are already sufficient to prove ellipsoid embedding stability for general closed symplectic <math id="S1.SS5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds. This special case of Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm2" title="Theorem B (Ellipsoid embedding stability). ‣ 1.2. Ellipsoid embedding stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> resolves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Conjecture 1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Another challenge is that a general symplectic <math id="S1.SS5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S1.SS5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is not necessarily symplectomorphic to the subgraph of some Hamiltonian on a surface. Our strategy is to decompose <math id="S1.SS5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> into pieces that are symplectomorphic to subgraphs. To achieve this, it becomes necessary to replace the <math id="S1.SS5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere <math id="S1.SS5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with the annulus <math id="S1.SS5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S1.SS5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> could be exact and, in particular, not contain any closed symplectic surfaces. Working on the annulus requires versions of Banyaga’s results for Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms on a surface with boundary, where the diffeomorphisms are not required to restrict to the identity on the boundary. We establish such generalizations in a separate paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edtb</a>]</cite>. They are reviewed in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms of surfaces with boundary ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The decomposition of a general manifold <math id="S1.SS5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> into subgraphs of Hamiltonians on the annulus <math id="S1.SS5.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is somewhat technical in nature and is carried out in Sections <a href="#S7" title="7. Symplectic frusta ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, <a href="#S8" title="8. Proof of ellipsoid embedding stability ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, and <a href="#S9" title="9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">A more subtle issue with our toy example above is the role of the negative constant <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that we only explained how to decompose the domain <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> into balls and polydisks for sufficiently negative <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. The problem is that the threshold of how negative <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has to be depends on <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of half-rotations in the factorization of <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, the oscillation of the Hamiltonian <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is of the same order of magnitude as <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C|</annotation></semantics></math> must also be of the order of <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the number <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> of half-rotations in the factorization of <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be arbitrarily large. In fact, this number is bounded from below by the autonomous norm of <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is defined as the least number of autonomous Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms required in a factorization of <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The autonomous norm on Hamiltonian diffeomorphism groups is known to be unbounded in many cases; see, for instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">GG04</a>, §6.3]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">BK13</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">BKS18</a>]</cite>. This is problematic because we would like to decompose <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> into balls and polydisks for a fixed value of <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, not just for sufficiently negative values. In order to resolve this, we establish certain quantitative refinements of Banyaga’s results in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edtb</a>]</cite>. These refinements are of independent interest and are reviewed in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms of surfaces with boundary ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. As a consequence, we show in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> that the number of rotations needed to factor a Hamiltonian diffeomorphism is bounded in some <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> open neighbourhood of the identity. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we use this quantitative factorization result to decompose subgraphs of Hamiltonians on <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> that are <math id="S1.SS5.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> small perturbations of affine Hamiltonians into balls and polydisks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S10" title="10. Bounding the subleading asymptotics ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, we prove Theorems <a href="#Thmintrothm4" title="Theorem D (Subleading asymptotics ECH). ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, <a href="#Thmintrothm5" title="Theorem E (Subleading asymptotics PFH). ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, and <a href="#Thmintrothm6" title="Theorem F (Subleading asymptotics link spectral invariants). ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, establishing bounds on the error terms in the Weyl laws for ECH capacities, PFH spectral invariants, and link spectral invariants. The proof of the ECH Weyl law for domains in <math id="S1.SS5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut11a</a>]</cite> relies on packing domains with balls up to arbitrarily small error, where the number of balls depends on the error. The more efficient ball packing provided by Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A (Ball packing stability). ‣ 1.1. Ball packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> allows us to upgrade the estimates in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hut11a</a>]</cite> and obtain <math id="S1.SS5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> bounds on the error terms. Similarly, in the case of PFH, the computations in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH</a>]</cite>, which also rely on ball packings, can be improved to yield bounded error terms. Finally, the bounds on the error terms in the Weyl law for link spectral invariants are derived from the PFH bounds via Chen’s works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chea</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheb</a>]</cite>, which relate the two sets of spectral invariants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in Section <a href="#S11" title="11. 𝐶^{2-𝜀} failure of packing stability ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, we construct a star-shaped domain <math id="S1.SS5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with regularity slightly less than <math id="S1.SS5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for which packing stability fails. To prove the failure of packing stability, we estimate the error terms in the ECH Weyl law (strictly speaking, the Weyl law for alternative ECH capacities, see Remark <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Remark 1.2. ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) and show that they diverge to negative infinity. It is noteworthy that our domain <math id="S1.SS5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is not toric, so we cannot appeal to known computational techniques for ECH capacities of toric domains. Instead, our estimates rely on translating the problem of estimating the ECH capacities of <math id="S1.SS5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> into estimating the link spectral invariants of a certain Hamiltonian, which, in our situation, is much easier. This approach to estimating ECH capacities may be of independent interest; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aim</a>, Problem 2.4 &amp; 2.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.6. </span>Discussion and open problems</h3>

<div id="S1.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of the main insights of this work is the connection between symplectic packing stability for manifolds with smooth boundary and the algebraic structure of Hamiltonian diffeomorphism groups, particularly their simplicity and perfectness properties. This reflects a broader pattern: several deep questions about Hamiltonian diffeomorphism groups—especially those related to Hofer geometry—have counterparts in the theory of symplectic embeddings. Illustrative examples include recent parallel developments concerning the large-scale geometry of the Hofer metric on Hamiltonian diffeomorphism groups and the symplectic Banach–Mazur metric on spaces of symplectic domains; see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx76" title="" class="ltx_ref">PS23</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHS24b</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHb</a>]</cite> and the references therein. Another instance is provided by closely related results on the local structure of geodesics with respect to the Hofer distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BP94</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx64" title="" class="ltx_ref">LM95</a>]</cite> and the symplectic Banach–Mazur distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">ABE</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this light, the failure of packing stability for domains with rough boundary (Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm3" title="Theorem C (𝐶^{2-𝜀} failure of packing stability). ‣ 1.3. Limits of packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>) may be viewed as an analogue of recent non-simplicity theorems for various groups of Hamiltonian homeomorphisms, beginning with the inital breakthrough <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHS24a</a>]</cite> resolving the long-standing <span id="S1.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">simplicity conjecture</span>. It is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHS24b</a>]</cite> that the group of area preserving homeomorphisms of the <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere, denoted <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\operatorname{Ham}}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Ham</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\operatorname{Ham}}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, is not simple—contrasting the simplicity of the smooth group <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. This non-simplicity result does not directly correspond to failure of packing stability for domains with rough boundary, since the proof relies, loosly speaking, on detecting area preserving homeomorphisms with “infinite” Hofer energy and Calabi invariant, which would correspond to symplectic domains of infinite volume, for which the ball packing numbers are not even defined. However, <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\operatorname{Ham}}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Ham</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\operatorname{Ham}}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> contains a natural subgroup <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hameo}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hameo</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hameo}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> of <span id="S1.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hamiltonian hameomorphisms</span>, introduced by Oh and Müller <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">OM07</a>]</cite>, whose elements have finite Hofer energy. It was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHMSS</a>]</cite> that <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hameo}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hameo</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hameo}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is not a simple group either, and this result serves as a close analogue of Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm3" title="Theorem C (𝐶^{2-𝜀} failure of packing stability). ‣ 1.3. Limits of packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a compact, connected symplectic <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth boundary, a natural problem is to estimate the threshold <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}=k_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}=k_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A (Ball packing stability). ‣ 1.1. Ball packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> at which the ball packing numbers <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> stabilize to <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. This appears to be a difficult question in general, as the threshold is highly sensitive to the geometry of the boundary of <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, if <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of smooth domains converging to a rough domain <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm3" title="Theorem C (𝐶^{2-𝜀} failure of packing stability). ‣ 1.3. Limits of packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, for which packing stability fails, then the stability thresholds <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}(X_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}(X_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> tend to infinity. Interestingly, for the toy model domains <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> considered in Subsection <a href="#S1.SS5" title="1.5. Overview of the proofs and outline of the paper ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>, the stability threshold <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> can be estimated in terms of the minimal number of rotations needed to factor <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Closely related quantities—such as the <span id="S1.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">autonomous norm</span> of a Hamiltonian diffeomorphism—have been studied by various authors; see, for instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">GG04</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">BK13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">BKS18</a>]</cite>. It would be interesting to further investigate this relationship.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Another natural problem is to determine explicit bounds on the error terms in the ECH Weyl law. As before, this is challenging due to the strong dependence of the error terms on the boundary of the domain. Notably, the problem of bounding these error terms is closely tied to that of estimating ball packing stability thresholds. Indeed, as seen in our proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm4" title="Theorem D (Subleading asymptotics ECH). ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> in Section <a href="#S10" title="10. Bounding the subleading asymptotics ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, one can obtain explicit bounds on the error terms <math id="S1.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for a smooth domain <math id="S1.SS6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of the stability thresholds <math id="S1.SS6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}(B\setminus X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}(B\setminus X)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sufficiently large ball containing <math id="S1.SS6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Ruelle invariant <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ru}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ru</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ru}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, which appears in Hutchings’ conjecture (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) on the error terms in the ECH Weyl law, can be defined for star-shaped domains <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> boundary and it is continuous with respect to the <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> topology on the space of star-shaped domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE22</a>, Proposition 2.13 c]</cite>. In light of Hutchings’ conjecture, one would therefore expect that the error terms <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> for compact domains with <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> boundary. However, we do not yet know how to prove this. We show (Proposition <a href="#S11.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 11.2. ‣ 11. 𝐶^{2-𝜀} failure of packing stability ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.2</span></a>) that the domains in Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm3" title="Theorem C (𝐶^{2-𝜀} failure of packing stability). ‣ 1.3. Limits of packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, which have regularity just below <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and for which packing stability fails, have unbounded error terms <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}^{\operatorname{alt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>alt</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}^{\operatorname{alt}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Weyl law for the alternative ECH capacities (see Remark <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Remark 1.2. ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>). The same is expected to hold for the error terms <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in the usual ECH Weyl law, but we strictly speaking do not prove this since we rely on results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH</a>]</cite> which are only stated for the alternative ECH capacities. This leads to the following natural question, which could serve as a test for Hutchings’ conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the regularity threshold at which the error terms in the ECH Weyl law start to be <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">If we knew packing stability for domains with <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> boundary, this would imply <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> subleading asymptotics for such domains. As already noted above, we expect that our methods yield packing stability for domains in some finite differentiability class, but we do not expect that our present techniques can reach <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> regularity. The reason for this is that our packing construction uses (quantitative versions of) Banyaga’s simplicity results, which ultimately rely on a KAM result due to Herman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Her70</a>, Annexe, Theorem 2.2]</cite>. This result says that a <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> small Hamiltonian perturbation of a Diophantine rotation of the <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional torus <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is still conjugated to the original rotation. For sufficiently Diophantine rotation vectors, this continues to hold for <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{3+\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{3+\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> perturbations, but fails for <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{3-\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{3-\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> perturbations, see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Her83</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">CW13</a>]</cite>. Since <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{4+\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{4+\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> Hamiltonians generate <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{3+\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{3+\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms, it seems plausible that our methods can show packing stability with <math id="S1.SS6.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{4+\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{4+\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> boundary, but they likely break down for lower regularity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the regularity threshold at which packing stability starts to hold? Does it agree with the regularity threshold for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> subleading asymptotics in the ECH Weyl law?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The star-shaped domain <math id="S1.SS6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> of regularity just below <math id="S1.SS6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in our counterexample to packing stability (Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm3" title="Theorem C (𝐶^{2-𝜀} failure of packing stability). ‣ 1.3. Limits of packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>) is not convex. In fact, there does not even exist a symplectomorphism of <math id="S1.SS6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> mapping <math id="S1.SS6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to a convex domain. The reason is that the sectional curvature of the boundary of <math id="S1.SS6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (away from the single non-smooth point) is unbounded from below. A more interesting question is whether the interior of <math id="S1.SS6.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to the interior of a convex domain. It is reasonable to conjecture that this is not the case. Indeed, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE22</a>]</cite> the Ruelle invariant of a smooth convex domain can be bounded from above and below by constants only depending on the volume and the action of a systole of the domain. Since volume and action of a systole are continuous on the space of convex domains with respect to the Hausdorff topology, Hutchings’ conjecture (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) suggests that the error terms in the ECH Weyl law of any (not necessarily smooth) convex domain should be bounded. Since the error terms of the domain <math id="S1.SS6.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm3" title="Theorem C (𝐶^{2-𝜀} failure of packing stability). ‣ 1.3. Limits of packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> diverge to negative infinity, one expects the interior of <math id="S1.SS6.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to not be symplectomorphic to the interior of a convex domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In view of this discussion, ECH capacities conjecturally do not yield obstructions to symplectic packing stability for general convex domains and we are led to the following question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does packing stability hold for the interiors of arbitrary, not necessarily smooth, convex domains?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y^{3},\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y^{3},\xi)</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed connected contact <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold. Let <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\in H_{1}(Y;\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in H_{1}(Y;\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> be a homology class such that <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\xi)+2\operatorname{PD}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>PD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\xi)+2\operatorname{PD}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is a torsion element of <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}(Y;\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}(Y;\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be an absolute <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-grading of the embedded contact homology <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ECH(Y,\xi,\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ECH(Y,\xi,\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the ECH Weyl law in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHR15</a>, Theorem 1.3]</cite> states that, for every sequence of non-zero homogeneous ECH classes <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k}\in ECH(Y,\xi,\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k}\in ECH(Y,\xi,\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> with grading <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\sigma_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\sigma_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> diverging to <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and for every contact form <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> defining <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>, the ECH spectral invariants <math id="S1.SS6.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\sigma_{k}}(Y,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\sigma_{k}}(Y,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the asymptotic formula</p>
<table id="S1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\sigma_{k}}(Y,\lambda)=\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(Y,\lambda)I(\sigma_{k})}+o(I(\sigma_{k})^{1/2})\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\sigma_{k}}(Y,\lambda)=\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(Y,\lambda)I(\sigma_{k})}+o(I(\sigma_{k})^{1/2})\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.p9.2" class="ltx_p">In view of Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm4" title="Theorem D (Subleading asymptotics ECH). ‣ 1.4. Subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, the following question is natural:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1.8</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are the error terms in the ECH Weyl law (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.6. Discussion and open problems ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>) bounded as well?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In this direction, our packing stability result Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A (Ball packing stability). ‣ 1.1. Ball packing stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> together with the arguments in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">CHR15</a>, §3]</cite> imply the inequality</p>
<table id="S1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\sigma_{k}}(Y,\lambda)\geq\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(Y,\lambda)I(\sigma_{k})}+O(1)\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\sigma_{k}}(Y,\lambda)\geq\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(Y,\lambda)I(\sigma_{k})}+O(1)\qquad(k\rightarrow\infty).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.p10.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, if the error terms are <math id="S1.SS6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> for one single contact form <math id="S1.SS6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> defining <math id="S1.SS6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>, then they are <math id="S1.SS6.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> for every contact form <math id="S1.SS6.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> defining <math id="S1.SS6.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, it is natural to explore packing stability in higher dimensions. Since Banyaga’s results apply in arbitrary dimensions, it seems promising to attempt to extend the <math id="S1.SS6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional symplectic embedding techniques developed in this paper to higher dimensions. However, the question of whether there exist higher-dimensional domains with rough boundaries for which packing stability fails remains wide open. Given the connections between symplectic packing and the algebraic structure of Hamiltonian diffeomorphism groups established in this paper, this question can be viewed as a variant of the higher-dimensional version of the simplicity conjecture, which asks whether the groups of compactly supported Hamiltonian homeomorphisms <math id="S1.SS6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\operatorname{Ham}}(B^{2n}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Ham</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\operatorname{Ham}}(B^{2n}(1))</annotation></semantics></math> and hameomorphisms <math id="S1.SS6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hameo}(B^{2n}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hameo</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hameo}(B^{2n}(1))</annotation></semantics></math> of the open ball <math id="S1.SS6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> in dimension <math id="S1.SS6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> are simple or not; see, for instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">MSS</a>]</cite>. We expect these questions to be of the same level of difficulty.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Acknowledgements</h4>

<div id="S1.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The author thanks Dan Cristofaro-Gardiner, Richard Hind, and Michael Hutchings for helpful discussions and Felix Schlenk for his interest and comments on a draft of this paper. The author is supported by Dr. Max Rössler, the Walter Haefner Foundation, and the ETH Zürich Foundation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms of surfaces with boundary</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a symplectic surface with boundary, we introduce Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms which are not required to restrict to the identity on the boundary. This is a slightly non-standard notion, but has appeared before in the literature in similar forms; see, for instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">ABHS18</a>, §2]</cite>. Moreover, we state Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 (Quantitative perfectness []). ‣ 2. Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms of surfaces with boundary ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edtb</a>]</cite>, which is a quantitative refinement of Banyaga’s perfectness and simplicity results for Hamiltonian diffeomorphism groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ban78</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ban97</a>]</cite>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold equipped with an area form. We allow <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to have boundary and do not require it to be compact. Let <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the group of all compactly supported diffeomorphisms of <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> which preserve the area form <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Diffeomorphisms <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are not required to restrict to the identity on the boundary <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider an isotopy <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the time dependent vector field generating <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a compactly supported area preserving isotopy, the vector field <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is compactly supported, tangent to the boundary <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, and satisfies <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{X_{t}}\omega=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{X_{t}}\omega=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, any such vector field <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> generates a compactly supported isotopy in <math id="S2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Vector fields <math id="S2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying these properties are in bijective correspondence with families of compactly supported closed <math id="S2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-forms <math id="S2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> which vanish on vectors tangent to the boundary <math id="S2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. This correspondence is characterized by the identity <math id="S2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=\iota_{X_{t}}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=\iota_{X_{t}}\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a time dependent Hamiltonian <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:[0,1]\times\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:[0,1]\times\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The induced family of closed <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-forms <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dH_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dH_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is compactly supported and vanishes on vectors tangent to the boundary <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if, for every fixed time <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the function <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally constant on the boundary <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and outside some compact subset of <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. If this is the case, we call the Hamiltonian <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-admissible</span>. If <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible, then the vector field <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{H_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{H_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> characterized by <math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dH_{t}=\iota_{X_{H_{t}}}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dH_{t}=\iota_{X_{H_{t}}}\omega</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isotopy <math id="S2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonians, we define new Hamiltonians <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\#G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\#G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H\#G)_{t}\coloneqq H_{t}+G_{t}\circ(\varphi_{G}^{t})^{-1}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\overline{H}_{t}\coloneqq-H_{t}\circ\varphi_{H}^{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mi>#</mi><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mtext>and</mtext><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">≔</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H\#G)_{t}\coloneqq H_{t}+G_{t}\circ(\varphi_{G}^{t})^{-1}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\overline{H}_{t}\coloneqq-H_{t}\circ\varphi_{H}^{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The Hamiltonians <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\#G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\#G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}</annotation></semantics></math> are also <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible. They generate the isotopies <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{t}\circ\varphi_{G}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{t}\circ\varphi_{G}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{H}^{t})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{H}^{t})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We call an isotopy <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> a <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compactly supported Hamiltonian isotopy</span> if it is of the form <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{t}=\varphi_{H}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{t}=\varphi_{H}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for a <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> which in addition is compactly supported and vanishes on the boundary <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. We call a diffeomorphism <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> a <span id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compactly supported Hamiltonian diffeomorphism</span> if there exists a compactly supported Hamiltonian isotopy <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> starting at <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{0}=\operatorname{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{0}=\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math> and ending at <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{1}=\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{1}=\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. The compactly supported Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms form a subgroup</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)\subset\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)\subset\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">We emphasize that since <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is only required to vanish on the boundary <math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> but not in a neighbourhood of it, Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms need not restrict to the identity on the boundary. We caution that while every <math id="S2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonian <math id="S2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isotopy in <math id="S2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Diff}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, this isotopy is not necessarily a Hamiltonian isotopy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose now that <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is connected and not closed. Let <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\operatorname{Ham}}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Ham</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\operatorname{Ham}}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the universal cover of <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Calabi homomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Cal}:\widetilde{\operatorname{Ham}}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cal</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Ham</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Cal}:\widetilde{\operatorname{Ham}}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">is defined as follows: Given an element <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\varphi}\in\widetilde{\operatorname{Ham}}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Ham</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\varphi}\in\widetilde{\operatorname{Ham}}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, choose a Hamiltonian isotopy <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> representing <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be the unique Hamiltonian generating <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> which is compactly supported and vanishes on the boundary <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Cal}(\widetilde{\varphi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Cal}(\widetilde{\varphi})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Cal}(\widetilde{\varphi})\coloneqq\int_{[0,1]\times\Sigma}Hdt\wedge\omega." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Cal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Cal}(\widetilde{\varphi})\coloneqq\int_{[0,1]\times\Sigma}Hdt\wedge\omega.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">This integral turns out to be independent of the choice of representative <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)\subset\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)\subset\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.4" class="ltx_p">to be the subgroup consisting of all Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> which possess a lift <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\varphi}\in\widetilde{\operatorname{Ham}}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Ham</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\varphi}\in\widetilde{\operatorname{Ham}}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> which is contained in the kernel of the Calabi homomorphism <math id="S2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Cal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Cal</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Cal}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For closed surfaces <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, it will be convenient to set <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)\coloneqq\operatorname{Ham}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)\coloneqq\operatorname{Ham}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the case that <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is disconnected, we define <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the subgroup consisting of all Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every connected component <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction of <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma^{\prime},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma^{\prime},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">It was proved by Banyaga in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ban78</a>]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ban97</a>, §4.3]</cite>) that if <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> does not have boundary, then the group <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is perfect, i.e. it agrees with its commutator subgroup. If in addition <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, then <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is also simple, i.e. it does not have any non-trivial normal subgroups. If <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> has non-empty boundary, then the group <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is never simple because the diffeomorphisms restricting to the identity on the boundary form a non-trival normal subgroup. However, we prove in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edtb</a>]</cite> that <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is still perfect. In the context of general diffeomorphism groups, an analogous result is obtained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ryb98</a>]</cite>. However, the methods there do not adapt well to the conservative setting and our proofs in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edtb</a>]</cite> rely on different tools.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For our purposes, the following refinement of the perfectness of <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edtb</a>]</cite> will be essential. In the non-conservative setting, similar results were obtained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">HRT13</a>]</cite>, but it is unclear whether the proofs there can be adapted to the conservative settings and our arguments in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edtb</a>]</cite> are rather different.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Quantitative perfectness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edtb</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a positive integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following is true. Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic surface, possibly with boundary and not necessarily compact, and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\Subset V\Subset\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⋐</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>⋐</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\Subset V\Subset\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be relatively compact open subsets. Consider the map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(V)^{2m}\rightarrow\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(V)\quad(u_{1},v_{1},\dots,u_{m},v_{m})\mapsto\prod\limits_{i=1}^{m}[u_{i},v_{i}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(V)^{2m}\rightarrow\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(V)\quad(u_{1},v_{1},\dots,u_{m},v_{m})\mapsto\prod\limits_{i=1}^{m}[u_{i},v_{i}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let us equip both <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> with the topology of uniform <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> convergence on the compact set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exist a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> open neighbourhood <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}\subset\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}\subset\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> of the identity and a continuous map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi:\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(V)^{2m}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi:\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(V)^{2m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which is a right inverse of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. which satisfies <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\circ\Psi=\operatorname{id}_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>id</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\circ\Psi=\operatorname{id}_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we can choose <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(\operatorname{id})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>id</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(\operatorname{id})</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrarily <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to the tuple <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\operatorname{id},\dots,\operatorname{id})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>id</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\operatorname{id},\dots,\operatorname{id})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The right inverse <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 (Quantitative perfectness []). ‣ 2. Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms of surfaces with boundary ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> can in fact be chosen to be smooth in a suitable sense, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edtb</a>]</cite>. However, we will not need this here.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Quantitative factorization into rotations</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main result of this section is Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, which allows us to write Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms of the annuls which are <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to the identity and have vanishing Calabi invariant as a composition of finitely many smooth conjugates of rotations in a quantitatively controlled way. A key ingredient is the quantitative perfectness result for Hamiltonian diffeomorphism groups Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 (Quantitative perfectness []). ‣ 2. Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms of surfaces with boundary ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. Some of our arguments are inspired by the proof that perfectness implies simplicity for certain groups of homeomorphisms; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eps70</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ban97</a>, Theorem 2.1.7]</cite>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{T}\coloneqq\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{T}\coloneqq\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the circle and consider the annulus <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}\coloneqq[0,1]\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}\coloneqq[0,1]\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with coordinates <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and the area form <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\coloneqq dx\wedge dy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\coloneqq dx\wedge dy</annotation></semantics></math>. For every real number <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the autonomous Hamiltonian</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\alpha}:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad H_{\alpha}(t,x,y)\coloneqq\alpha x." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\alpha}:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad H_{\alpha}(t,x,y)\coloneqq\alpha x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The Hamiltonian <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible and the Hamiltonian vector field of <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{H_{\alpha}}=-\alpha\partial_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{H_{\alpha}}=-\alpha\partial_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. The time-<math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> map <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H_{\alpha}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H_{\alpha}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the induced flow is the rotation</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha}:\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{A}\qquad R_{\alpha}(x,y)\coloneqq(x,y-\alpha)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha}:\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{A}\qquad R_{\alpha}(x,y)\coloneqq(x,y-\alpha).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists an integer <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> divisible by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following statement is true for every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and for every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> neighbourhood <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}\subset\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Ham</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}\subset\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> of the identity:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every Hamiltonian diffeomorphism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Ham</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> which is sufficiently <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to the identity, there exist Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1},\dots,\psi_{\ell}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1},\dots,\psi_{\ell}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> depending continuously on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> topology and satisfying the following property: For <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}\coloneqq+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}\coloneqq+1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is congruent to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> modulo <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}\coloneqq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}\coloneqq-1</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. Moreover, define <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}\coloneqq\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}\coloneqq\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi=\prod\limits_{i=1}^{\ell}\varphi_{H_{i}}^{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=\prod\limits_{i=1}^{\ell}\varphi_{H_{i}}^{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let us abbreviate the two components of <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{+}\mathbb{A}\coloneqq\left\{1\right\}\times\mathbb{T}\quad\text{and}\quad\partial_{-}\mathbb{A}\coloneqq\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.000em;" width="1.000em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>−</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{+}\mathbb{A}\coloneqq\left\{1\right\}\times\mathbb{T}\quad\text{and}\quad\partial_{-}\mathbb{A}\coloneqq\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer as in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. Then for every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> neighbourhood <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}\subset\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Ham</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}\subset\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> of the identity, and every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> neighbourhood <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}\subset C^{\infty}([0,1]\times\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}\subset C^{\infty}([0,1]\times\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> of zero, the following statement holds:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is sufficiently <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\limits_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}Hdt\wedge\omega=0\quad\text{and}\quad\int\limits_{[0,1]}H(t,\partial_{\pm}\mathbb{A})dt=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\limits_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}Hdt\wedge\omega=0\quad\text{and}\quad\int\limits_{[0,1]}H(t,\partial_{\pm}\mathbb{A})dt=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">there exists a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter family of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonians <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> starting at <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}=H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}=H</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfying the following properties:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The time-</span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-map </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H^{\lambda}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H^{\lambda}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is independent of </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\limits_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}H^{\lambda}dt\wedge\omega=0\quad\text{and}\quad\int_{[0,1]}H^{\lambda}(t,\partial_{\pm}\mathbb{A})dt=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\limits_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}H^{\lambda}dt\wedge\omega=0\quad\text{and}\quad\int_{[0,1]}H^{\lambda}(t,\partial_{\pm}\mathbb{A})dt=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, we have </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\lambda}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\lambda}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}=\#_{i=1}^{\ell}\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>#</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}=\#_{i=1}^{\ell}\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the signs </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are defined as in Theorem </span><a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that equation (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Corollary 3.2. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) implies that <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\coloneqq\varphi_{H}^{1}\in\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>≔</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Ham</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\coloneqq\varphi_{H}^{1}\in\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, define a <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonian <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> by</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad K(t,x,y)\coloneqq-xH(t,\partial_{+}\mathbb{A})-(1-x)H(t,\partial_{-}\mathbb{A})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>−</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad K(t,x,y)\coloneqq-xH(t,\partial_{+}\mathbb{A})-(1-x)H(t,\partial_{-}\mathbb{A}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\#H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\#H</annotation></semantics></math> clearly vanishes on <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, it follows from equation (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Corollary 3.2. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) that <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{K}^{1}=\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>𝔸</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{K}^{1}=\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}Kdt\wedge\omega=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}Kdt\wedge\omega=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{K\#H}^{1}=\varphi_{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{K\#H}^{1}=\varphi_{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}K\#Hdt\wedge\omega=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}K\#Hdt\wedge\omega=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We deduce that <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{H}^{1}\in\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Ham</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{H}^{1}\in\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We can therefore apply Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> to <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. Proving Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 3.2. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> then boils down to proving the following elementary assertion:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be two <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> small <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonians on <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{G^{0}}^{1}=\varphi_{G^{1}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{G^{0}}^{1}=\varphi_{G^{1}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\limits_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}G^{\lambda}dt\wedge\omega=0\quad\text{and}\quad\int_{[0,1]}G^{\lambda}(t,\partial_{\pm}\mathbb{A})dt=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\limits_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}G^{\lambda}dt\wedge\omega=0\quad\text{and}\quad\int_{[0,1]}G^{\lambda}(t,\partial_{\pm}\mathbb{A})dt=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">for <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\left\{0,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\left\{0,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a family <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> small <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonians on <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfying <math id="S3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{G^{\lambda}}^{1}=\varphi_{G^{0}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{G^{\lambda}}^{1}=\varphi_{G^{0}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and identity (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) for all <math id="S3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">In order to see this, first note that we can connect <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to a Hamiltonian which vanishes on <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Define</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\lambda}:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad K^{\lambda}(t,x,y)\coloneqq-\lambda(xG^{0}(t,\partial_{+}\mathbb{A})+(1-x)G^{0}(t,\partial_{-}\mathbb{A}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>−</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\lambda}:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad K^{\lambda}(t,x,y)\coloneqq-\lambda(xG^{0}(t,\partial_{+}\mathbb{A})+(1-x)G^{0}(t,\partial_{-}\mathbb{A})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">As already observed above for the Hamiltonian <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{K^{\lambda}}^{1}=\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>𝔸</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{K^{\lambda}}^{1}=\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K^{\lambda}\#G^{0})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K^{\lambda}\#G^{0})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> connects <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the Hamiltonian <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{1}\#G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{1}\#G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which vanishes on <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Via an analogous construction, we connect <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to a Hamiltonian vanishing on <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We may therefore replace <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by Hamiltonians vanishing on <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this situation, the Hamiltonians <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\left\{0,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\left\{0,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> generate paths <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{G^{j}}^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{G^{j}}^{t})_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> starting at the identity and ending at <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. The desired claim follows from the fact that <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> is locally contractible with respect to the <math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> topology. As in the usual case of Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms on symplectic manifolds without boundary, this can be proved using generating functions. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">ABHS18</a>, §2]</cite> for a discussion of generating functions in the context of Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms which do not necessarily restrict to the identity on the boundary.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Before turning to the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we establish some preliminary lemmas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a topological space and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be an open subset. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> be homeomorphisms of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> supported in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> be a homeomorphisms of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that the three sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(U)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(U)</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise disjoint. Then the following identity holds:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[u,v]=[[u,\varphi],[v,\psi]]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[u,v]=[[u,\varphi],[v,\psi]].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">We observe that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{supp}([u,\varphi])\subset U\cup\varphi(U)\quad\text{and}\quad[u,\varphi]|_{U}=u|_{U}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{supp}([u,\varphi])\subset U\cup\varphi(U)\quad\text{and}\quad[u,\varphi]|_{U}=u|_{U}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{supp}([v,\psi])\subset U\cup\psi(U)\quad\text{and}\quad[v,\psi]|_{U}=v|_{U}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{supp}([v,\psi])\subset U\cup\psi(U)\quad\text{and}\quad[v,\psi]|_{U}=v|_{U}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since any two homeomorphisms with disjoint supports commute, the desired identity (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) is an immediate consequence of (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In Proof. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be an open subset of the annulus. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and assume that the sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha}^{-1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha}^{-1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise disjoint. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> be compactly supported Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms. Then we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[u,v]=\varphi_{(u^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-(v^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-(u^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{(v^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[u,v]=\varphi_{(u^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-(v^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-(u^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{(v^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">We apply Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> with <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi=R_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=R_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi=R_{\alpha}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi=R_{\alpha}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluate the right hand side of identity (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The following fragmentation lemma is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edtb</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},\dots,U_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},\dots,U_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an open covering of the annulus <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Ham</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> which is sufficiently <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> close to the identity, there exists a fragmentation</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi=\varphi_{1}\circ\cdots\circ\varphi_{n}\qquad\text{with}\kern 5.0pt\varphi_{i}\in\operatorname{Ham}^{0}_{c}(U_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">with</mtext><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=\varphi_{1}\circ\cdots\circ\varphi_{n}\qquad\text{with}\kern 5.0pt\varphi_{i}\in\operatorname{Ham}^{0}_{c}(U_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The diffeomorphisms <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> depend continuously on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> topology on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Ham</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ham}^{0}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> and if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi=\operatorname{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}=\operatorname{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}=\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">For every <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{r}(p)\subset\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{r}(p)\subset\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the open ball of radius <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="S3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, where we equip <math id="S3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> with the distance function induced by the Riemannian metric <math id="S3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dx^{2}+dy^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dx^{2}+dy^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every positive integer <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the lattice</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{k}\coloneqq(\frac{1}{k}\mathbb{Z}\cap[0,1])\times(\frac{1}{k}\mathbb{Z}/\mathbb{Z})\subset\mathbb{A}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{k}\coloneqq(\frac{1}{k}\mathbb{Z}\cap[0,1])\times(\frac{1}{k}\mathbb{Z}/\mathbb{Z})\subset\mathbb{A}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then there exists a positive integer <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> with the following property: For every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and for every positive integer <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2}{k}&lt;\operatorname{dist}(\alpha,\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2}{k}&lt;\operatorname{dist}(\alpha,\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for any two distinct points <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq q\in P^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq q\in P^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of the same color, the six sets</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p),\kern 5.0ptR_{\alpha}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)),\kern 5.0ptR_{\alpha}^{-1}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)),\kern 5.0ptB_{\frac{1}{k}}(q),\kern 5.0ptR_{\alpha}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q)),\kern 5.0ptR_{\alpha}^{-1}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p),\kern 5.0ptR_{\alpha}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)),\kern 5.0ptR_{\alpha}^{-1}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)),\kern 5.0ptB_{\frac{1}{k}}(q),\kern 5.0ptR_{\alpha}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q)),\kern 5.0ptR_{\alpha}^{-1}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">are pairwise disjoint.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive integer such that <math id="S3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2}{k}&lt;\operatorname{dist}(\alpha,\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2}{k}&lt;\operatorname{dist}(\alpha,\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that this condition implies that, for every point <math id="S3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, the three sets <math id="S3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p))</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha}^{-1}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha}^{-1}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p))</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise disjoint. We construct a graph <math id="S3.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with vertex set <math id="S3.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows: For any two distinct <math id="S3.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq q\in P^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq q\in P^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we insert an edge between <math id="S3.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the intersection</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)\cup R_{\alpha}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p))\cup R_{\alpha}^{-1}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)))\cap(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q)\cup R_{\alpha}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q))\cup R_{\alpha}^{-1}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q)))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)\cup R_{\alpha}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p))\cup R_{\alpha}^{-1}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)))\cap(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q)\cup R_{\alpha}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q))\cup R_{\alpha}^{-1}(B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q)))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">is non-empty. Note that vertex colorings of <math id="S3.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. colorings of the vertex set of <math id="S3.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that no two vertices of the same color are connected by an edge, are precisely colorings of the lattice <math id="S3.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the property in the statement of the lemma. Hence our task is to show that the chromatic number <math id="S3.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the least number of colors needed to color <math id="S3.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, admits an upper bound independent of <math id="S3.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the maximal vertex degree of <math id="S3.p14.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the maximal number of incident edges of any vertex of <math id="S3.p14.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. By Brooks’ theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bro41</a>]</cite>, the chromatic number of any graph satisfies the bound <math id="S3.p14.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(G)\leq\Delta(G)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(G)\leq\Delta(G)+1</annotation></semantics></math>. We observe that, for each <math id="S3.p14.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in P^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in P^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist at most <math id="S3.p14.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> distinct points <math id="S3.p14.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in P^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in P^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p14.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p14.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\frac{1}{k}}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> have non-empty intersection. This implies that <math id="S3.p14.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> admits an upper bound independent of <math id="S3.p14.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p14.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. We deduce that the same is true for <math id="S3.p14.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, which concludes the proof of the lemma.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Pick a positive integer <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the conclusion of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 (Quantitative perfectness []). ‣ 2. Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms of surfaces with boundary ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> holds. Moreover, pick a positive integer <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the conclusion of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> holds. Our goal is to show that Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> holds with</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\coloneqq 8mn." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\coloneqq 8mn.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a positive integer <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2}{k}&lt;\operatorname{dist}(\alpha,\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2}{k}&lt;\operatorname{dist}(\alpha,\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Pick an <math id="S3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring of the lattice <math id="S3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{k}\subset\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{k}\subset\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>. Fix a number <math id="S3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}&lt;r&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}&lt;r&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. For each color <math id="S3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}\coloneqq\bigcup\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}p\in P^{k}\\
\text{$p$ has color $i$}\end{subarray}}B_{\frac{r}{k}}(p)\qquad\text{and}\qquad V_{i}\coloneqq\bigcup\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}p\in P^{k}\\
\text{$p$ has color $i$}\end{subarray}}B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mtext> has color </mtext><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mtext> has color </mtext><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}\coloneqq\bigcup\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}p\in P^{k}\\
\text{$p$ has color $i$}\end{subarray}}B_{\frac{r}{k}}(p)\qquad\text{and}\qquad V_{i}\coloneqq\bigcup\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}p\in P^{k}\\
\text{$p$ has color $i$}\end{subarray}}B_{\frac{1}{k}}(p)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">Both unions are disjoint and <math id="S3.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}\Subset V_{i}\Subset\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋐</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋐</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}\Subset V_{i}\Subset\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the property of the coloring of <math id="S3.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in the statement of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, the sets <math id="S3.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha}^{-1}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha}^{-1}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise disjoint. Moreover, note that <math id="S3.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{i})_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{i})_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> forms an open cover of <math id="S3.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to the identity. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, there exists a fragmentation</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi=\varphi_{1}\circ\cdots\circ\varphi_{n}\quad\text{with $\varphi_{i}\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(U_{i})$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>φ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mtext>with </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=\varphi_{1}\circ\cdots\circ\varphi_{n}\quad\text{with $\varphi_{i}\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(U_{i})$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">The diffeomorphisms <math id="S3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> depend continuously on <math id="S3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> and are <math id="S3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to the identity. By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 (Quantitative perfectness []). ‣ 2. Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms of surfaces with boundary ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, we can write</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi_{i}=[u_{i}^{1},v_{i}^{1}]\circ\cdots\circ[u_{i}^{m},v_{i}^{m}]\quad\text{with $u_{i}^{j},v_{i}^{j}\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(V_{i})$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext>with </mtext><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ham</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}=[u_{i}^{1},v_{i}^{1}]\circ\cdots\circ[u_{i}^{m},v_{i}^{m}]\quad\text{with $u_{i}^{j},v_{i}^{j}\in\operatorname{Ham}_{c}^{0}(V_{i})$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p17.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.3.1" class="ltx_text">The diffeomorphisms <math id="S3.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> also depend continuously on <math id="S3.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> and are <math id="S3.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to the identity.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[u_{i}^{j},v_{i}^{j}]=\varphi_{((u_{i}^{j})^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-((v_{i}^{j})^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-((u_{i}^{j})^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{((v_{i}^{j})^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[u_{i}^{j},v_{i}^{j}]=\varphi_{((u_{i}^{j})^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-((v_{i}^{j})^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-((u_{i}^{j})^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{-H_{\alpha}}^{1}\circ\varphi_{((v_{i}^{j})^{-1})^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.2.1" class="ltx_text">For an appropriate choice of Hamiltonian diffoemorphisms <math id="S3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1},\dots,\psi_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1},\dots,\psi_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> from the set</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\operatorname{id},(u_{i}^{j})^{-1},(v_{i}^{j})^{-1}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\operatorname{id},(u_{i}^{j})^{-1},(v_{i}^{j})^{-1}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.3.1" class="ltx_text">we then have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi=\prod\limits_{i=1}^{\ell}\varphi_{\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=\prod\limits_{i=1}^{\ell}\varphi_{\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha}}^{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.4.1" class="ltx_text">Since the diffeomorphisms <math id="S3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> depend continuously on <math id="S3.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> and are <math id="S3.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to the identity, the same if true for the diffeomorphisms <math id="S3.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Stratification of subgraphs</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we prove Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, which says that the subgraph of a Hamiltonian on the annulus which is a <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> small perturbation of an affine Hamiltonian can be decomposed into domains symplectomorphic to subgraphs of affine Hamiltonians. This decomposition induces a decomposition into symplectic ellipsoids and polydisks; see Remark <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Remark 4.2. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It will be useful to introduce the following notation. Given a set <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and functions <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,g:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,g:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(f,g)\subset\mathbb{R}\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(f,g)\subset\mathbb{R}\times X</annotation></semantics></math> denote the intersection between the supergraph of <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and the subgraph of <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. we set</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(f,g)\coloneqq\left\{(s,x)\in\mathbb{R}\times X\mid f(x)\leq s\leq g(x)\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(f,g)\coloneqq\left\{(s,x)\in\mathbb{R}\times X\mid f(x)\leq s\leq g(x)\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we abbreviate <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(f)\coloneqq D(0,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(f)\coloneqq D(0,f)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the subgraph of <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, truncated at <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We are particularly interested in the case that the set <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=[0,1]\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=[0,1]\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for a symplectic surface <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, we equip <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\coloneqq\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\coloneqq\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with the symplectic form <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\coloneqq ds\wedge dt+\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\coloneqq ds\wedge dt+\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> are the coordinates on <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. This turns the domains <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(f,g)\subset(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(f,g)\subset(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> into symplectic manifolds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> that <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}=[0,1]\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}=[0,1]\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the annulus. Moreover, recall that the Hamiltonian <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\alpha}:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\alpha}:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\alpha}(t,x,y)=\alpha x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\alpha}(t,x,y)=\alpha x</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a real number <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the annulus of area <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, we set <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}(a)\coloneqq[0,a]\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}(a)\coloneqq[0,a]\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and equip it with the area form <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\coloneqq dx\wedge dy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\coloneqq dx\wedge dy</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Hamiltonian of the form <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(t,x,y)=a+bx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(t,x,y)=a+bx</annotation></semantics></math> for real numbers <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly positive. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonian. If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists a stratification <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the closure <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of any top-dimensional stratum <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic either to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H_{\alpha}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H_{\alpha}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})</annotation></semantics></math> for a rational number <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=p/q&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=p/q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a real number <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> or to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(C|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(C|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})</annotation></semantics></math> for a positive constant function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and a real number <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists an upper bound on the denominator <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> which can be chosen uniformly among all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to a fixed <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, there exists a uniform positive lower bound on the constants <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The full volume of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H_{\alpha}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H_{\alpha}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})</annotation></semantics></math> can be filled by a single copy of the ellipsoid <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(c,\alpha c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(c,\alpha c)</annotation></semantics></math>; see Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Similarly, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(C|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(C|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})</annotation></semantics></math> can be fully filled by the polydisk <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(c,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(c,C)</annotation></semantics></math> of widths <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> therefore says that every sufficiently small perturbation of an affine subgraph can be fully packed by some finite collection of polydisks and rational ellipsoids.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with some preliminary lemmas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact surface, possibly with boundary. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be an area form on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:[0,1]\times\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:[0,1]\times\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonian. Then the map</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{H}:\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times\Sigma\qquad(s,t,p)\mapsto(s+H(t,\varphi_{H}^{t}(p)),t,\varphi_{H}^{t}(p))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{H}:\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times\Sigma\qquad(s,t,p)\mapsto(s+H(t,\varphi_{H}^{t}(p)),t,\varphi_{H}^{t}(p))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a symplectomorphism of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F,G:[0,1]\times\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F,G:[0,1]\times\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonians, then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(F,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(F,G)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H\#F,H\#G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H\#F,H\#G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Both assertions follow from a straightforward direct computation.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact connected surface, possibly with boundary. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be an area form on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\pm}^{\lambda}:[0,1]\times\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\pm}^{\lambda}:[0,1]\times\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be two smooth families of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonians on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> parametrized by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that the strict inequality <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{+}^{\lambda}&gt;H_{-}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{+}^{\lambda}&gt;H_{-}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the following two statements are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a smooth family </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of symplectomorphisms of </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> starting at the identity such that </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}(D(H_{-}^{0},H_{+}^{0}))=D(H_{-}^{\lambda},H_{+}^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}(D(H_{-}^{0},H_{+}^{0}))=D(H_{-}^{\lambda},H_{+}^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following three assertions hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a smooth isotopy </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\chi^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\chi^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of area preserving diffeomorphisms of </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> starting at the identity such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{H_{-}^{\lambda}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{H_{+}^{\lambda}}^{1}=\chi^{\lambda}\circ((\varphi_{H_{-}^{0}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{H_{+}^{0}}^{1})\circ(\chi^{\lambda})^{-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{H_{-}^{\lambda}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{H_{+}^{\lambda}}^{1}=\chi^{\lambda}\circ((\varphi_{H_{-}^{0}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{H_{+}^{0}}^{1})\circ(\chi^{\lambda})^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all boundary components </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the quantity</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{[0,1]}(H_{+}^{\lambda}(t,S)-H_{-}^{\lambda}(t,S))dt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{[0,1]}(H_{+}^{\lambda}(t,S)-H_{-}^{\lambda}(t,S))dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is independent of </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The volume</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(D(H_{-}^{\lambda},H_{+}^{\lambda}))=\int_{[0,1]\times\Sigma}(H_{+}^{\lambda}-H_{-}^{\lambda})dt\wedge\omega" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(D(H_{-}^{\lambda},H_{+}^{\lambda}))=\int_{[0,1]\times\Sigma}(H_{+}^{\lambda}-H_{-}^{\lambda})dt\wedge\omega</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is independent of </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us abbreviate <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\lambda}\coloneqq D(H_{-}^{\lambda},H_{+}^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\lambda}\coloneqq D(H_{-}^{\lambda},H_{+}^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a manifold with boundary and corners. The boundary <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> has the following top dimensional strata:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}\coloneqq\operatorname{graph}(H_{\pm}^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}\coloneqq\operatorname{graph}(H_{\pm}^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}\coloneqq(\mathbb{R}\times\left\{j\right\}\times\Sigma)\cap A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}\coloneqq(\mathbb{R}\times\left\{j\right\}\times\Sigma)\cap A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\left\{0,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\left\{0,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}\coloneqq(\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times S)\cap A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}\coloneqq(\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times S)\cap A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a component of <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Restricting the natural projection <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a fibration of <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> over the interval <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with fiber <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. The strata <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\left\{0,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\left\{0,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> also fiber over the interval with fiber <math id="S4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. We can choose the fibration such that the fibers are transverse to the vertical vector field <math id="S4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the Hamiltonians <math id="S4.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\pm}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\pm}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> are assumed to be <math id="S4.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible, there exists, for every boundary component <math id="S4.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, a subset <math id="S4.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{S}^{\lambda}\subset\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{S}^{\lambda}\subset\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}=Q_{S}^{\lambda}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}=Q_{S}^{\lambda}\times S</annotation></semantics></math>. The set <math id="S4.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{S}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{S}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smoothly embedded strip in <math id="S4.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="S4.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}\times\left\{0\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}\times\left\{0\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}\times\left\{1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}\times\left\{1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The area of <math id="S4.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{S}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{S}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the integral</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{area}(Q_{S}^{\lambda})=\int_{[0,1]}(H_{+}^{\lambda}(t,S)-H_{-}^{\lambda}(t,S))dt." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{area}(Q_{S}^{\lambda})=\int_{[0,1]}(H_{+}^{\lambda}(t,S)-H_{-}^{\lambda}(t,S))dt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">The symplectic form <math id="S4.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> induces a characteristic foliation on <math id="S4.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us orient this foliation such that the orientation of the foliation followed by the coorientation induced by the restriction of <math id="S4.p7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> yields the boundary orientation on <math id="S4.p7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the strata <math id="S4.p7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the characteristic foliation is positively tangent to the vector field</span></p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pm(\partial_{t}H_{\pm}^{\lambda}\cdot\partial_{s}+\partial_{t}+X_{H_{\pm}^{\lambda}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm(\partial_{t}H_{\pm}^{\lambda}\cdot\partial_{s}+\partial_{t}+X_{H_{\pm}^{\lambda}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">In particular, it is transverse to the fibers of <math id="S4.p7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. The boundary of the boundary strata <math id="S4.p7.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of two types of strata: the fibers over <math id="S4.p7.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> of the projection <math id="S4.p7.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and the annuli of the form <math id="S4.p7.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}\cap\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∩</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}\cap\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p7.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a component of <math id="S4.p7.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. The characteristic foliation is transverse to the former type. Since the Hamiltonians <math id="S4.p7.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\pm}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\pm}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> are assumed to be <math id="S4.p7.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible, the characteristic foliation is tangent to the latter type. On the strata <math id="S4.p7.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the characteristic foliation is positively tangent to <math id="S4.p7.m41" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(-1)^{j}\partial_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{j}\partial_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. For our choice of fibration <math id="S4.p7.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, it is therefore transverse to the fibers. The union</span></p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}\coloneqq\partial_{+}A^{\lambda}\cup\partial_{1}A^{\lambda}\cup\partial_{-}A^{\lambda}\cup\partial_{0}A^{\lambda}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>−</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}\coloneqq\partial_{+}A^{\lambda}\cup\partial_{1}A^{\lambda}\cup\partial_{-}A^{\lambda}\cup\partial_{0}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.5.1" class="ltx_text">is not smooth at the corners <math id="S4.p7.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}\cap\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∩</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}\cap\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the above discussion, the characteristic foliation is transverse to these corners. Moreover, it is tangent to the boundary of <math id="S4.p7.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the characteristic foliation induces a flow on <math id="S4.p7.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> which is well-defined up positive reparametrization. This flow is transverse to the fibers of the fibrations of the strata <math id="S4.p7.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, each fiber is a surface of section of the flow. Consider the fiber over <math id="S4.p7.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> of the projection <math id="S4.p7.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{+}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{+}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p7.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\lambda}:\Sigma\hookrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\lambda}:\Sigma\hookrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> denote the parametrization of this fiber which has the property the composition with the natural projection <math id="S4.p7.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\rightarrow\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\rightarrow\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the identity map of <math id="S4.p7.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. With respect to the parametrization <math id="S4.p7.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the first return map of the flow is given by <math id="S4.p7.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{H_{-}^{\lambda}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{H_{+}^{\lambda}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{H_{-}^{\lambda}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{H_{+}^{\lambda}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, we can regard <math id="S4.p7.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> as a (non-smooth) mapping torus of the diffeomorphism <math id="S4.p7.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{H_{-}^{\lambda}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{H_{+}^{\lambda}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{H_{-}^{\lambda}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{H_{+}^{\lambda}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the strata <math id="S4.p7.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the characteristic foliation is tangent to the circles <math id="S4.p7.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{s\right\}\times\left\{t\right\}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{s\right\}\times\left\{t\right\}\times S</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we can regard <math id="S4.p7.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> as the mapping torus of the identity map on the strip <math id="S4.p7.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{S}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{S}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is an easy consequence of the above discussion that statement (<a href="#S4.I1.i1" title="item 1 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> implies statement (<a href="#S4.I1.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>): A family of symplectomorphisms <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S4.I1.i1" title="item 1 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) clearly has to preserve <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(A^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(A^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{area}(Q_{S}^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{area}(Q_{S}^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, it maps the characteristic foliation on the boundary of <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the characteristic foliation on the boundary of <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus it induces a familiy of conjugating diffeomorphisms for the first return maps of the mapping tori <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, assume that statement (<a href="#S4.I1.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) in Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> holds. Our construction of the family of symplectomorphisms <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> proceeds in several steps.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Step 1:</em><span id="S4.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text"> We reduce ourselves to the case that <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\lambda}=\operatorname{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\lambda}=\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. To this end, we define Hamiltonians <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{\pm}^{\lambda}\coloneqq H_{\pm}^{\lambda}\circ(\chi^{\lambda})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{\pm}^{\lambda}\coloneqq H_{\pm}^{\lambda}\circ(\chi^{\lambda})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from assumption (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i1" title="item 2i ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2i</span></a>) that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{\widetilde{H}_{-}^{\lambda}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{\widetilde{H}_{+}^{\lambda}}^{1}=(\varphi_{\widetilde{H}_{-}^{0}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{\widetilde{H}_{+}^{0}}^{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{\widetilde{H}_{-}^{\lambda}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{\widetilde{H}_{+}^{\lambda}}^{1}=(\varphi_{\widetilde{H}_{-}^{0}}^{1})^{-1}\circ\varphi_{\widetilde{H}_{+}^{0}}^{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">In other words, the Hamiltonians <math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{\pm}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{\pm}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy assumption (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i1" title="item 2i ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2i</span></a>) with <math id="S4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\chi}^{\lambda}=\operatorname{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\chi}^{\lambda}=\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the Hamiltonians <math id="S4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{\pm}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{\pm}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy assumptions (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i2" title="item 2ii ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2ii</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i3" title="item 2iii ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2iii</span></a>). Let <math id="S4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{A}^{\lambda}\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{A}^{\lambda}\subset M</annotation></semantics></math> denote the region between the graphs of <math id="S4.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{\pm}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{\pm}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is not hard to see that if statement (<a href="#S4.I1.i1" title="item 1 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) holds for the family of sets <math id="S4.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{A}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{A}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, then it also holds for <math id="S4.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, given a family of symplectomorphisms <math id="S4.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\psi}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\psi}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> starting at the identity and mapping <math id="S4.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{A}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{A}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{A}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{A}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, simply define</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}\coloneqq(\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]}\times\chi^{\lambda})^{-1}\circ\widetilde{\psi}^{\lambda}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>id</mi><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}\coloneqq(\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]}\times\chi^{\lambda})^{-1}\circ\widetilde{\psi}^{\lambda}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">The symplectomorphism <math id="S4.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S4.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}=\widetilde{A}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}=\widetilde{A}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. After replacing <math id="S4.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\pm}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\pm}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{\pm}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>±</mo><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{\pm}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can therefore assume that <math id="S4.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\lambda}=\operatorname{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\lambda}=\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Step 2:</em><span id="S4.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text"> We define a family of homeomorphisms</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}:T^{0}\rightarrow T^{\lambda}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}:T^{0}\rightarrow T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">with the following properties:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{0}=\operatorname{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{0}=\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> maps the fiber over <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> of the fibration <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{+}A^{0}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{+}A^{0}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to the fiber over <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{+}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{+}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the parametrizations <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ι</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> of these fibers defined above. With respect to these parametrizations, the restriction of <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to these fibers is given by the identity map <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>id</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, the boundary of <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> fibers over <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{S}\partial Q_{S}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">∂</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{S}\partial Q_{S}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> with circle fibers of the form <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{s\right\}\times\left\{t\right\}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{s\right\}\times\left\{t\right\}\times S</annotation></semantics></math>. The map <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> maps circle fibers of <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial T^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial T^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to circle fibers of <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(d)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The restriction of <math id="S4.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a diffeomorphism onto <math id="S4.I3.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(e)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.I3.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\left\{0,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\left\{0,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction of <math id="S4.I3.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.I3.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{j}A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{j}A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a diffeomorphism onto <math id="S4.I3.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(f)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I3.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S4.I3.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.I3.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi^{\lambda})^{*}\Omega|_{T^{\lambda}}=\Omega|_{T^{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi^{\lambda})^{*}\Omega|_{T^{\lambda}}=\Omega|_{T^{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, <math id="S4.I3.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> extends to a family of symplectomorphisms between open neighbourhoods of <math id="S4.I3.i6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I3.i6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.I3.i6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.p11.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.3.1" class="ltx_text">The construction uses assumption (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i1" title="item 2i ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2i</span></a>). Here are the details. Let <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi^{\lambda}_{t})_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi^{\lambda}_{t})_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be a parametrization of the flow on <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the characteristic foliation. We can choose <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\lambda}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\lambda}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the time it takes a flow line to traverse each of the strata <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{j}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly <math id="S4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we can choose <math id="S4.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{t}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{t}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for each fixed <math id="S4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, circle fibers on the boundary of <math id="S4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> are mapped to circle fibers. It follows from assumption (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i1" title="item 2i ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2i</span></a>) and the reduction in Step 1 that there exists a unique family of homeomorphisms <math id="S4.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}:T^{0}\rightarrow T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}:T^{0}\rightarrow T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a conjugacy between the flows <math id="S4.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi^{0}_{t})_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi^{0}_{t})_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi^{\lambda}_{t})_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi^{\lambda}_{t})_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi^{\lambda})^{-1}\circ\varphi^{\lambda}_{t}\circ\psi^{\lambda}=\varphi^{0}_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi^{\lambda})^{-1}\circ\varphi^{\lambda}_{t}\circ\psi^{\lambda}=\varphi^{0}_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.4.1" class="ltx_text">and such that property (<a href="#S4.I3.i2" title="item b ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">b</span></a>) holds. The family of homeomorphisms <math id="S4.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> clearly satisfies properties (<a href="#S4.I3.i1" title="item a ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">a</span></a>) - (<a href="#S4.I3.i5" title="item e ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">e</span></a>). Moreover, we have <math id="S4.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi^{\lambda})^{*}\Omega|_{T^{\lambda}}=\Omega|_{T^{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi^{\lambda})^{*}\Omega|_{T^{\lambda}}=\Omega|_{T^{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{t}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{t}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen with some care near the non-smooth locus of <math id="S4.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can extend <math id="S4.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to a symplectomorphism between open neighbourhoods of <math id="S4.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore also guarantee property (<a href="#S4.I3.i6" title="item f ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">f</span></a>).
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Step 3:</em><span id="S4.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text"> We extend the family of homeomorphism <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> from Step 2 to a family of homeomorphisms</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}:\partial A^{0}\rightarrow\partial A^{\lambda}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}:\partial A^{0}\rightarrow\partial A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">such that:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I4.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(g)</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I4.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{0}=\operatorname{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{0}=\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(h)</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I4.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to a diffeomorphism between <math id="S4.I4.i8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I4.i8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.I4.i8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I4.i9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S4.I4.i9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> and extends to a symplectomorphism between open neighbourhoods of <math id="S4.I4.i9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I4.i9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.I4.i9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.p12.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.3.1" class="ltx_text">The construction makes use of assumption (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i2" title="item 2ii ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2ii</span></a>). Recall that <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}=Q_{S}^{\lambda}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}=Q_{S}^{\lambda}\times S</annotation></semantics></math>. The intersection of <math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial(\partial_{S}A^{\lambda})=\partial Q_{S}^{\lambda}\times S." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial(\partial_{S}A^{\lambda})=\partial Q_{S}^{\lambda}\times S.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p12.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.4.1" class="ltx_text">By property (<a href="#S4.I3.i3" title="item c ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">c</span></a>), the restriction of <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Q_{S}^{0}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Q_{S}^{0}\times S</annotation></semantics></math> maps circle fibers of <math id="S4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Q_{S}^{0}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Q_{S}^{0}\times S</annotation></semantics></math> to circle fibers of <math id="S4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Q_{S}^{\lambda}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Q_{S}^{\lambda}\times S</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, it descends to a homeomorphism <math id="S4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\psi}^{\lambda}:\partial Q_{S}^{0}\rightarrow\partial Q_{S}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\psi}^{\lambda}:\partial Q_{S}^{0}\rightarrow\partial Q_{S}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> which maps smooth strata diffeomorphically to smooth strata. Since <math id="S4.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{S}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{S}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{S}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{S}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same total area by assumption (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i2" title="item 2ii ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2ii</span></a>), we can extend to an area preserving diffeomorphism <math id="S4.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\psi}^{\lambda}:Q_{S}^{0}\rightarrow Q_{S}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\psi}^{\lambda}:Q_{S}^{0}\rightarrow Q_{S}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the extension of <math id="S4.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{S}^{0}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{S}^{0}\times S</annotation></semantics></math> to be a lift <math id="S4.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{S}^{0}\times S\rightarrow Q_{S}^{\lambda}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{S}^{0}\times S\rightarrow Q_{S}^{\lambda}\times S</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\psi}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\psi}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> which agrees with the map <math id="S4.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> already defined on the boundary <math id="S4.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Q_{S}^{0}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Q_{S}^{0}\times S</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, this extension restricts to a diffeomorphism between <math id="S4.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, it is compatible with <math id="S4.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> since it lifts the area preserving diffeomorphism <math id="S4.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\psi}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\psi}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. By property (<a href="#S4.I3.i1" title="item a ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">a</span></a>) in Step 2 we can arrange <math id="S4.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the identity. For a careful choice of extension and lift, the family <math id="S4.p12.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> extends to a family of symplectomorphisms between open neighbourhoods of <math id="S4.p12.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p12.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Step 4:</em><span id="S4.p13.1.2" class="ltx_text"> Using assumption (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i3" title="item 2iii ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2iii</span></a>), we extend the family of homeomorphisms <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> form Step 3 to the desired family of symplectomorphisms of <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. First, extend <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to a family of symplectomorphisms between open neighbourhoods of <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is not hard to further extend to a family of symplectic embeddings</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}:\operatorname{nb}(M\setminus A^{0})\rightarrow M" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>nb</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}:\operatorname{nb}(M\setminus A^{0})\rightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">defined on a neighbourhood of the complement of <math id="S4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and starting at the inclusion <math id="S4.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The subtle part is to extend <math id="S4.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> over the interior of <math id="S4.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. This makes use of assumption (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i3" title="item 2iii ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2iii</span></a>). Let <math id="S4.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the family of symplectic vector fields generating the family of symplectic embeddings <math id="S4.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}:\operatorname{nb}(M\setminus A^{0})\hookrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>nb</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}:\operatorname{nb}(M\setminus A^{0})\hookrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math>. Here the vector field <math id="S4.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on a neighbourhood of <math id="S4.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\setminus A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\setminus A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to extend <math id="S4.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to a family of symplectic vector fields defined on all of <math id="S4.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. The desired extension of <math id="S4.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to all of <math id="S4.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> will simply be the flow generated by <math id="S4.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> be the unique family of closed <math id="S4.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-forms characterized by the identity <math id="S4.p13.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\Omega=\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\Omega=\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. The closed <math id="S4.p13.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S4.p13.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on <math id="S4.p13.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{nb}(M\setminus A^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>nb</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{nb}(M\setminus A^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> and we need to extend to a closed <math id="S4.p13.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-form on all of <math id="S4.p13.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us first choose an arbitrary extension of <math id="S4.p13.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to a family of not necessarily closed <math id="S4.p13.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-forms <math id="S4.p13.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="S4.p13.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. The differential <math id="S4.p13.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed <math id="S4.p13.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-form with compact support contained in the interior <math id="S4.p13.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(A^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(A^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>. We need to show that there exists a <math id="S4.p13.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S4.p13.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> compactly supported in <math id="S4.p13.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(A^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(A^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p13.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\beta^{\lambda}=d\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\beta^{\lambda}=d\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Once we have such <math id="S4.p13.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can take <math id="S4.p13.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}\coloneqq\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}-\beta^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}\coloneqq\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}-\beta^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the desired closed extension of <math id="S4.p13.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Showing the existence of <math id="S4.p13.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to showing that the cohomology class <math id="S4.p13.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}]</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes in <math id="S4.p13.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}_{c}(\operatorname{int}(A^{\lambda});\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}_{c}(\operatorname{int}(A^{\lambda});\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>. This in turn is equivalent to showing that the homomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,d\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}\rangle:H_{2}(A^{\lambda},\partial A^{\lambda})\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,d\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}\rangle:H_{2}(A^{\lambda},\partial A^{\lambda})\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">vanishes. The homomorphism <math id="S4.p13.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,d\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,d\alpha_{0}^{\lambda}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the composition of the boundary homomorphism <math id="S4.p13.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial:H_{2}(A^{\lambda},\partial A^{\lambda})\rightarrow H_{1}(\partial A^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial:H_{2}(A^{\lambda},\partial A^{\lambda})\rightarrow H_{1}(\partial A^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> with the homomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\alpha^{\lambda}\rangle:H_{1}(\partial A^{\lambda})\rightarrow\mathbb{R}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\alpha^{\lambda}\rangle:H_{1}(\partial A^{\lambda})\rightarrow\mathbb{R}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.4.1" class="ltx_text">Thus we need to verify that <math id="S4.p13.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\alpha^{\lambda}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\alpha^{\lambda}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on the image of <math id="S4.p13.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p13.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be any loop in <math id="S4.p13.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. After homotoping and possibly inverting the loop <math id="S4.p13.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, we can assume that it is given by a finite concatenation of path segments of one of the following two types:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(A)</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Path segments contained in <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the fiber over <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> of the projection <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{+}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{+}A^{\lambda}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(B)</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Flow line segments of <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{t}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{t}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> starting at a point contained in the interior of <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> and ending at the next intersection point with <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.p13.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.5.1" class="ltx_text">Our goal is to show that the integral of <math id="S4.p13.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> over paths of type (<a href="#S4.I5.i1" title="item A ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) or (<a href="#S4.I5.i2" title="item B ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>) vanishes. It follows from this assertion that <math id="S4.p13.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\alpha^{\lambda}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\alpha^{\lambda}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on all loops <math id="S4.p13.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p13.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us begin with paths of type (<a href="#S4.I5.i1" title="item A ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>). It follows from property (<a href="#S4.I3.i2" title="item b ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">b</span></a>) that the restriction of the vector field <math id="S4.p13.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p13.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is parallel to <math id="S4.p13.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the pull-back of <math id="S4.p13.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p13.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes. In particular, the integral of <math id="S4.p13.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> over type (<a href="#S4.I5.i1" title="item A ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) paths is zero. Let us now turn to path segments of type (<a href="#S4.I5.i2" title="item B ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). We define</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\lambda}:[0,4]\times\Sigma\rightarrow T^{\lambda}\qquad u^{\lambda}(t,p)\coloneqq\varphi^{\lambda}_{t}(\iota^{\lambda}(p))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\lambda}:[0,4]\times\Sigma\rightarrow T^{\lambda}\qquad u^{\lambda}(t,p)\coloneqq\varphi^{\lambda}_{t}(\iota^{\lambda}(p)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.6.1" class="ltx_text">Clearly, <math id="S4.p13.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{p}^{\lambda}\coloneqq u^{\lambda}(\cdot,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{p}^{\lambda}\coloneqq u^{\lambda}(\cdot,p)</annotation></semantics></math> is a path of type (<a href="#S4.I5.i2" title="item B ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>) for every <math id="S4.p13.m62" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.p13.m63" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed in a neighbourhood of <math id="S4.p13.m64" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and the restriction of <math id="S4.p13.m65" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p13.m66" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\lambda}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, the integral <math id="S4.p13.m67" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{[0,4]}(u_{p}^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{[0,4]}(u_{p}^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S4.p13.m68" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that, for all <math id="S4.p13.m69" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{area}(\Sigma)\int_{[0,4]}(u_{p}^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}=\int_{[0,4]\times\Sigma}(u^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}\wedge\omega." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{area}(\Sigma)\int_{[0,4]}(u_{p}^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}=\int_{[0,4]\times\Sigma}(u^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}\wedge\omega.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.7.1" class="ltx_text">Using that <math id="S4.p13.m70" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u^{\lambda})^{*}\Omega=\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u^{\lambda})^{*}\Omega=\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(u^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}\wedge\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(u^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}\wedge\omega</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(u^{\lambda})^{*}(\alpha^{\lambda}\wedge\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(u^{\lambda})^{*}(\alpha^{\lambda}\wedge\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(u^{\lambda})^{*}(\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\Omega\wedge\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(u^{\lambda})^{*}(\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\Omega\wedge\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(u^{\lambda})^{*}\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\Omega\wedge\Omega\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(u^{\lambda})^{*}\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\Omega\wedge\Omega\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.8.1" class="ltx_text">We compute</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="6" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{d\lambda}\operatorname{vol}(A^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{d\lambda}\operatorname{vol}(A^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\partial A^{\lambda}}\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\Omega\wedge\Omega\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\partial A^{\lambda}}\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\Omega\wedge\Omega\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{T^{\lambda}}\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\Omega\wedge\Omega\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{T^{\lambda}}\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\Omega\wedge\Omega\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{[0,4]\times\Sigma}(u^{\lambda})^{*}\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\Omega\wedge\Omega\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{[0,4]\times\Sigma}(u^{\lambda})^{*}\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\Omega\wedge\Omega\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{[0,4]\times\Sigma}(u^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}\wedge\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{[0,4]\times\Sigma}(u^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}\wedge\omega</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{area}(\Sigma)\int_{[0,4]}(u_{p}^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{area}(\Sigma)\int_{[0,4]}(u_{p}^{\lambda})^{*}\alpha^{\lambda}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.9.1" class="ltx_text">Here the first equality follows from assumption (<a href="#S4.I1.i2.I1.i3" title="item 2iii ‣ item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2iii</span></a>). The second equality is a consequence of Stokes’ theorem. The vector field <math id="S4.p13.m71" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to <math id="S4.p13.m72" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the integral of <math id="S4.p13.m73" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\Omega\wedge\Omega\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{X^{\lambda}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\Omega\wedge\Omega\right)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.p13.m74" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{S}A^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, which implies the third equality. The forth equality follows because <math id="S4.p13.m75" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a parametrization of <math id="S4.p13.m76" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. The fifth equality is immediate from equation (<a href="#S4.Ex18" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>). Finally, the sixth equality follows from identity (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>). Since equation (<a href="#S4.Ex23" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>) holds for all <math id="S4.p13.m77" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that the integral of <math id="S4.p13.m78" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> over all paths of type <span id="S4.p13.9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(<a href="#S4.I5.i2" title="item B ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>)</span> vanishes. This concludes Step 4 and therefore the proof of the lemma.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">It will be useful to introduce the following notation. For every <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonian <math id="S4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)\coloneqq\int\limits_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}Gdt\wedge\omega\quad\text{and}\quad A_{\pm}(G)\coloneqq\int_{0}^{1}G(t,\partial_{\pm}\mathbb{A})dt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)\coloneqq\int\limits_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}}Gdt\wedge\omega\quad\text{and}\quad A_{\pm}(G)\coloneqq\int_{0}^{1}G(t,\partial_{\pm}\mathbb{A})dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{+}\mathbb{A}=\left\{1\right\}\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{+}\mathbb{A}=\left\{1\right\}\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{-}\mathbb{A}=\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>−</mo></msub><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{-}\mathbb{A}=\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p15.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Step 1:</em><span id="S4.p15.1.2" class="ltx_text"> Let us begin with the case that <math id="S4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is constant and that the Hamiltonian <math id="S4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)=A_{\pm}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)=A_{\pm}(H)</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix an integer <math id="S4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the conclusion of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> holds. Pick a positive rational number <math id="S4.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> not contained in <math id="S4.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\alpha&lt;\frac{1}{2}\min h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\alpha&lt;\frac{1}{2}\min h</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that if <math id="S4.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S4.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S4.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, then the Hamiltonian <math id="S4.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\coloneqq H-V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\coloneqq H-V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the hypotheses of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 3.2. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Therefore, there exists a family of Hamiltonians <math id="S4.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> starting at <math id="S4.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}=G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}=G</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfying all the assertions of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 3.2. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Set <math id="S4.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\lambda}\coloneqq G^{\lambda}+V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\lambda}\coloneqq G^{\lambda}+V(H)</annotation></semantics></math>. The constant family of Hamiltonians equal to zero and the family <math id="S4.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{\lambda})_{\lambda\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the hypotheses of Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>. We conclude that <math id="S4.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)=D(H^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)=D(H^{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to <math id="S4.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus it suffices to construct a stratification of the latter subgraph.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">It follows from Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 3.2. ‣ 3. Quantitative factorization into rotations ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> that the Hamiltonian <math id="S4.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}=V(H)+\#_{i=1}^{\ell}\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>#</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}=V(H)+\#_{i=1}^{\ell}\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">for signs <math id="S4.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}\in\left\{\pm 1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}\in\left\{\pm 1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> and Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms <math id="S4.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}\in\operatorname{Ham}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}\in\operatorname{Ham}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us define</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}\coloneqq\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha}+\operatorname{min}(\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}\coloneqq\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha}+\operatorname{min}(\varepsilon_{i}\psi_{i}^{*}H_{\alpha}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p16.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.3.1" class="ltx_text">Note that each <math id="S4.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-negative and vanishes exactly on one of the boundary components of <math id="S4.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is straightforward to see that <math id="S4.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to <math id="S4.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>. We have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}=V(H)-\ell\alpha/2+\#_{i=1}^{\ell}H_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>#</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}=V(H)-\ell\alpha/2+\#_{i=1}^{\ell}H_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p16.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.4.1" class="ltx_text">We inductively define</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}\coloneqq V(H)-\ell\alpha/2\quad\text{and}\quad F_{i}\coloneqq F_{i-1}\#H_{i}\kern 5.0pt\text{for $1\leq i\leq\ell$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}\coloneqq V(H)-\ell\alpha/2\quad\text{and}\quad F_{i}\coloneqq F_{i-1}\#H_{i}\kern 5.0pt\text{for $1\leq i\leq\ell$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p16.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.5.1" class="ltx_text">This induces a stratification of <math id="S4.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H^{1})=D(F_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H^{1})=D(F_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math> top-dimensional strata</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(F_{0})\quad\text{and}\quad D(F_{i-1},F_{i})\kern 5.0pt\text{for $1\leq i\leq\ell$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(F_{0})\quad\text{and}\quad D(F_{i-1},F_{i})\kern 5.0pt\text{for $1\leq i\leq\ell$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p16.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.6.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, the stratum <math id="S4.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(F_{i-1},F_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(F_{i-1},F_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to <math id="S4.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, which in turn is symplectomorphic to <math id="S4.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus all of these strata are of the desired form. Since <math id="S4.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is constant, the stratum <math id="S4.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(F_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(F_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is of the desired form as well. Note that the rational number <math id="S4.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=p/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=p/q</annotation></semantics></math> was chosen independently of <math id="S4.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the uniform upper bound on the denominator <math id="S4.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is automatic. Moreover, the parameter <math id="S4.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> in the statement of the theorem is just equal to <math id="S4.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> for all strata. Finally, the constant <math id="S4.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of the stratum <math id="S4.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(F_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(F_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> clearly admits a positive lower bound independent of <math id="S4.p16.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. This concludes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> in the case <math id="S4.p16.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)=A_{\pm}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)=A_{\pm}(H)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p17.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Step 2:</em><span id="S4.p17.1.2" class="ltx_text"> Next, let us assume that</span></p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)=\frac{1}{2}(A_{-}(H)+A_{+}(H))\qquad\text{and}\qquad A_{+}(H)-A_{-}(H)\in\mathbb{Q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)=\frac{1}{2}(A_{-}(H)+A_{+}(H))\qquad\text{and}\qquad A_{+}(H)-A_{-}(H)\in\mathbb{Q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S4.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{+}(H)=A_{-}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{+}(H)=A_{-}(H)</annotation></semantics></math>, this is precisely the case treated in Step 1, so we assume that <math id="S4.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{+}(H)\neq A_{-}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{+}(H)\neq A_{-}(H)</annotation></semantics></math>. We may in addition assume that <math id="S4.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\coloneqq A_{+}(H)-A_{-}(H)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\coloneqq A_{+}(H)-A_{-}(H)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We stratify <math id="S4.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math> into the following two pieces:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H_{\alpha})\qquad\text{and}\qquad D(H_{\alpha},H)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H_{\alpha})\qquad\text{and}\qquad D(H_{\alpha},H).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p17.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p17.3.1" class="ltx_text">The first of the two pieces is already of the desired form. By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, the second piece is symplectomorphic to <math id="S4.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\overline{H}_{\alpha}\#H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\overline{H}_{\alpha}\#H)</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from assumption (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>) and the definition of <math id="S4.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> that the Hamiltonian <math id="S4.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{\alpha}\#H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{\alpha}\#H</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely of the form considered in Step 1. Thus we may further stratify <math id="S4.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\overline{H}_{\alpha}\#H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\overline{H}_{\alpha}\#H)</annotation></semantics></math> as in Step 1. Note that while the parameters of the substrata of <math id="S4.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\overline{H}_{\alpha}\#H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\overline{H}_{\alpha}\#H)</annotation></semantics></math> admit uniform bounds independent of <math id="S4.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, this is not true for the stratum <math id="S4.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S4.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=A_{+}(H)-A_{-}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=A_{+}(H)-A_{-}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by <math id="S4.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and may have arbitrarily large denominator. This will be rectified in the next step of the proof.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p18.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Step 3:</em><span id="S4.p18.1.2" class="ltx_text"> Let us now turn to the general case. Consider numbers <math id="S4.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=x_{0}&lt;x_{1}&lt;x_{2}&lt;x_{3}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=x_{0}&lt;x_{1}&lt;x_{2}&lt;x_{3}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and decompose the annulus <math id="S4.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}=[0,1]\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}=[0,1]\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> into three smaller annuli <math id="S4.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{i}\coloneqq[x_{i-1},x_{i}]\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{i}\coloneqq[x_{i-1},x_{i}]\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. The area of <math id="S4.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S4.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\coloneqq x_{i}-x_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\coloneqq x_{i}-x_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Or goal is to adjust the numbers <math id="S4.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and to construct a Hamiltonian <math id="S4.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(t,x,y)=G(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(t,x,y)=G(x)</annotation></semantics></math> only depending on <math id="S4.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following properties hold. Let <math id="S4.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}\coloneqq G|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msub><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}\coloneqq G|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the restriction.</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S4.I6.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h-\frac{1}{2}\min h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h-\frac{1}{2}\min h</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{-}(H)-A_{-}(G)=A_{+}(H)-A_{+}(G)=V(H)-V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{-}(H)-A_{-}(G)=A_{+}(H)-A_{+}(G)=V(H)-V(G)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G_{i})=\frac{1}{2}a_{i}(A_{-}(G_{i})+A_{+}(G_{i}))\qquad\text{and}\qquad\alpha_{i}\coloneqq\frac{A_{+}(G_{i})-A_{-}(G_{i})}{a_{i}}\in\mathbb{Q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G_{i})=\frac{1}{2}a_{i}(A_{-}(G_{i})+A_{+}(G_{i}))\qquad\text{and}\qquad\alpha_{i}\coloneqq\frac{A_{+}(G_{i})-A_{-}(G_{i})}{a_{i}}\in\mathbb{Q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.p18.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p18.2.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, we would like to have an upper bound on the minimal denominators of the rational numbers <math id="S4.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a lower bound on the areas <math id="S4.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, both of which are uniform in <math id="S4.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Before turning to the construction of such a Hamiltonian <math id="S4.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, let us first describe how we use it to stratify <math id="S4.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math>. First, we stratify <math id="S4.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math> into the following four pieces:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G,H)\qquad\text{and}\qquad D(G_{i})\kern 5.0pt\text{for $1\leq i\leq 3$}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G,H)\qquad\text{and}\qquad D(G_{i})\kern 5.0pt\text{for $1\leq i\leq 3$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p19.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, the first piece is symplectomorphic to <math id="S4.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\overline{G}\#H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\overline{G}\#H)</annotation></semantics></math>. The Hamiltonian <math id="S4.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G}\#H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G}\#H</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the assumption in Step 1 and we can therefore further stratify <math id="S4.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G,H)</annotation></semantics></math> into pieces of the desired form. Each of the pieces <math id="S4.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is, up to scaling of the symplectic form, of the form considered in Step 2 and can thus be further stratified as well. The result is a stratification of <math id="S4.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math> into pieces of the desired form. The parameters of the resulting strata admit uniform bounds in <math id="S4.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. For the substrata of <math id="S4.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G,H)</annotation></semantics></math> this follows from Step 1. For the substrata of <math id="S4.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> this follows from the uniform upper bound on the minimal denominators of <math id="S4.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the uniform lower bound on <math id="S4.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to construct <math id="S4.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that the Hamiltonian <math id="S4.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S4.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(t,x,y)=a+bx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(t,x,y)=a+bx</annotation></semantics></math>. Let is pick a rational number <math id="S4.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> slightly bigger than <math id="S4.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and a rational number <math id="S4.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> slightly smaller than <math id="S4.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that if <math id="S4.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S4.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S4.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\coloneqq A_{+}(H)-A_{-}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\coloneqq A_{+}(H)-A_{-}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> will be contained in the interval <math id="S4.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{-},\alpha_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{-},\alpha_{+})</annotation></semantics></math> and be uniformly bounded away from its entpoints. Let <math id="S4.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;w&lt;\frac{\alpha_{+}-\alpha}{\alpha_{+}-\alpha_{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;w&lt;\frac{\alpha_{+}-\alpha}{\alpha_{+}-\alpha_{-}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a parameter which is to be determined. Define</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\coloneqq w\quad\text{and}\quad x_{2}\coloneqq w+\frac{\alpha-\alpha_{-}}{\alpha_{+}-\alpha_{-}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\coloneqq w\quad\text{and}\quad x_{2}\coloneqq w+\frac{\alpha-\alpha_{-}}{\alpha_{+}-\alpha_{-}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p20.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p20.2.1" class="ltx_text">and let <math id="S4.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:[0,1]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:[0,1]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be the unique continuous piecewise linear function such that</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(0)=A_{-}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(0)=A_{-}(H)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(1)=A_{+}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(1)=A_{+}(H)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I7.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> has slope <math id="S4.I7.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> on the intervals <math id="S4.I7.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{0},x_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{0},x_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I7.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{2},x_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{2},x_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I7.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S4.I7.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I7.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I7.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> has slope <math id="S4.I7.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> on the interval <math id="S4.I7.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{1},x_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{1},x_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.p20.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p20.3.1" class="ltx_text">By taking <math id="S4.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently <math id="S4.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S4.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, we can make the difference between <math id="S4.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p20.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}(A_{-}(H)+A_{+}(H))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}(A_{-}(H)+A_{+}(H))</annotation></semantics></math> arbitrarily small. In particular, the integral <math id="S4.p20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{1}g(x)dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{1}g(x)dx</annotation></semantics></math> will be bigger than <math id="S4.p20.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p20.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S4.p20.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and smaller than <math id="S4.p20.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p20.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S4.p20.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\alpha_{+}-\alpha}{\alpha_{+}-\alpha_{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\alpha_{+}-\alpha}{\alpha_{+}-\alpha_{-}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus there exists a unique value <math id="S4.p20.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p20.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{1}g(x)dx=V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{1}g(x)dx=V(H)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S4.p20.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently <math id="S4.p20.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S4.p20.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, this value <math id="S4.p20.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly bounded away from the endpoints of the interval <math id="S4.p20.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\frac{\alpha_{+}-\alpha}{\alpha_{+}-\alpha_{-}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\frac{\alpha_{+}-\alpha}{\alpha_{+}-\alpha_{-}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us pick a smoothing <math id="S4.p20.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p20.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> which has the following properties:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I8" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I8.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with <math id="S4.I8.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> at the points <math id="S4.I8.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I8.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I8.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I8.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The integrals of <math id="S4.I8.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I8.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> agree on each of the intervals <math id="S4.I8.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{0},x_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{0},x_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I8.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{1},x_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{1},x_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I8.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{2},x_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{2},x_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I8.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I8.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I8.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I8.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.I8.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S4.I8.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we can control this <math id="S4.I8.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> distance purely in terms of the inital pick of the rational numbers <math id="S4.I8.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S4.I8.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and uniformly among all <math id="S4.I8.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently close to <math id="S4.I8.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.p20.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p20.4.1" class="ltx_text">Now define <math id="S4.p20.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(t,x,y)\coloneqq\tilde{g}(x)-\frac{1}{2}\min h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(t,x,y)\coloneqq\tilde{g}(x)-\frac{1}{2}\min h</annotation></semantics></math>. It is straightforward to check that this Hamiltonian has all desired properties.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Symplectic ribbon complexes</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the notion of a <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symplectic ribbon complex</span>. The main result of this section, Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, states that ellipsoid embedding stability holds for all connected symplectic ribbon complexes consisting of balls and polydisks. This result plays a crucial role in our proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm2" title="Theorem B (Ellipsoid embedding stability). ‣ 1.2. Ellipsoid embedding stability ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, as it allows us to deduce ellipsoid embedding stability of a symplectic <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold from the existence of certain decompositions into balls and polydisks; see Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional symplectic ellipsoid <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> of widths <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)\coloneqq\left\{z\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\kern 5.0pt\Big|\kern 5.0pt\frac{\pi|z_{1}|^{2}}{a}+\frac{\pi|z_{2}|^{2}}{b}\leq 1\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)\coloneqq\left\{z\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\kern 5.0pt\Big|\kern 5.0pt\frac{\pi|z_{1}|^{2}}{a}+\frac{\pi|z_{2}|^{2}}{b}\leq 1\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional ball of width <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is the ellipsoid <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a)\coloneqq E(a,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a)\coloneqq E(a,a)</annotation></semantics></math>. The polydisk of widths <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(a,b)\coloneqq\left\{z\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\mid\pi|z_{1}|^{2}\leq a\kern 5.0pt\text{and}\kern 5.0pt\pi|z_{2}|^{2}\leq b\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(a,b)\coloneqq\left\{z\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\mid\pi|z_{1}|^{2}\leq a\kern 5.0pt\text{and}\kern 5.0pt\pi|z_{2}|^{2}\leq b\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define the <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symplectic ribbon</span> <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(a)</annotation></semantics></math> of width <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> to be</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(a)\coloneqq[0,1]\times B^{2}(a)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(a)\coloneqq[0,1]\times B^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional disk of area <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. The area form on <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> induces a closed <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(a)</annotation></semantics></math> which is characterized by the requirements that the restriction of <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> to the fibers <math id="S5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{*\right\}\times B^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{*\right\}\times B^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the area form on <math id="S5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> and that the contraction <math id="S5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\partial_{t}}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\partial_{t}}\omega</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, where <math id="S5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the coordinate of the <math id="S5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> factor. The leaves of the characteristic foliation on <math id="S5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(a)</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S5.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> are therefore simply the fibers <math id="S5.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times\left\{*\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times\left\{*\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The ribbon <math id="S5.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(a)</annotation></semantics></math> has two ends <math id="S5.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{j\right\}\times B^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{j\right\}\times B^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\left\{0,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\left\{0,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We think of <math id="S5.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(a)</annotation></semantics></math> as not carrying a preferred orientation, i.e. we ignore the obvious orientation of the interval <math id="S5.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now consider a smooth <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional star-shaped domain <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and a symplectic ribbon <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be one of its ends. An <span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">attaching map</span> from this end to <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is simply a smooth embedding <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:B\rightarrow\partial X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:B\rightarrow\partial X</annotation></semantics></math> such that the pull-back of the symplectic form on <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the standard area form on <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. We would also like to allow certain non-smooth star-shaped domains <math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such as polydisks. In this case, we always assume that the image of an attaching map is disjoint from the non-smooth locus of the boundary of the domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a finite collection of star-shaped domains and ribbons of variable widths. For some ribbon ends, fix an attaching map to one of the star-shaped domains. We require that the images of these attaching maps are pairwise disjoint. Moreover, we require that, for each ribbon, we have an attaching map for at least one of its ends. Take the disjoint union of all the star-shaped domains and ribbons and then take the quotient by gluing ribbon ends to the star-shaped domains via the chosen attaching maps. We call the resulting object a <span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symplectic ribbon complex</span>. Note that not all ribbon ends are required to be attached to some star-shaped domain. We call such an end a <span id="S5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">free end</span>. The corresponding ribbon is called a <span id="S5.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">free ribbon</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We can alternatively think of a symplectic ribbon complex as the following datum:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a collection <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of finitely many star-shaped domains;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a collection <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> of finitely many disjoint symplectic disks in the boundaries of domains contained in <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a partition of <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> into individual disks and pairs of disks;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for each pair of disks, a diffeomorphism between them compatible with the restriction of the symplectic form to the disks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Here the individual disks in the partition correspond to ribbons with a free end. The area-preserving diffeomorphism between a pair of disks corresponds to the map obtained by traversing a ribbon following the characteristic foliation. A <span id="S5.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symplectomorphism</span> between two symplectic ribbon complexes is a symplectomorphism between the disjoint unions of the underlying shar-shaped domains which is compatible with the collections of disks, their partitions, and the transition maps between pairs of disks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider an ellipsoid <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. For every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> and for every angle <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a unique map</p>
<table id="S5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(\theta):B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\partial E(a,b)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(\theta):B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\partial E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying the following properties: For <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\left\{1,2\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\left\{1,2\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{j}:\mathbb{C}^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{j}:\mathbb{C}^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection onto the <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th factor. Then the composition <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{2}\circ\iota(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{2}\circ\iota(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is simply the inclusion of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(b)</annotation></semantics></math> and the image of the composition <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{1}\circ\iota(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{1}\circ\iota(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the ray <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\cdot e^{i\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\cdot e^{i\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. An easy computation shows that the pull-back of the standard symplectic form on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the standard area form on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math>. For each positive integer <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math> be the symplectic ribbon complex built from one copy of the ellipsoid <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> copies of the ribbon <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(c)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows: For each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq j&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq j&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, attach a copy of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(c)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> on one of its ends via the attaching map <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(2\pi j/n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(2\pi j/n)</annotation></semantics></math>. The complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> free ends. By construction, each of these free ends comes with a preferred identification with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math>. This preferred identification is not part of the datum of a symplectic ribbon complex, but we will occasionally make use of it below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If we replace the collection of attaching angles <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi j/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi j/n</annotation></semantics></math> by any other collection of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> distinct attaching angles <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}&lt;\dots&lt;\theta_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}&lt;\dots&lt;\theta_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the resulting symplectic ribbon complex is symplectomorphic to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, there exists a symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> which, for every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq j&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq j&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, maps the image of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(\theta_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(\theta_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> to the image of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(2\pi j/n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(2\pi j/n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Note, however, that this isomorphism is not compatible with the preferred identifications of the free ribbon ends with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> mentioned above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,a)=B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,a)=B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> is a ball, let us abbreviate the resulting complex by <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a ribbon graph <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. a graph such that the incident edges of each vertex are cyclically ordered. Assume that the vertices of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, except possibly for some of the leaves of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, are labelled by positive real numbers. Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> denote the labelling of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix real numbers <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>. Given these data, we can construct a symplectic ribbon complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(G,A,b,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(G,A,b,c)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows: For each labelled vertex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> denote its label, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> the number of edges incident to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and take a copy of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{v},b;c,n_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{v},b;c,n_{v})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the free ends of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{v},b;c,n_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{v},b;c,n_{v})</annotation></semantics></math> are naturally cyclically ordered. To each incident edge of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, assign a free end of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{v},b;c,n_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{v},b;c,n_{v})</annotation></semantics></math> respecting the cyclic order. Now glue together any pair of free ends assigned to the same edge via the identity map of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a ribbon complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(G,A,b,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(G,A,b,c)</annotation></semantics></math>. The free ends of this ribbon complex correspond precisely to the unlabelled vertices of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that in contrast to Example <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 5.1. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, the angles <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> at which ribbons are attached to the ellipsoid <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{v},b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{v},b)</annotation></semantics></math> can no longer be changed freely if the graph <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has cycles: Indeed, consider the simplest example of just one ribbon <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(c)</annotation></semantics></math> attached to an ellipsoid <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> at both of its ends via <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(\theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(\theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(\theta_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(\theta_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> for two angles <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}&lt;\theta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}&lt;\theta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The disk <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained from <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> by flowing forward via the Reeb flow on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> for some amount of time. This induces a transition map <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{0})\rightarrow\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{0})\rightarrow\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> which assigns to each point in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> the first intersection point of the trajectory starting at that point with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Traversing the ribbon following the characteristic foliation induces a map <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{1})\rightarrow\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{1})\rightarrow\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Composing these two maps yields an area preserving diffeomorphism of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}\iota(\theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> which is a rotation by angle <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a(\theta_{1}-\theta_{0})}{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>b</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a(\theta_{1}-\theta_{0})}{b}</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly, this number is invariant under symplectomorphisms of ribbon complexes. On the other hand, if <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree, then only the cyclic order of the ribbons attached to an ellipsoid matters and the angles can be varied freely.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional symplectic manifold. A <span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symplectic embedding</span> of a symplectic ribbon complex <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is an injective continuous map <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following properties:</p>
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The restriction <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> to each domain <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth symplectic embedding.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The restriction <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> to each ribbon <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth embedding which pulls back the symplectic form on <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> to the closed <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-from <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is attached to <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial X</annotation></semantics></math> at one of its ends. Then <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{X}(\partial X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{X}(\partial X)</annotation></semantics></math> transversely, i.e. <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\varphi_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\varphi_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is no-where tangent to <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{X}(\partial X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{X}(\partial X)</annotation></semantics></math> along the image of the gluing region of <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every symplectic ribbon complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> admits a symplectic embedding into some symplectic <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold. There is a neighbourhood theorem for symplectic embeddings of symplectic ribbon complexes: Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic ribbon complex and let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\prime}:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\prime}:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be two symplectic embeddings into symplectic <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the manifolds have bounary, we assume that the images of the embeddings are contained in the interior. Then there exist open neighbourhoods <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(K)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\prime}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\prime}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and a symplectomorphism <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:U\rightarrow U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:U\rightarrow U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\prime}=\psi\circ\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\prime}=\psi\circ\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. This can be proved using Gotay’s neighbourhood theorem for coisotropic submanifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Got82</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-empty symplectic ribbon complex. The <span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">volume</span> <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be the sum of the volumes of its domains. Let <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{i})_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{i})_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the collection of all the Gromov widths of the domains of <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b_{j})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b_{j})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the collection of all widths of ribbons of <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the <span id="S5.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">width</span> of <math id="S5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> to be the quantity</p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(K)\coloneqq\min\left\{\min_{i}a_{i},\min_{j}b_{j}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>i</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>j</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(K)\coloneqq\min\left\{\min_{i}a_{i},\min_{j}b_{j}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we define the <span id="S5.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">volume normalized width</span> by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{w}(K)\coloneqq\frac{w(K)}{\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(K)}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{w}(K)\coloneqq\frac{w(K)}{\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(K)}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.3" class="ltx_p">Note that this quantity is invariant under scalings of the symplectic form on <math id="S5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. For every <math id="S5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the ellipsoid embedding number <math id="S5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{E}_{a}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{E}_{a}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> to be</p>
<table id="S5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{E}_{a}(K)\coloneqq\sup_{\lambda}\frac{\operatorname{vol}(E(\lambda,\lambda a))}{\operatorname{vol}(K)}\in[0,1]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>λ</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{E}_{a}(K)\coloneqq\sup_{\lambda}\frac{\operatorname{vol}(E(\lambda,\lambda a))}{\operatorname{vol}(K)}\in[0,1],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.4" class="ltx_p">where the supremum is taken over all <math id="S5.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> into the interior of a symplectic <math id="S5.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S5.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and for every open neighbourhood <math id="S5.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(K)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the ellipsoid <math id="S5.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\lambda,\lambda a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\lambda,\lambda a)</annotation></semantics></math> symplectically embeds into <math id="S5.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-empty connected symplectic ribbon complex built using only balls and polydisks. Then ellipsoid embedding stability holds for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. there exists a finite number <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{E}_{a}(K)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{E}_{a}(K)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, for every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the number <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be chosen uniformly among all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{w}(K)\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{w}(K)\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this section is concerned with the proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{0}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{0}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{E}_{a}(E(1,e))=p^{E}_{a}(P(1,e))=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{E}_{a}(E(1,e))=p^{E}_{a}(P(1,e))=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq e\leq e_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq e\leq e_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">The statement about <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{E}_{a}(E(1,e))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{E}_{a}(E(1,e))</annotation></semantics></math> is an immediate consequence of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH13</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite>. The statement about <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{E}_{a}(P(1,e))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{E}_{a}(P(1,e))</annotation></semantics></math> can be proved via the same method. Indeed, a <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional ellipsoid <math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> symplectically embeds into a <math id="S5.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polydisk <math id="S5.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S5.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(E)\leq c_{k}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(E)\leq c_{k}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ECH capacities, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cri19</a>]</cite>. One can therefore prove the assertion by analysing the formulas for ECH capacities of ellipsoids and polydisks. Alternatively, the statement also follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Theorem 3]</cite> in combination with the fact that the problem of embedding an ellipsoid into a polydisk is equivalent to embedding a certain collection of balls associated to the weight sequence of the ellipsoid, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cri19</a>]</cite>. Note that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Theorem 3]</cite> applies to target manifolds which are pseudoballs, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Definition 1.3]</cite>. While polydisks are not pseudoballs, they are contained in the closure of the space of pseudoballs. The proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Theorem 3]</cite> also applies to polydisks without changes.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,A)</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected labelled ribbon graph and let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> be two positive numbers. Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=K(G,A,b,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=K(G,A,b,c)</annotation></semantics></math> be the ribbon complex constructed in Example <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Example 5.2. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>. Define <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\coloneqq\sum_{v}a_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\coloneqq\sum_{v}a_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> where the sum runs over all labelled vertices of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of free ends of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ribbon complex obtained from <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> by deleting all ribbons with a free end. Then for every symplectic embedding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> into a symplectic <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly with boundary, such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(K^{\prime})\subset\operatorname{int}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(K^{\prime})\subset\operatorname{int}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, every open neighbourhood <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(K^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(K^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\gamma&lt;\beta\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\gamma&lt;\beta\leq c</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:E(\alpha,\beta;\gamma,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:E(\alpha,\beta;\gamma,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following is true:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The image </span><math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(E(\alpha,\beta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(E(\alpha,\beta))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is contained in </span><math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each free ribbon </span><math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there exists exactly one free ribbon </span><math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\alpha,\beta;\gamma,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\alpha,\beta;\gamma,n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(S)\subset\varphi(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(S)\subset\varphi(R)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Moreover, the image of the free end of </span><math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> under </span><math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is contained in the image of the free end of </span><math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> under </span><math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">After possibly deleting some ribbons of <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we can assume that <math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree. Let us begin by constructing a special symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Remark <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Remark 5.3. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, it will be sufficient to prove the lemma for this specific embedding. Let us begin by embedding the ribbon tree <math id="S5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> into the complex plane <math id="S5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that all edges are straight line segments. For each labelled vertex <math id="S5.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, take a copy of <math id="S5.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(a_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(a_{v})</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="S5.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. After dilating the embedding of the tree <math id="S5.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> if necessary, we can assume that the disks centered at two distinct vertices are disjoint and that no unlabelled vertex is contained in any of the disks. Moreover, we can assume that each edge only meets the disks centered at the vertices it is incident to. It is straightforward to construct a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the image <math id="S5.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(K)</annotation></semantics></math> is the union of one copy of the ellipsoid <math id="S5.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{v},b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{v},b)</annotation></semantics></math> for each labelled vertex <math id="S5.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, translated such that it is centered at <math id="S5.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{v\right\}\times\left\{0\right\}\in\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{v\right\}\times\left\{0\right\}\in\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and all the sets of the form <math id="S5.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\times\left\{B^{2}(c)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\times\left\{B^{2}(c)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge. Note that the projection <math id="S5.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{1}(\varphi(K))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{1}(\varphi(K))</annotation></semantics></math> to the first factor of <math id="S5.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely given by the above configuration of disks and line segments. In order to simplify notation, let us identify <math id="S5.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> with its image under <math id="S5.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix an arbitrary open neighbourhood <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and numbers <math id="S5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\gamma&lt;\beta\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\gamma&lt;\beta\leq c</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset\mathbb{C}\cong\mathbb{C}\times\left\{0\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset\mathbb{C}\cong\mathbb{C}\times\left\{0\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a topological disk which contains <math id="S5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{1}(K^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{1}(K^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in its interior and which is contained in <math id="S5.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\cap(\mathbb{C}\times\left\{0\right\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\cap(\mathbb{C}\times\left\{0\right\})</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, Let <math id="S5.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ribbon complex obtained from <math id="S5.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing each domain <math id="S5.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{v},b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{v},b)</annotation></semantics></math> by the smaller domain <math id="S5.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{v},c+\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{v},c+\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> while leaving the widths of all ribbons unchanged. If <math id="S5.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small, it is possible to find a smooth non-negative function <math id="S5.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:D\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:D\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> with the following properties:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on the boundary <math id="S5.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has precisely one local maximum and no other critical points.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The set</p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A(D,f)\coloneqq\left\{(z_{1},z_{2})\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\mid z_{1}\in D,\kern 5.0pt\pi|z_{2}|^{2}\leq f(z_{1})\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(D,f)\coloneqq\left\{(z_{1},z_{2})\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\mid z_{1}\in D,\kern 5.0pt\pi|z_{2}|^{2}\leq f(z_{1})\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I4.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is contained in <math id="S5.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and contains <math id="S5.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> in its interior.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">Choose an area preserving diffeomorphism <math id="S5.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}\coloneqq\psi(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}\coloneqq\psi(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is a round disk cetered at the origin and such that <math id="S5.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}\coloneqq f\circ\psi^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}\coloneqq f\circ\psi^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is rotation invariant. Clearly, <math id="S5.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\times\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\times\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectomorphism mapping <math id="S5.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(D,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(D,f)</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S5.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\tilde{D},\tilde{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\tilde{D},\tilde{f})</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to show that <math id="S5.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\tilde{D},\tilde{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\tilde{D},\tilde{f})</annotation></semantics></math> contains the ellipsoid <math id="S5.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Once we know this, <math id="S5.p12.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi\times\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{C}})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi\times\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{C}})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a symplectic embedding of <math id="S5.p12.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p12.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider a general ellipsoid <math id="S5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{area}(\left\{z\in B^{2}(p)\mid\operatorname{area}(E(p,q)\cap\left\{z\right\}\times\mathbb{C})\geq s\right\})=\frac{p(q-s)}{q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{area}(\left\{z\in B^{2}(p)\mid\operatorname{area}(E(p,q)\cap\left\{z\right\}\times\mathbb{C})\geq s\right\})=\frac{p(q-s)}{q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">It follows from this identity, the fact that <math id="S5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="S5.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(D,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(D,f)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the fact that <math id="S5.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is area preserving that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{area}\left(\left\{z\in\tilde{D}\mid\tilde{f}(z)\geq s\right\}\right)=\operatorname{area}(\left\{z\in D\mid f(z)\geq s\right\})\geq\sum_{v}\frac{a_{v}(c+\varepsilon-s)}{c+\varepsilon}=\frac{\alpha(c+\varepsilon-s)}{c+\varepsilon}\geq\frac{a(\beta-s)}{\beta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>v</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>β</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{area}\left(\left\{z\in\tilde{D}\mid\tilde{f}(z)\geq s\right\}\right)=\operatorname{area}(\left\{z\in D\mid f(z)\geq s\right\})\geq\sum_{v}\frac{a_{v}(c+\varepsilon-s)}{c+\varepsilon}=\frac{\alpha(c+\varepsilon-s)}{c+\varepsilon}\geq\frac{a(\beta-s)}{\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">Again using identity (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) and the fact that <math id="S5.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is rotation invariant and non-increasing along the radial direction, we see that <math id="S5.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="S5.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\tilde{D},\tilde{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\tilde{D},\tilde{f})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we observe that it is straightforward to attach <math id="S5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> copies of the ribbon <math id="S5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi\times\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{C}})^{-1}(E(\alpha,\beta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi\times\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{C}})^{-1}(E(\alpha,\beta))</annotation></semantics></math> in such a way that property (<a href="#S5.I3.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Lemma 5.6. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) in the statement of the lemma holds. The existence of the desired embedding of <math id="S5.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\alpha,\beta;\gamma,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\alpha,\beta;\gamma,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is an immediate consequence.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the ball <math id="S5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a)\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a)\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\partial B^{4}(a)\rightarrow\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\partial B^{4}(a)\rightarrow\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hopf map. Here <math id="S5.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S5.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere of area <math id="S5.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. We call a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">free</span> if the composition <math id="S5.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\circ\psi:B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\circ\psi:B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedding. We call a finite collection <math id="S5.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{j})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{j})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of symplectic embeddings <math id="S5.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.p15.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">free</span> if the compositions <math id="S5.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\circ\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\circ\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise disjoint embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.7</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the symplectomorphism group of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on free <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{j})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{j})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of symplectic embeddings <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider two free <math id="S5.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples of symplectic embeddings <math id="S5.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{j})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{j})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{j}^{\prime})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{j}^{\prime})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. First note that the symplectomorphism group of <math id="S5.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on <math id="S5.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples of pairwise disjoint symplectic embeddings of <math id="S5.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math>. Any symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> maps Hopf fibers on the boundary <math id="S5.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> to Hopf fibers and therefore induces a symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, any symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we can find a symplectomorphism <math id="S5.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\circ\psi_{j}^{\prime}=\rho\circ\chi^{\prime}\circ\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\circ\psi_{j}^{\prime}=\rho\circ\chi^{\prime}\circ\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider two symplectic embeddings <math id="S5.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi,\psi^{\prime}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi,\psi^{\prime}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> which are both free. If <math id="S5.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\circ\psi=\rho\circ\psi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\circ\psi=\rho\circ\psi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are related by a symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> whose support can be taken to be contained in an arbitrarily small neighbourhood of the preimage <math id="S5.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{-1}(\operatorname{im}(\rho\circ\psi))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{-1}(\operatorname{im}(\rho\circ\psi))</annotation></semantics></math>. Using this observation, we can correct <math id="S5.p16.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to a symplectomorphism <math id="S5.p16.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p16.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfies <math id="S5.p16.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}^{\prime}=\chi\circ\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}^{\prime}=\chi\circ\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p16.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.8</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a ball and let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-negative integer. Then the following is true for every sufficiently small <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>: Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(E(a,b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(E(a,b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(B)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq j&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq j&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic embedding and assume that the collection <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{j})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{j})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is free. Then there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:E(a,b;c,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:E(a,b;c,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B</annotation></semantics></math> whose restriction to the <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th free ribbon end of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the embedding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we use the preferred identification of the free ribbon ends with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math>, see Example <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 5.1. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Moreover, the image <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}(\varphi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}(\varphi)</annotation></semantics></math> intersects <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B</annotation></semantics></math> precisely at the free ends and the intersection is transverse.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Before turning to the proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>, we need to establish some preliminary results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.9</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose that the ellipsoid <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=E(a,b)\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=E(a,b)\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> symplectically embeds into <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathbb{C}P^{2}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathbb{C}P^{2}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset M</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex line. If <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:E\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:E\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{-1}(C)=E\cap(\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{-1}(C)=E\cap(\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is a special case of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Lemma 1.6]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.10</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the ellipsoid <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:E(a,b;c,1)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}E(ra,rb)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:E(a,b;c,1)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}E(ra,rb)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the restriction of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> maps the free end of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,1)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and such that the image of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> does not intersect the set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> in any other point.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the <math id="S5.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional half space <math id="S5.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\coloneqq\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\times\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\coloneqq\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\times\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. One can find an explicit Hamiltonian diffeomorphism <math id="S5.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> compactly supported in the interior of <math id="S5.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(ra,rb)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(ra,rb)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the image of <math id="S5.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is disjoint from <math id="S5.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>, contained in <math id="S5.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, and of the form</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C})=\left\{(f(z),z)\mid z\in B^{2}(rb)\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C})=\left\{(f(z),z)\mid z\in B^{2}(rb)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p19.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text">for some compactly supported non-negative function <math id="S5.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:B^{2}(rb)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:B^{2}(rb)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can connect the boundary of <math id="S5.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> to the image <math id="S5.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> via a ribbon <math id="S5.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(c)</annotation></semantics></math> contained in the half space <math id="S5.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, apply <math id="S5.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the desired embedding of the ribbon complex <math id="S5.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> in the case <math id="S5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</h6>
<div id="S5.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">After scaling, we may assume that <math id="S5.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=B^{4}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=B^{4}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> has symplectic width equal to <math id="S5.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">The case <math id="S5.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> follows immediately from the well-known statement that a sufficiently skinny ellipsoid symplectically embeds into a ball if and only if the volume constraint is satisfied, see Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5.5. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">The case <math id="S5.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> follows from Lemmas <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 5.9. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 5.10. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>. Indeed, let <math id="S5.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be small and consider <math id="S5.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(E(a,b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(E(a,b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(B)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small, then <math id="S5.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> symplectically embeds into <math id="S5.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{2}=\mathbb{C}P^{2}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{2}=\mathbb{C}P^{2}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> by the case <math id="S5.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We can pick <math id="S5.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently close to <math id="S5.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p22.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(ra,rb)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(ra,rb)</annotation></semantics></math> still embeds into <math id="S5.p22.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a complex line <math id="S5.p22.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{C}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{C}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining Lemmas <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 5.9. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 5.10. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>, we see that there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p22.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,1)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{C}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,1)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{C}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which maps the free ribbon end into <math id="S5.p22.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and is otherwise disjoint from <math id="S5.p22.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a map <math id="S5.p22.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\rightarrow\mathbb{C}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\rightarrow\mathbb{C}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which maps the interior of <math id="S5.p22.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> symplectomorphically onto the complement of <math id="S5.p22.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and whose restriction to the boundary of <math id="S5.p22.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is simply the Hopf map <math id="S5.p22.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\partial B\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\partial B\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math>. Via this map, we can pull back the symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p22.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,1)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{C}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,1)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{C}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p22.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:E(a,b;c,1)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:E(a,b;c,1)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly, <math id="S5.p22.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> embeds the free ribbon end of <math id="S5.p22.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,1)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p22.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B</annotation></semantics></math> and the composition with the Hopf map <math id="S5.p22.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is still an embedding. Since any other free embedding of <math id="S5.p22.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p22.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B</annotation></semantics></math> is related to this embedding via a symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.p22.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 5.7. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>, this concludes the proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> in the case <math id="S5.p22.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.11</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a ball and let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-negative integer. Then for all sufficiently small <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> the following is true: Consider <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(E(a,b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(E(a,b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(B)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial B</annotation></semantics></math> be a free embedding. Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},\dots,D_{n-1}\subset\partial B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},\dots,D_{n-1}\subset\partial B</annotation></semantics></math> be embedded disks such that, for each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction of the Hopf map <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedding with the same image as <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\circ\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\circ\psi</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that the disks <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}(\psi),D_{1},\dots,D_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}(\psi),D_{1},\dots,D_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise disjoint. Then there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:E(a,b;c,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:E(a,b;c,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B</annotation></semantics></math> such that the restriction of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> to one free ribbon end of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of a free ribbon end of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the special case <math id="S5.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> we just proved, we can find a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:E(a,b;c,1)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:E(a,b;c,1)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B</annotation></semantics></math> such that the restriction of <math id="S5.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> to the free ribbon end of <math id="S5.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S5.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=R(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=R(c)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ribbon of <math id="S5.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the thickened ribbon</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\varepsilon}\coloneqq[0,\varepsilon]\times R=[0,\varepsilon]\times[0,1]\times B^{2}(c)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\varepsilon}\coloneqq[0,\varepsilon]\times R=[0,\varepsilon]\times[0,1]\times B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p23.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p23.2.1" class="ltx_text">and equip it with the symplectic form <math id="S5.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ds\wedge dt+\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ds\wedge dt+\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> are the coordinates on <math id="S5.p23.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\varepsilon]\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\varepsilon]\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p23.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard area form on <math id="S5.p23.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p23.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small, we can find a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p23.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:R_{\varepsilon}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:R_{\varepsilon}\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B</annotation></semantics></math> such that the restriction of <math id="S5.p23.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p23.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{0\right\}\times R\cong R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{0\right\}\times R\cong R</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the restriction of <math id="S5.p23.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p23.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p23.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> maps the two boundary strata <math id="S5.p23.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\varepsilon]\times\left\{j\right\}\times B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\varepsilon]\times\left\{j\right\}\times B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p23.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\left\{0,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\left\{0,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p23.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\varphi(E(a,b))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\varphi(E(a,b))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p23.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. By the assumption that <math id="S5.p23.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(D_{j})=\operatorname{im}(\rho\circ\psi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(D_{j})=\operatorname{im}(\rho\circ\psi)</annotation></semantics></math>, up to applying a symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.p23.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and relabelling the disks <math id="S5.p23.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can assume that <math id="S5.p23.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{j}=\alpha(\left\{j/(n-1)\right\}\times\left\{1\right\}\times B^{2}(c))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{j}=\alpha(\left\{j/(n-1)\right\}\times\left\{1\right\}\times B^{2}(c))</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p23.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S5.p23.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, let us attach the ribbon <math id="S5.p23.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(\left\{j/(n-1)\right\}\times R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(\left\{j/(n-1)\right\}\times R)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p23.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(E(a,b))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(E(a,b))</annotation></semantics></math>. As observed in Example <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 5.1. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, the resulting ribbon complex is symplectomorphic to <math id="S5.p23.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is the image of a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p23.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}B</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying all the desired properties at the free ribbon ends.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the triangle <math id="S5.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with vertices <math id="S5.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and the square <math id="S5.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with vertices <math id="S5.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well-known that the interior of the Lagrangian product <math id="S5.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to the interior of the ball <math id="S5.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tra95</a>, Proposition 5.2]</cite>). Even though <math id="S5.p24.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> is not smooth everywhere, one can still make sense of a generalized Reeb flow on its boundary. The dynamics of this generalized Reeb flow is equivalent to the Minkowski billard dynamics of <math id="S5.p24.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p24.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">GT02</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">AO14</a>, §2.4]</cite>). In the following, we give a detailed description of the generalized Reeb flow. For this purpose, let us abbreviate the edges of <math id="S5.p24.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p24.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p24.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p24.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, as indicated in Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 5.1 ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Moreover, let <math id="S5.p24.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p24.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p24.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p24.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the edges of <math id="S5.p24.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S5.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:1.0pt;height:0.41304348pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="1.33" height="0.55" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,0.55) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,113.57) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="274.97" height="113.57" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15390v1/x1.svg" id="S5.F1.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="286" height="118" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.35,0.03)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="15.28" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="t_{b}" text="t _ b" xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m1">
                      <XMath xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m1.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m1.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">t</XMTok>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">b</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.43,0.26)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="16.21" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="t_{d}" text="t _ d" xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m2">
                      <XMath xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m2.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m2.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">t</XMTok>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">d</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.1,0.26)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="14.08" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="t_{l}" text="t _ l" xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m3">
                      <XMath xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m3.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m3.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">t</XMTok>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">l</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(1.25,0.27)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="17.40" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="q_{r}" text="q _ r" xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m4">
                      <XMath xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m4.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m4.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">q</XMTok>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">r</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(1.01,0.03)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="16.87" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="q_{b}" text="q _ b" xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m5">
                      <XMath xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m5.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m5.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">q</XMTok>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">b</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.75,0.27)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="15.67" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="q_{l}" text="q _ l" xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m6">
                      <XMath xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m6.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S5.F1.pic1.m6.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
