<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5.1. </span>The triangle <math id="S5.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and the square <math id="S5.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p25.1" class="ltx_p">The boundary of <math id="S5.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> has seven <math id="S5.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional faces, three of the form <math id="S5.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{*}\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{*}\times Q</annotation></semantics></math> and four of the form <math id="S5.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times q_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times q_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. On each <math id="S5.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional face, the characteristic foliation is very simple: It is parallel to <math id="S5.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is the unit outer normal vector of the face. There are twelve <math id="S5.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional faces <math id="S5.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{*}\times q_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{*}\times q_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Four of these <math id="S5.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional faces, <math id="S5.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{l}\times q_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{l}\times q_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{l}\times q_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{l}\times q_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p25.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b}\times q_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b}\times q_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p25.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b}\times q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b}\times q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, are Lagrangian, i.e. the restriction of the symplectic form vanishes on them. The remaining eight <math id="S5.p25.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional faces are symplectic. At each of the symplectic <math id="S5.p25.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional faces, the Reeb flow simply transitions between the two <math id="S5.p25.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional faces meeting there. At the Lagrangian <math id="S5.p25.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional faces, the generalized Reeb flow is discontinuous. The Reeb flow admits a very simple description, see Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 5.2 ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
