                        </XMath>
                      </Math></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5.6. </span>The labelled ribbon graph <math id="S5.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p38.1.1" class="ltx_text">By construction, <math id="S5.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is embedded in <math id="S5.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> and its two free ends are mapped into the diagonal part <math id="S5.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{d}\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{d}\times Q</annotation></semantics></math> of the boundary of <math id="S5.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>. We compose the embedding of <math id="S5.p38.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p38.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> with the symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{L}Q\rightarrow B\qquad(\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\theta_{1},\theta_{2})\mapsto(\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{1}}{\pi}}e^{2\pi i\theta_{1}},\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{2}}{\pi}}e^{2\pi i\theta_{2}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>π</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>π</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{L}Q\rightarrow B\qquad(\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\theta_{1},\theta_{2})\mapsto(\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{1}}{\pi}}e^{2\pi i\theta_{1}},\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{2}}{\pi}}e^{2\pi i\theta_{2}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p38.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p38.2.1" class="ltx_text">This yields a symplectic embedding of <math id="S5.p38.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p38.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. It is straightforward to check that the two free ends of <math id="S5.p38.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p38.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B</annotation></semantics></math> form a collection of two free disks. By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 5.6. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>, there exists a symplectic embedding of <math id="S5.p38.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,2)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p38.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> such that images of the two free ends are contained in the images of the two free ends of <math id="S5.p38.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that the two free ends of <math id="S5.p38.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math> form a collection of two free disks in <math id="S5.p38.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 5.7. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>, the case <math id="S5.p38.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> is an immediate consequence.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text">The method used to deal with the case <math id="S5.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> can be easily adapted to also treat the case <math id="S5.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead of decomposing <math id="S5.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> into four balls, we decompose into <math id="S5.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> balls, see Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 5.7 ‣ Proof of Proposition in the case n≥2. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>. We omit the details.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S5.F7.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:1.0pt;height:1.0pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="1.33" height="1.33" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,1.33) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,137.48) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="137.48" height="137.48" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15390v1/x16.svg" id="S5.F7.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" width="143" height="143" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5.7. </span>Decomposition of <math id="S5.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.F7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> balls for <math id="S5.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p40.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p40.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p41.1" class="ltx_p">Before turning to the proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, we need to state and prove a version of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> for polydisks. Consider a polydisk <math id="S5.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=P(a_{1},a_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=P(a_{1},a_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the smooth locus of the boundary of <math id="S5.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> has two components</p>
<table id="S5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}P=\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{1}))\times\partial B^{2}(a_{2})\quad\text{and}\quad\partial_{2}P=\partial B^{2}(a_{1})\times\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{2}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.000em;" width="1.000em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}P=\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{1}))\times\partial B^{2}(a_{2})\quad\text{and}\quad\partial_{2}P=\partial B^{2}(a_{1})\times\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{2})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p41.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\left\{1,2\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\left\{1,2\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we have a projection <math id="S5.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{i}:\partial_{i}P\rightarrow\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{i}:\partial_{i}P\rightarrow\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p41.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive real number. A symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p41.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{i}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{i}P</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S5.p41.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">free</span> if the composition <math id="S5.p41.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi:B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi:B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedding. Let <math id="S5.p41.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-negative integer. Consider symplectic embeddings <math id="S5.p41.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{i}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{i}P</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p41.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. We call the tuple <math id="S5.p41.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{j})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{j})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.p41.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">free</span> if the compositions <math id="S5.p41.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise disjoint symplectic embeddings. If <math id="S5.p41.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{j})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{j})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is a tuple of symplectic embeddings <math id="S5.p41.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math>, we call it <span id="S5.p41.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">free</span> if and only if the two subcollections of embeddings into <math id="S5.p41.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p41.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math> are free.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.13</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{j})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{j})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi^{\prime}_{j})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi^{\prime}_{j})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be two free collection of symplectic embeddings <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a symplectomorphism <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> preserving <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfying <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}_{j}=\chi\circ\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}_{j}=\chi\circ\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if, for every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the images of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are contained in the same component of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.11" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text">If there exists such a symplectomorphism <math id="S5.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math>, then clearly the images of <math id="S5.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> must be contained in the same component of <math id="S5.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p43.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p43.1.1" class="ltx_text">In order to show the converse direction, first note that the compactly supported symplectomorphism group of <math id="S5.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on <math id="S5.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-typles of pairwise disjoint symplectic embedings of <math id="S5.p43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p43.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S5.p43.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Since any compactly supported symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.p43.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(B^{2}(a_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.p43.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> preserving <math id="S5.p43.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p43.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math>, we can find such a symplectomorphism <math id="S5.p43.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the property that <math id="S5.p43.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi_{j}^{\prime}=\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\chi^{\prime}\circ\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi_{j}^{\prime}=\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\chi^{\prime}\circ\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S5.p43.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p43.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that the images of <math id="S5.p43.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p43.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are contained in <math id="S5.p43.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{i}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{i}P</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, observe that if <math id="S5.p43.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi,\psi^{\prime}:B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\partial_{i}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi,\psi^{\prime}:B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\partial_{i}P</annotation></semantics></math> are to free symplectic embeddings such that <math id="S5.p43.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi=\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi=\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.p43.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p43.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are related by a symplectomorphism of <math id="S5.p43.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> which is supported near <math id="S5.p43.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}_{i}^{-1}(\operatorname{im}(\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>pr</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}_{i}^{-1}(\operatorname{im}(\operatorname{pr}_{i}\circ\psi))</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows the existence of the desired symplectomorphism <math id="S5.p43.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> with the property that <math id="S5.p43.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}^{\prime}=\chi\circ\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}^{\prime}=\chi\circ\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.14</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional symplectic polydisk and let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-negative integer. Then there exists <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following is true for every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;b\leq b_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;b\leq b_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>: Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(E(a,b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(E(a,b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq j&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq j&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic embedding into the smooth locus of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial P</annotation></semantics></math> and assume that the collection <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{j})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{j})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is free. After possibly permuting the indices of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:E(a,b;c,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:E(a,b;c,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}P</annotation></semantics></math> whose restriction to the <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th free ribbon end of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the embedding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we use the preferred identification of the free ribbon ends with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math>, see Example <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 5.1. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Moreover, the image <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}(\varphi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}(\varphi)</annotation></semantics></math> intersects <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial P</annotation></semantics></math> precisely at the free ends and the intersection is transverse.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For fixed <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a compact subset <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can choose <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly for all polydisks <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.15</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,\alpha,\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,\alpha,\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose that the ellipsoid <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=E(a,b)\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=E(a,b)\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> symplectically embeds into <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathbb{C}P^{1}(\alpha)\times\mathbb{C}P^{1}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathbb{C}P^{1}(\alpha)\times\mathbb{C}P^{1}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\coloneqq\left\{*\right\}\times\mathbb{C}P^{1}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\coloneqq\left\{*\right\}\times\mathbb{C}P^{1}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:E\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:E\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{-1}(C)=E\cap(\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{-1}(C)=E\cap(\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.12" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text">In contrast to Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 5.9. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a>, which is an analogous statement with target manifold <math id="S5.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S5.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{1}\times\mathbb{C}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{1}\times\mathbb{C}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we cannot directly apply <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Lemma 1.6]</cite> because this result is only stated for <math id="S5.p44.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, as we sketch below, the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Lemma 1.6]</cite>, which relies on the symplectic blow up construction for ellipsoid embeddings introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx69" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>]</cite> and the singular inflation techniques of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO15</a>]</cite>, readily adapts to show Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem15" title="Lemma 5.15. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.15</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p45.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p45.1.1" class="ltx_text">The strategy of the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Lemma 1.6]</cite> is the following. For <math id="S5.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small, there clearly exists an embedding <math id="S5.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau E\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau E\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> intersecting the symplectic sphere <math id="S5.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in the desired way. Blowing up this ellipsoid embedding yields a nodal symplectic sphere configuration <math id="S5.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> inside a suitable blowup <math id="S5.p45.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p45.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.p45.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau E</annotation></semantics></math> is in special position with respect to <math id="S5.p45.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, one also obtains a sympelctic surface <math id="S5.p45.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S5.p45.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a proper transform of <math id="S5.p45.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and intersects <math id="S5.p45.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> positively and transverely. The embedding of the full ellipsoid <math id="S5.p45.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by changing the symplectic areas of the sphere components of <math id="S5.p45.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> via symplectic inflation relative to the singular symplectic surface <math id="S5.p45.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cup\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cup\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. In order to carry out this inflation, one needs an embedded symplectic surface intersecting <math id="S5.p45.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cup\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cup\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> positively and transversely and representing a suitable homology class with positive self intersection number. In the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHO16</a>, Lemma 1.6]</cite>, the existence of such a symplectic surface is deduced from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO15</a>, Corollary 1.2.17]</cite>, which deals with blow ups of <math id="S5.p45.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the present setting of <math id="S5.p45.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathbb{C}P^{1}(\alpha)\times\mathbb{C}P^{1}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathbb{C}P^{1}(\alpha)\times\mathbb{C}P^{1}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>, one can obtain the desired symplectic surface from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO15</a>, Theorem 1.2.16]</cite>, which more generally applies to blow ups of arbitrary rational or ruled symplectic manifolds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.13" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition 5.14. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p46.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p46.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is similar to the proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>. The case <math id="S5.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> is immediate from Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5.5. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>. The case <math id="S5.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> is deduced from Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 5.10. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a> in combination with Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem15" title="Lemma 5.15. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.15</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p47.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p47.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that the interior of the polydisk <math id="S5.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to the interior of the Lagrangian product <math id="S5.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a rectangle of side lengths <math id="S5.p47.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p47.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> parallel to the axes and <math id="S5.p47.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, as before, is a square of side lenth <math id="S5.p47.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Again, the generalized Reeb flow on the boundary of <math id="S5.p47.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> is just given by the Minkowski billiard dynamics of <math id="S5.p47.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p47.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us label the edges of <math id="S5.p47.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p47.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> as in Figure <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 5.8 ‣ Proof of Proposition . ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>. The generalized Reeb flow on the boundary of <math id="S5.p47.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> is depicted in Figure <a href="#S5.F9" title="Figure 5.9 ‣ Proof of Proposition . ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S5.F8.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:1.0pt;height:0.3961039pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="1.33" height="0.53" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,0.53) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,108.92) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="274.97" height="108.92" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15390v1/x17.svg" id="S5.F8.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="286" height="114" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.45,0.11)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="16.74" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="s_{b}" text="s _ b" xml:id="S5.F8.pic1.m1">
