                              <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">l</XMTok>
                            </XMApp>
                          </XMApp>
                        </XMath>
                      </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,462.67) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="458.28" height="462.67" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15390v1/x22.svg" id="S5.F9.pic1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" width="476" height="481" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5.9. </span>Reeb dynamics on the boundary of <math id="S5.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p48.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p48.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\xi&lt;x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\xi&lt;x</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset s_{b}\times q_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset s_{b}\times q_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> be a disk of area <math id="S5.p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by first taking a rectangle of area <math id="S5.p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> and then smoothing its corners. Using the Reeb flow, we produce copies of <math id="S5.p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S5.p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{b}\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{b}\times Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\times Q</annotation></semantics></math>. As before, let <math id="S5.p48.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S5.p48.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>) denote the horizantal (resp. vertical) line segment dividing the square <math id="S5.p48.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> into two rectangles of equal area, see Figure <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 5.8 ‣ Proof of Proposition . ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>. If we start at <math id="S5.p48.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{b}\times q_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{b}\times q_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the Reeb flow meets both cross sections <math id="S5.p48.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{b}\times h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{b}\times h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p48.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}\times h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}\times h</annotation></semantics></math> exactly once before returning to <math id="S5.p48.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{b}\times q_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{b}\times q_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S5.p48.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}_{h}\subset(r_{b}\cup r_{t})\times h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}_{h}\subset(r_{b}\cup r_{t})\times h</annotation></semantics></math> to be the intersection of all characteristics starting at <math id="S5.p48.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p48.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{b}\cup r_{t})\times h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{b}\cup r_{t})\times h</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we can arrange <math id="S5.p48.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> in such a way that if we place a triangle at the bottom or top edge of <math id="S5.p48.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as in Figure <a href="#S5.F10" title="Figure 5.10 ‣ Proof of Proposition . ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a> and if <math id="S5.p48.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}\subset\partial(T\times_{L}Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}\subset\partial(T\times_{L}Q)</annotation></semantics></math> is the collection of four disks of area <math id="S5.p48.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> used in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>, then the component of <math id="S5.p48.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> contained in the interface between <math id="S5.p48.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p48.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> exactly matches the component of <math id="S5.p48.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}</annotation></semantics></math> contained in this interface.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S5.F10.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:1.0pt;height:1.46pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="1.33" height="1.94" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,1.94) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,133.77) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="91.66" height="133.77" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15390v1/x23.svg" id="S5.F10.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" width="96" height="139" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.42,1.17)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="14.74" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="T" text="T" xml:id="S5.F10.pic1.m1">
                        <XMath xml:id="S5.F10.pic1.m1.1">
                          <XMTok color="#1A1A1A" font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">T</XMTok>
                        </XMath>
                      </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.7,0.4)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="14.08" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="S" text="S" xml:id="S5.F10.pic1.m2">
                        <XMath xml:id="S5.F10.pic1.m2.1">
                          <XMTok color="#1A1A1A" font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">S</XMTok>
                        </XMath>
                      </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.67,0.83)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="40.25" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="t_{b}=s_{t}" text="t _ b = s _ t" xml:id="S5.F10.pic1.m3">
                        <XMath xml:id="S5.F10.pic1.m3.1">
                          <XMApp color="#1A1A1A" xml:id="S5.F10.pic1.m3.1.1">
                            <XMTok meaning="equals" role="RELOP">=</XMTok>
                            <XMApp xml:id="S5.F10.pic1.m3.1.1.2">
                              <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                              <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">t</XMTok>
                              <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">b</XMTok>
                            </XMApp>
                            <XMApp xml:id="S5.F10.pic1.m3.1.1.3">
                              <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                              <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">s</XMTok>
                              <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">t</XMTok>
                            </XMApp>
                          </XMApp>
                        </XMath>
                      </Math></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5.10. </span>A triangle <math id="S5.F10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> on top of a rectangle <math id="S5.F10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> meeting it at the common edge <math id="S5.F10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b}=s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b}=s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p49.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p49.1.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly, for <math id="S5.p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\eta&lt;y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\eta&lt;y</annotation></semantics></math> we can construct <math id="S5.p49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}_{v}\subset(r_{l}\cup r_{r})\times v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}_{v}\subset(r_{l}\cup r_{r})\times v</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of two disk components of area <math id="S5.p49.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p50.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p50.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now consider a polydisk <math id="S5.p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=P(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=P(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and an integer <math id="S5.p50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. By symmetry, we may reduce ourselves to the case <math id="S5.p50.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq y</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p50.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the axis parallel rectangle of width <math id="S5.p50.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and height <math id="S5.p50.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. We divide <math id="S5.p50.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> into rectangles and triangles as follows: First, we divide <math id="S5.p50.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p50.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> rectangles of width <math id="S5.p50.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and height <math id="S5.p50.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y/(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y/(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Each of these <math id="S5.p50.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> rectangles, we further divide into squares of side length <math id="S5.p50.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y/(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y/(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and a single rectangle of height <math id="S5.p50.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y/(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y/(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> whose width is contained in the interval <math id="S5.p50.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[y/(n+1),2y/(n+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[y/(n+1),2y/(n+1))</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that since <math id="S5.p50.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq y</annotation></semantics></math> by assumption, each of the <math id="S5.p50.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> rectangles from the first division step gives rise to at least <math id="S5.p50.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> squares. Each of the squares, we further divide into two triangles along one of the diagonals. See Figure <a href="#S5.F11" title="Figure 5.11 ‣ Proof of Proposition . ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a> for an illustration of the resulting decomposition of <math id="S5.p50.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p50.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p50.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the rectangles and triangles, respectively, in the decomposition. We obtain an induced decomposition of <math id="S5.p50.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> into polydisks <math id="S5.p50.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{k}\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{k}\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> and balls <math id="S5.p50.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F11" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S5.F11.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:1.0pt;height:0.62941176pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="1.33" height="0.84" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,0.84) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,173.08) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="274.97" height="173.08" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15390v1/x24.svg" id="S5.F11.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="286" height="180" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5.11. </span>A decomposition of <math id="S5.F11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> into rectangles and triangles and a schematic of the ribbon complexes <math id="S5.F11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> (blue) and <math id="S5.F11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (green).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p51.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p51.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be sufficiently small and <math id="S5.p51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p51.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(E(a,b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(E(a,b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, let <math id="S5.p51.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.p51.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}:B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math> be a free collection of <math id="S5.p51.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> symplectic embeddings. We construct an embedding of <math id="S5.p51.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p51.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> following the same strategy used in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p52" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p52.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p52.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, we embed a copy of <math id="S5.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(\beta;\gamma,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(\beta;\gamma,4)</annotation></semantics></math> for suitable parameters <math id="S5.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p52.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p52.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> such that the free ends land in the disks <math id="S5.p52.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}\subset\partial(T_{j}\times_{L}Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}\subset\partial(T_{j}\times_{L}Q)</annotation></semantics></math> introduced in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>. Moreover, we embed a copy of <math id="S5.p52.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\alpha,\beta;\gamma,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\alpha,\beta;\gamma,2)</annotation></semantics></math> for suitable <math id="S5.p52.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p52.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{k}\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{k}\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> such that the two free ends land in the disks <math id="S5.p52.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> introduced above. Here we use Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Lemma 5.12. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a> and its polydisk analogue. Then we connect matching free ribbons at the interfaces between neighbouring cells. A schematic of the resulting ribbon complex <math id="S5.p52.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is depicted in Figure <a href="#S5.F11" title="Figure 5.11 ‣ Proof of Proposition . ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p53" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p53.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p53.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, we embed a copy of <math id="S5.p53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=K(G,A,f,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=K(G,A,f,b)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> for suitable <math id="S5.p53.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f&gt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f&gt;b</annotation></semantics></math> and a suitable labelled ribbon graph <math id="S5.p53.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,A)</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S5.F11" title="Figure 5.11 ‣ Proof of Proposition . ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>. This uses the case <math id="S5.p53.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition 5.14. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 5.11. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>. Composition with an appropriate embedding <math id="S5.p53.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\times_{L}Q\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\times_{L}Q\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}P</annotation></semantics></math> yields an embedding of <math id="S5.p53.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p53.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> mapping the free ribbon ends into <math id="S5.p53.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial P</annotation></semantics></math> via a free collection of <math id="S5.p53.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> embeddings <math id="S5.p53.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math>. We can build <math id="S5.p53.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> such that the number of free ribbon ends of <math id="S5.p53.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> which land in <math id="S5.p53.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}P</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S5.p53.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math>) matches the number of embeddings <math id="S5.p53.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with image in <math id="S5.p53.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}P</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S5.p53.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math>). Moreover, we can arrange the parameters of <math id="S5.p53.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> such that Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 5.6. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a> yields an embedding <math id="S5.p53.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b;c,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b;c,n)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}P</annotation></semantics></math>, again mapping the free ribbon ends into <math id="S5.p53.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}P\cup\partial_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math> via a free collection of maps. By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Lemma 5.13. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a> we can assume that these maps precisely agree with the <math id="S5.p53.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, after possibly permuting the indices of <math id="S5.p53.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p54.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p54.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, observe that if <math id="S5.p54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed and <math id="S5.p54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> range over a compact subset <math id="S5.p54.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the parameters of all polydisks and balls in our division of <math id="S5.p54.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> are uniformly bounded away from zero. Therefore, it ultimately follows from Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5.5. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> that the threshold <math id="S5.p54.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be chosen uniformly.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.14" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.p55" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p55.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p55.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us normalized the volume of <math id="S5.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> to be equal to <math id="S5.p55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. In this situation, we have <math id="S5.p55.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(K)=\overline{w}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(K)=\overline{w}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p55.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and assume that <math id="S5.p55.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(K)\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(K)\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p56" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p56.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p56.1.1" class="ltx_text">Observe that we can bound the total number of domains of <math id="S5.p56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> from above in terms of <math id="S5.p56.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. After possibly removing some ribbons, we can assume that the graph underlying the ribbon complex <math id="S5.p56.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree. In this situation, we can in addition bound the total number of ribbons of <math id="S5.p56.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> from above in terms of <math id="S5.p56.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. After possibly shrinking the widths of the ribbons of <math id="S5.p56.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we can assume that, for every domain <math id="S5.p56.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p56.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, the collection of all attaching maps of ribbons attached to <math id="S5.p56.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is free. Note that we can carry out this simplification in such a way that the new width <math id="S5.p56.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(K)</annotation></semantics></math> can be bounded from below just in terms of <math id="S5.p56.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p57" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p57.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p57.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each domain <math id="S5.p57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p57.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.p57.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of ribbons attached to <math id="S5.p57.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us choose <math id="S5.p57.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small such that, for every domain <math id="S5.p57.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p57.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, the assertion of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> holds for the pair <math id="S5.p57.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,n_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,n_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.p57.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a ball and the assertion of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition 5.14. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a> holds if <math id="S5.p57.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a polydisk. Moreover, we choose <math id="S5.p57.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> smaller than any width of a ribbon of <math id="S5.p57.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that this choice of <math id="S5.p57.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> can be made to depend only on <math id="S5.p57.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. The reason is that the parameters of the balls, polydisks, and ribbons of <math id="S5.p57.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded away from zero in terms of <math id="S5.p57.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and that all <math id="S5.p57.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded from above in terms of <math id="S5.p57.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p57.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be arbitrary. Pick a positive number <math id="S5.p57.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p57.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-\varepsilon&lt;c&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-\varepsilon&lt;c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>. For every domain <math id="S5.p57.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p57.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, pick <math id="S5.p57.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{X}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{X}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p57.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(X)-\varepsilon&lt;\operatorname{vol}(E(a_{X},b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(X)-\varepsilon&lt;\operatorname{vol}(E(a_{X},b))&lt;\operatorname{vol}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p58" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p58.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p58.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider a domain <math id="S5.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p58.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{j})_{1\leq j\leq n_{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{j})_{1\leq j\leq n_{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the collection of attaching maps of all the ribbons attached to <math id="S5.p58.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Pick a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.p58.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{X}:E(a_{X},b;c,n_{X})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{X}:E(a_{X},b;c,n_{X})\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}X</annotation></semantics></math> which maps the free ribbon ends into <math id="S5.p58.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial X</annotation></semantics></math> via the restricted attaching maps <math id="S5.p58.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}|_{B^{2}(c)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}|_{B^{2}(c)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a symplectic embedding exists by Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 5.8. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> if <math id="S5.p58.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a ball and by Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition 5.14. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a> if <math id="S5.p58.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a polydisk. We connect the complexes <math id="S5.p58.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{X},b;c,n_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{X},b;c,n_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> to a single complex <math id="S5.p58.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Let <math id="S5.p58.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=[0,1]\times B^{2}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=[0,1]\times B^{2}(d)</annotation></semantics></math> be a ribbon of <math id="S5.p58.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider one of its ends <math id="S5.p58.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{i\right\}\times B^{2}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{i\right\}\times B^{2}(d)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p58.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be the domain of <math id="S5.p58.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> this end is attached to. The image of one of the free ends of <math id="S5.p58.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{X},b;c,n_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{X},b;c,n_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the end <math id="S5.p58.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{i\right\}\times B^{2}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{i\right\}\times B^{2}(d)</annotation></semantics></math>, which we identify with its image in <math id="S5.p58.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial X</annotation></semantics></math>. The restriction of <math id="S5.p58.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to the free end <math id="S5.p58.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p58.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{X},b;c,n_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{X},b;c,n_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is simply given by inclusion <math id="S5.p58.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\left\{i\right\}\times B^{2}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(c)\hookrightarrow\left\{i\right\}\times B^{2}(d)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us glue the free end to the end <math id="S5.p58.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{i\right\}\times B^{2}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{i\right\}\times B^{2}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> of the ribbon <math id="S5.p58.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times B^{2}(c)\subset R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times B^{2}(c)\subset R</annotation></semantics></math>. We perform this gluing at all the free ends of all the complexes <math id="S5.p58.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{X},b;c,n_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{X},b;c,n_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.p58.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> denote the resulting complex. By construction, this complex <math id="S5.p58.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> symplectically embeds into <math id="S5.p58.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, it is a complex of the form considered in Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 5.6. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p59" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p59.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p59.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now suppose that <math id="S5.p59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:K\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic embedding and let <math id="S5.p59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be an open neighbourhood of <math id="S5.p59.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(K)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 5.6. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>, the ellipsoid <math id="S5.p59.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,c)</annotation></semantics></math> symplectically embeds into <math id="S5.p59.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Letting <math id="S5.p59.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> tend to zero, we see that <math id="S5.p59.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{E}_{a/b}(K)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{E}_{a/b}(K)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S5.p59.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a/b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a/b</annotation></semantics></math> can be bounded from above just in terms of <math id="S5.p59.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. It therefore follows from Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5.5. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> that there exists a threshold <math id="S5.p59.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> only depending on <math id="S5.p59.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p59.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{E}_{e}(K)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{E}_{e}(K)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p59.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\geq a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\geq a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Tame packings by balls and polydisks</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the notion of a <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tame packing by balls and polydisks</span>. The motivation behind this definition is that the existence of a tame packing by balls and polydisks implies ellipsoid embedding stability. This is an easy corollary of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>. We also introduce the stronger notion of a <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-tame packing by balls and polydisks</span>. The advantage of this stronger notion is that if a symplectic manifold <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into pieces which admit <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packings by balls and polydisks, then so does <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we show that, for every <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonian <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> on the annulus <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> small perturbation of a strictly positive affine Hamiltonian, the subgraph <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing by balls and polydisks and therefore satisfies ellipsoid embedding stability; see Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 6.6. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>. We extend this result to Hamiltonians <math id="S6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> which are allowed to vanish on one boundary component of <math id="S6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the content of Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 6.7. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold of finite volume. We say that <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tame packing by balls and polydisks</span> (or simply a <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tame packing</span>) if there exists <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a connected symplectic ribbon complex <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, all of whose domains are balls or polydisks, which admits a symplectic embedding into <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, and which satisfies <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(K)\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(K)\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(K)\geq\operatorname{vol}(M)-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(K)\geq\operatorname{vol}(M)-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following is an immediate corollary of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold of finite volume. If <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a tame packing by balls and polydisks, then ellipsoid embedding stability holds for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}^{E}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}^{E}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all sufficiently large <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact symplectic <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold with boundary and corners. We say that <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-tame packing by balls and polydisks</span> (or simply a <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-tame packing</span>) if there exists an open and dense subset <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\partial M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\partial M</annotation></semantics></math> contained in the smooth locus of the boundary of <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with the following property: For every finite collection <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> of pairwise disjoint closed symplectic disks contained <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every <math id="S6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a connected symplectic ribbon complex <math id="S6.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, all of whose domains are balls or polydisks, which admits a symplectic embedding into <math id="S6.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that the images of the free ribbon ends of <math id="S6.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are precisely given by the disks <math id="S6.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and which satisfies <math id="S6.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(K)\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(K)\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vol}(K)\geq\operatorname{vol}(M)-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vol}(K)\geq\operatorname{vol}(M)-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact connected symplectic <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold with boundary and corners. Suppose that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a stratification <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that each top-dimensional stratum of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing by balls and polydisks. Then <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing by balls and polydisks.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each top-dimensional stratum <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, pick an open and dense subset <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}\subset\partial T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}\subset\partial T</annotation></semantics></math> as in the definition of a <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing. Let <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\partial M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\partial M</annotation></semantics></math> be the intersection with <math id="S6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial M</annotation></semantics></math> of the union of all the sets <math id="S6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The set <math id="S6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is open and dense in <math id="S6.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial M</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\subset U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\subset U</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary collection of pairwise disjoint symplectic disks. Our goal is to fill the volume of <math id="S6.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> up to arbitrarily small error by a connected symplectic ribbon complex with free ribbon ends at the disks <math id="S6.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and with width bounded away from zero. For any pair of distinct top-dimensional strata <math id="S6.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> which meet at a stratum <math id="S6.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of codimension <math id="S6.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, pick a closed symplectic disk contained in <math id="S6.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cap U_{T}\cap U_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cap U_{T}\cap U_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the resulting collection of symplectic disks. For each top-dimensional stratum <math id="S6.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can fill arbitrarily much of the volume of <math id="S6.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by a connected symplectic ribbon complex <math id="S6.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with free ends at the disks <math id="S6.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial T\cap(\mathcal{D}\cup\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial T\cap(\mathcal{D}\cup\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math> and with width bounded away from zero. Now connect all the ribbon complexes <math id="S6.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to a single connected ribbon complex <math id="S6.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> by gluing all pairs of free ribbon ends meeting at disks in <math id="S6.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. The free ends of <math id="S6.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are precisely the disks <math id="S6.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we can arrange <math id="S6.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> to fill the volume of <math id="S6.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> up to arbitrarily small error while keeping the width <math id="S6.p5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(K)</annotation></semantics></math> bounded away from zero.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a)\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a)\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the triangle with vertices <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\coloneqq[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\coloneqq[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the square. Then the Lagrangian product <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>, whose interior is symplectomorphic to the interior of the ball <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing. Indeed, recall from the proof of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Lemma 5.12. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a> that the ball <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(ra)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(ra)</annotation></semantics></math> symplectically embeds into <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the image of the embedding can be arranged to approximate <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\sqrt{r}a)\times_{L}\sqrt{r}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>r</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mrow><msqrt><mi>r</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\sqrt{r}a)\times_{L}\sqrt{r}Q</annotation></semantics></math>, translated into the interior of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>, arbitrarily well. Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be a collection of symplectic disks in the smooth locus of the boundary of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>. For every <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently close to <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, we can connect the disks <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> to the boundary of the symplectically embedded ball <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(ra)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(ra)</annotation></semantics></math> via a disjoint collection of symplectic ribbons. We can view the result as an embedding of a symplectic ribbon complex <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of a single ball and ribbons whose free ends are mapped to the disks <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> approaches <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the volume of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> tends to the volume of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>, while the width <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(K)</annotation></semantics></math> remains bounded away from <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, showing that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(a,b)\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(a,b)\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the rectangle with vertices <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. Again, let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the square. Then the Lagrangian product <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(a,b)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(a,b)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>, whose interior is symplectomorphic to the interior of the polydisk <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>, admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing. In order to see this, note that we can alternatively view <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(a,b)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(a,b)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> as the symplectic product <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(a,1)\times\square(b,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(a,1)\times\square(b,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, pick a symplectic embedding <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(ra)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\square(a,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(ra)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\square(a,1)</annotation></semantics></math> whose image is a rectangle with slightly rounded corners contained in the interior of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(a,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(a,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Pick an analogous embedding <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(rb)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\square(b,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(rb)\overset{s}{\hookrightarrow}\square(b,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The product of these two embeddings yields a symplectic embedding of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(ra,rb)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(ra,rb)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(a,1)\times\square(b,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(a,1)\times\square(b,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. As in Example <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="Example 6.3. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>, we can connect the image of this symplectic embedding to any given collection of symplectic disks in the smooth locus of the boundary of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(a,b)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(a,b)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> via disjoint symplectic ribbons. Letting <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> tend to <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(a,b)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(a,b)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the triangle with vertices <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that if <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a/b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a/b</annotation></semantics></math> is rational, then the Lagrangian product <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>, whose interior is symplectomorphic to the interior of the ellipsoid <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>, admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing. First, we argue that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing, where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-torus obtained from the square <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> by identifying opposite sides. Recall that for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a/b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a/b</annotation></semantics></math> rational, one can decompose <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> into finitely many triangles <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, each of which can be obtained from a triangle of the form <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> by translations and the action of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx69" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>, §3.1]</cite>). This yields a stratification of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with top-dimensional strata given by <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each of these strata is symplectomorphic to <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a_{i})\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a_{i})\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall from Example <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="Example 6.3. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a_{i})\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a_{i})\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing. In view of the natural inclusion <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(\Delta(a_{i})\times_{L}Q)\hookrightarrow\Delta(a_{i})\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(\Delta(a_{i})\times_{L}Q)\hookrightarrow\Delta(a_{i})\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, this implies that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a_{i})\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a_{i})\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing. Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 6.2. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> thus implies that that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\times\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\times\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is well-known that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(ra,rb)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(ra,rb)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> symplectically embeds into <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Explicit embeddings can be obtained via a variant of the symplectic embedding construction from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch03</a>, §2]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch05a</a>, §4.1]</cite> which we reviewed in the proof of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Lemma 5.12. ‣ 5. Symplectic ribbon complexes ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a>. For <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}(s)\coloneqq[0,s]\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}(s)\coloneqq[0,s]\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the annulus of area <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we can regard the Lagrangian product <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as a subset of the symplectic product <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}(a)\times\mathbb{A}(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}(a)\times\mathbb{A}(b)</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we can regard the Lagrangian product <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> as a subset of the symplectic product <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(a,1)\times\square(b,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(a,1)\times\square(b,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a family of loops <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> in the complement of a line segment <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> in the rectangle <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(\square(a,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(\square(a,1))</annotation></semantics></math> as depicted in Figure <a href="#S6.F1" title="Figure 6.1 ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>. Then there exists a symplectomorphism <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{a}:\operatorname{int}(\mathbb{A}(a))\rightarrow\operatorname{int}(\square(a,1))\setminus L_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{a}:\operatorname{int}(\mathbb{A}(a))\rightarrow\operatorname{int}(\square(a,1))\setminus L_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> which maps circles of the form <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{x\right\}\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{x\right\}\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> to members of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{b}:\operatorname{int}(\mathbb{A}(b))\rightarrow\operatorname{int}(\square(b,1))\setminus L_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{b}:\operatorname{int}(\mathbb{A}(b))\rightarrow\operatorname{int}(\square(b,1))\setminus L_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> be the analogous symplectomorphism arising from a family of loops <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> in the rectangle <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(\square(b,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(\square(b,1))</annotation></semantics></math>. Now consider the triangle <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(ra,rb)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(ra,rb)</annotation></semantics></math> and slightly translate it off the axes into the interior of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}(ra,rb)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}(ra,rb)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the resulting triangle. We can arrange the families of loops <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the image of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}(ra,rb)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}(ra,rb)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{a}\times\varphi_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{a}\times\varphi_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> approximates <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(ra,rb)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(ra,rb)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> arbitrarily well. This yields the desired embedding of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(ra,rb)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(ra,rb)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\times Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a collection of symplectic disks <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> in the smooth locus of the boundary of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>, we can connect them to symplectic disks in the boundary of the image of the symplectic embedding of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(ra,rb)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(ra,rb)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> via symplectic ribbons. Since we already know that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(ra,rb)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(ra,rb)\times_{L}\mathbb{T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing, we can conclude that the same is true for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S6.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:1.0pt;height:0.60747664pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="1.33" height="0.81" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,0.81) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,111.32) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="183.31" height="111.32" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15390v1/x25.svg" id="S6.F1.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="191" height="116" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.67,0.02)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="15.54" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="1" text="1" xml:id="S6.F1.pic1.m1">
                      <XMath xml:id="S6.F1.pic1.m1.1">
                        <XMTok color="#1A1A1A" meaning="1" role="NUMBER">1</XMTok>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(1.29,0.44)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="15.94" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="a" text="a" xml:id="S6.F1.pic1.m2">
                      <XMath xml:id="S6.F1.pic1.m2.1">
                        <XMTok color="#1A1A1A" font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">a</XMTok>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.87,0.22)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="24.31" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="L_{a}" text="L _ a" xml:id="S6.F1.pic1.m3">
                      <XMath xml:id="S6.F1.pic1.m3.1">
                        <XMApp color="#1A1A1A" xml:id="S6.F1.pic1.m3.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">L</XMTok>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">a</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6.1. </span>A special admissible family of loops <math id="S6.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(a,1)\setminus L_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(a,1)\setminus L_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.6</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the setting of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, the subgraph <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing by balls and polydisks. Therefore, ellipsoid embedding stability holds for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> yields a stratification of <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math> into subgraphs of the form <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H_{\alpha}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H_{\alpha}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})</annotation></semantics></math> for rational <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(C|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(C|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(c)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Cutting the annulus <math id="S6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}(c)=[0,c]\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}(c)=[0,c]\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> along the line segment <math id="S6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,c]\times\left\{0\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,c]\times\left\{0\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> turns the former type of stratum into <math id="S6.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(c,\alpha c)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(c,\alpha c)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math> and the latter type into <math id="S6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(c,C)\times_{L}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(c,C)\times_{L}Q</annotation></semantics></math>. By Examples <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Example 6.5. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a> and <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem4" title="Example 6.4. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>, both Lagrangian products admit <math id="S6.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packings. Thus by Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 6.2. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> the same is true for <math id="S6.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.7</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Hamiltonian of the form <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(t,x,y)=ax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(t,x,y)=ax</annotation></semantics></math> for a real number <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonian which vanishes on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing by balls and polydisks.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We divide the annulus <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> into three smaller annuli</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{0}\coloneqq[0,\frac{1}{3}]\times\mathbb{T}\qquad\mathbb{A}_{1}\coloneqq[\frac{1}{3},\frac{2}{3}]\times\mathbb{T}\qquad\mathbb{A}_{2}\coloneqq[\frac{2}{3},1]\times\mathbb{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{0}\coloneqq[0,\frac{1}{3}]\times\mathbb{T}\qquad\mathbb{A}_{1}\coloneqq[\frac{1}{3},\frac{2}{3}]\times\mathbb{T}\qquad\mathbb{A}_{2}\coloneqq[\frac{2}{3},1]\times\mathbb{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Pick a rational number <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that both <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\#\overline{H}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\#\overline{H}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S6.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S6.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h/2</annotation></semantics></math>. We may therefore chose a Hamiltonian <math id="S6.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G:[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=H\#\overline{H}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=H\#\overline{H}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> in a neighbourhood of <math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=H_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=H_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> in a neighbourhood of <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S6.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">We divide <math id="S6.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H)</annotation></semantics></math> into two pieces <math id="S6.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G,H)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 6.2. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>, it suffices to show that each of these two pieces <math id="S6.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G,H)</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="S6.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G,H)</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to <math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\overline{G}\#H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\overline{G}\#H)</annotation></semantics></math>, so it suffices to find a <math id="S6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing for <math id="S6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\overline{G}\#H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\overline{G}\#H)</annotation></semantics></math>. On the annulus <math id="S6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G}\#H=H_{\alpha}\#\overline{H}\#H=H_{\alpha}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G}\#H=H_{\alpha}\#\overline{H}\#H=H_{\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">We stratify <math id="S6.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\overline{G}\#H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\overline{G}\#H)</annotation></semantics></math> into two pieces <math id="S6.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\overline{G}\#H|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{0}})=D(H_{\alpha}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\overline{G}\#H|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{0}})=D(H_{\alpha}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\overline{G}\#H|_{[0,1]\times(\mathbb{A}_{1}\cup\mathbb{A}_{2})})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\overline{G}\#H|_{[0,1]\times(\mathbb{A}_{1}\cup\mathbb{A}_{2})})</annotation></semantics></math>. The former piece admits a <math id="S6.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing by Example <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Example 6.5. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>. The latter piece is precisely of the form considered in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Stratification of subgraphs ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and therefore admits a <math id="S6.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing by Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 6.6. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>. Applying again Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 6.2. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>, we see that <math id="S6.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\overline{G}\#H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\overline{G}\#H)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us now turn to <math id="S6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="S6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> by</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\coloneqq\min\left\{G,\frac{2\alpha}{3}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\coloneqq\min\left\{G,\frac{2\alpha}{3}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">We stratify <math id="S6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S6.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(F)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{clos}(\operatorname{int}D(F,G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>clos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{clos}(\operatorname{int}D(F,G))</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the latter stratum is symplectomorphic to <math id="S6.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(H_{\alpha}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(H_{\alpha}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math> and hence admits a <math id="S6.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing. It remains to deal with <math id="S6.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(F)</annotation></semantics></math>. On a neighbourhood of <math id="S6.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hamiltonian <math id="S6.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is autonomous. This is not necessarily the case outside of this neighbourhood, but it is possible to construct a Hamiltonian <math id="S6.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{F}</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="S6.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S6.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, agrees with <math id="S6.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighbourhood of <math id="S6.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfies <math id="S6.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{F}_{t}=\tilde{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{F}_{t}=\tilde{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently close to the endpoints of <math id="S6.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and has the property that <math id="S6.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(F)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F})</annotation></semantics></math> are symplectomorphic. Since <math id="S6.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{F}_{t}=\tilde{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{F}_{t}=\tilde{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S6.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hamiltonian <math id="S6.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{F}</annotation></semantics></math> descends to a <math id="S6.p9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic Hamiltonian defined on <math id="S6.p9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us write <math id="S6.p9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{F}|_{\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{F}|_{\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> to refer to this <math id="S6.p9.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic Hamiltonian.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">We claim that <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F}|_{\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F}|_{\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing. Indeed, consider the symplectomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:(\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{T})^{2}\rightarrow(\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{T})^{2}\qquad(s,t,x,y)\mapsto(x,y,s,t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:(\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{T})^{2}\rightarrow(\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{T})^{2}\qquad(s,t,x,y)\mapsto(x,y,s,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">swapping the two factors. The image of <math id="S6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F}|_{\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F}|_{\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S6.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S6.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(E)</annotation></semantics></math> for a <math id="S6.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible Hamiltonian <math id="S6.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}(2\alpha/3)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}(2\alpha/3)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a <math id="S6.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> small perturbation of</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e:\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}(2\alpha/3)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad e(t,x,y)=1-\frac{x}{2\alpha}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e:\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}(2\alpha/3)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad e(t,x,y)=1-\frac{x}{2\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">By Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 6.6. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>, <math id="S6.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(E|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(2\alpha/3)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(E|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(2\alpha/3)})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing. In view of the natural inclusion</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(D(E|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(2\alpha/3)}))\subset D(E)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(D(E|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}(2\alpha/3)}))\subset D(E),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p10.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.4.1" class="ltx_text">the same is true for <math id="S6.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(E)</annotation></semantics></math> and hence for <math id="S6.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F}|_{\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F}|_{\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to deduce that <math id="S6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing. This follows from an argument similar to the one explained in Example <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Example 6.5. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>. Set <math id="S6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\coloneqq\frac{2\alpha}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\coloneqq\frac{2\alpha}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F})</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of the symplectic product <math id="S6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}(r)\times\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}(r)\times\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of <math id="S6.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(r,1)\times\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(r,1)\times\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. As in Example <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Example 6.5. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>, we obtain a symplectomorphism <math id="S6.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S6.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(\mathbb{A}(r))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(\mathbb{A}(r))</annotation></semantics></math> and the complement of a line segment in <math id="S6.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(r,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(r,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S6.F1" title="Figure 6.1 ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>. The image of <math id="S6.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{int}(D(\tilde{F}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}(D(\tilde{F}))</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S6.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\times\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>𝔸</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\times\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an approximation of <math id="S6.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset\operatorname{int}(D(\tilde{F}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset\operatorname{int}(D(\tilde{F}))</annotation></semantics></math> be a domain conformally symplectomorphic to <math id="S6.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F})</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by slightly pushing the boundary of <math id="S6.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F})</annotation></semantics></math> inwards. Given a collection of disks in a suitable open and dense subset of the boundary of <math id="S6.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we can connect them to the image of <math id="S6.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S6.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\times\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>𝔸</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\times\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> via symplectic ribbons. Since we already know that <math id="S6.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S6.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing, we can conclude that the same is true for <math id="S6.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\tilde{F}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\tilde{F}|_{[0,1]\times\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Symplectic frusta</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In view of Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 6.2. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Corollary 6.1. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>, in order to show that ellipsoid embedding stability holds for a symplectic <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to decompose <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> into pieces which admit <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packings by balls and polydisks. We introduce the notion of a <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">perturbed symplectic frustum with cuts</span>, which will be technically convenient for this purpose. The main result of this section, Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 7.3. ‣ 7. Symplectic frusta ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>, states that each perturbed symplectic frustum with cuts admits a <math id="S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-tame packing by balls and polydisks. This is a relatively straightforward consequence of Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 6.6. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 6.7. ‣ 6. Tame packings by balls and polydisks ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be positive real numbers and suppose that <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;c</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the Hamiltonian</p>
<table id="S7.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{a,b,c}:[0,1]\times B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad H_{a,b,c}(t,p)\coloneqq a\cdot\min\left\{1,\frac{c-\pi|p|^{2}}{c-b}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{a,b,c}:[0,1]\times B^{2}(c)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad H_{a,b,c}(t,p)\coloneqq a\cdot\min\left\{1,\frac{c-\pi|p|^{2}}{c-b}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S7.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symplectic frustum</span> <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(a,b,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(a,b,c)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be</p>
<table id="S7.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(a,b,c)\coloneqq D(H_{a,b,c})\subset\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times B^{2}(c)\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(a,b,c)\coloneqq D(H_{a,b,c})\subset\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\times B^{2}(c)\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Any symplectic frustum <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=F(a,b,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=F(a,b,c)</annotation></semantics></math> is a manifold with boundary and corners. We abbreviate the five top-dimensional strata of the boundary <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial F</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S7.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial_{0}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial_{0}F</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial F\cap(\mathbb{R}\times\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{R}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial F\cap(\mathbb{R}\times\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{R}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial_{1}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial_{1}F</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial F\cap(\mathbb{R}\times\left\{1\right\}\times\mathbb{R}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial F\cap(\mathbb{R}\times\left\{1\right\}\times\mathbb{R}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial_{+}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial_{+}F</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial F\cap(\left\{a\right\}\times\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial F\cap(\left\{a\right\}\times\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial_{-}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>−</mo></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial_{-}F</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial F\cap(\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial F\cap(\left\{0\right\}\times\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial_{\bullet}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>∙</mo></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial_{\bullet}F</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{clos}(\partial F\cap(0,a)\times(0,1)\times\mathbb{R}^{2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>clos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{clos}(\partial F\cap(0,a)\times(0,1)\times\mathbb{R}^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p3.2" class="ltx_p">See Figure <a href="#S7.F1" title="Figure 7.1 ‣ 7. Symplectic frusta ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> for an illustration. The <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional strata of <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial F</annotation></semantics></math> are either symplectic, meaning that the ambient symplectic form on <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to an area form, or Lagrangian, meaning that the symplectic form vanishes. The symplectic strata are given by <math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{j}F\cap\partial_{\pm}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∩</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{j}F\cap\partial_{\pm}F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{j}F\cap\partial_{\bullet}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∩</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>∙</mo></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{j}F\cap\partial_{\bullet}F</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\left\{0,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\left\{0,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Lagrangian strata are <math id="S7.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\pm}F\cap\partial_{\bullet}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>±</mo></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∩</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mo>∙</mo></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\pm}F\cap\partial_{\bullet}F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
