                            <XMTok name="Sigma" role="UNKNOWN">Σ</XMTok>
                            <XMTok fontsize="70%" meaning="plus" role="ADDOP">+</XMTok>
                          </XMApp>
                        </XMath>
                      </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,243.89) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="458.28" height="243.89" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15390v1/x38.svg" id="S9.F1.pic1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="476" height="253" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9.1. </span>Intersection of the surfaces <math id="S9.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (green) and <math id="S9.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> (blue) with a flow box. The sets <math id="S9.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> are highlighted by dashed lines.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S9.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The sets <math id="S9.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used to define a stratification <math id="S9.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the <math id="S9.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-skeleton of this stratification to be the union <math id="S9.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma\cup S\cup S^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma\cup S\cup S^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S9.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-skeleton to be the set of non-smooth points of <math id="S9.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\Sigma\cup S\cup S^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\Sigma\cup S\cup S^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. The set of top-dimensional strata of <math id="S9.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the following (see Figure <a href="#S9.F2" title="Figure 9.2 ‣ 9.1. Stratifications compatible with a flow ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.2</span></a>):</p>
<ol id="S9.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S9.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(S1)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a small square at each T-shaped singularity of <math id="S9.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(S2)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a thin rectangle along each smooth arc of <math id="S9.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and a thin annulus along each smooth circle component of <math id="S9.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(S3)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for each component <math id="S9.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\setminus S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\setminus S</annotation></semantics></math>, a set <math id="S9.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> which is obtained from <math id="S9.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> by slightly pushing inwards some of its boundary arcs.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S9.F2" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S9.F2.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:1.0pt;height:0.98pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="1.33" height="1.30" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,1.30) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,224.62) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="229.14" height="224.62" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15390v1/x39.svg" id="S9.F2.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" width="238" height="233" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.71,0.68)"></g><g transform="translate(0.84,1.02)"></g><g transform="translate(1.02,0.66)"></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9.2. </span>Stratification <math id="S9.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of a disk component of <math id="S9.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Strata of type (S<a href="#S9.I2.i1" title="item 1 ‣ 9.1. Stratifications compatible with a flow ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) are drawn in yellow, strata of type (S<a href="#S9.I2.i2" title="item 2 ‣ 9.1. Stratifications compatible with a flow ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) in blue, and strata of type (S<a href="#S9.I2.i3" title="item 3 ‣ 9.1. Stratifications compatible with a flow ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) in green.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S9.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The top-dimensional strata of <math id="S9.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> are in bijection. Given a top-dimensional stratum <math id="S9.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding top-dimensional stratum of <math id="S9.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the flow box</p>
<table id="S9.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathcal{Y}}\coloneqq\left\{\varphi^{t}(p)\mid p\in C\kern 5.0pt\text{and}\kern 5.0ptt\in(0,\tau(p))\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathcal{Y}}\coloneqq\left\{\varphi^{t}(p)\mid p\in C\kern 5.0pt\text{and}\kern 5.0ptt\in(0,\tau(p))\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude this subsection by constructing exhaustive transverse surfaces <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> which are in good position in a precise quantitative sense. Let <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> be an auxiliary Riemannian metric on <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> has unit norm. Given a small positive number <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a point <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the transverse geodesic disk <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(p,r)\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(p,r)\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S9.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(p,r)\coloneqq\left\{\exp_{p}(v)\mid v\in T_{p}Y,\kern 5.0ptv\perp R_{p},\kern 5.0pt|v|\leq r\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>exp</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>⟂</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(p,r)\coloneqq\left\{\exp_{p}(v)\mid v\in T_{p}Y,\kern 5.0ptv\perp R_{p},\kern 5.0pt|v|\leq r\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a small positive number. If <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small, we can build an exhaustive transverse surface <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> by taking the disjoint union of finitely many transverse geodesic disks of radius <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. By adding more disks if necessary, we can make sure that <math id="S9.SS1.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\tau\leq\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\tau\leq\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. In the following we assume that this is the case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Now consider an arbitrary point <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> and choose an isometric identification <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta:\mathbb{R}^{2}\cong\langle R_{p}\rangle^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta:\mathbb{R}^{2}\cong\langle R_{p}\rangle^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the embedding</p>
<table id="S9.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:(-10,10)\times B(0,10)\rightarrow Y\qquad\psi(t,z)\coloneqq\varphi^{\rho t}(\exp_{p}(\rho z))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>exp</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:(-10,10)\times B(0,10)\rightarrow Y\qquad\psi(t,z)\coloneqq\varphi^{\rho t}(\exp_{p}(\rho z))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(0,10)\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(0,10)\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ball of radius <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Riemannian metric on <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-10,10)\times B(0,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-10,10)\times B(0,10)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by restricting the standard metric on <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, define the vector field <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{0}\coloneqq\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}\coloneqq\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is the coordinate of the factor <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-10,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-10,10)</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we have</p>
<table id="S9.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\psi^{*}R=R_{0}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\rho^{-2}\psi^{*}g,g_{0})=O(\rho)\quad(\rho\rightarrow 0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2.167em;" width="2.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\psi^{*}R=R_{0}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\rho^{-2}\psi^{*}g,g_{0})=O(\rho)\quad(\rho\rightarrow 0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p10.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset(-10,10)\times B(0,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset(-10,10)\times B(0,10)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the preimage under <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> of all disk components of <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> which are fully contained in the image of <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is small, each component of <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to a disk of the form <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{t\right\}\times\overline{B}(z,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{t\right\}\times\overline{B}(z,1)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,z)\in(-10,10)\times B(0,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,z)\in(-10,10)\times B(0,10)</annotation></semantics></math>. This <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> distance is controlled by <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and goes to zero as <math id="S9.SS1.p10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> tends to zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive number. We say that the surface <math id="S9.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S9.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-good position</span> if the following is true:</p>
<ol id="S9.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S9.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S9.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S9.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\tau\geq\delta\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\tau\geq\delta\rho</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S9.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:(-10,10)\times B(0,10)\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:(-10,10)\times B(0,10)\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be an embedding and <math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset(-10,10)\times B(0,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset(-10,10)\times B(0,10)</annotation></semantics></math> a surface as defined above for an arbitrary choice of point <math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> and identification <math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}\cong\langle R_{p}\rangle^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}\cong\langle R_{p}\rangle^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}:(-10,10)\times B(0,10)\rightarrow B(0,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>pr</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}:(-10,10)\times B(0,10)\rightarrow B(0,10)</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection onto the second factor. Consider the collection of circles <math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{pr}(\partial D)\subset B(0,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{pr}(\partial D)\subset B(0,10)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> ranges over all disk components of <math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Then this collection of circles does not have any tangency or triple intersection points. Moreover, the distance between any two disjoint circles and the distance between any two distinct intersection points is at least <math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">It is not hard to see that if the centers of the disk components of <math id="S9.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> are chosen generically, then <math id="S9.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S9.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-good position for all sufficiently small <math id="S9.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-good position. Given <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be the collection of transverse geodesic disks obtained from <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> by increasing the radius of every disk component from <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\varepsilon)\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\varepsilon)\rho</annotation></semantics></math> while leaving the center fixed. If <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small, then the pair <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,\Sigma^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,\Sigma^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to a stratification <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> as described above. We can choose <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> to only depend on <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, for example <math id="S9.SS1.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon(\delta)\coloneqq\delta^{2}/100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon(\delta)\coloneqq\delta^{2}/100</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.1</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a universal constant <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> with the following property. Let <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold, <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> a nowhere vanishing vector field on <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> a Riemannian metric such that <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> has unit norm. Then, for all sufficiently small <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an exhaustive transverse surface <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math> constructed as above as the disjoint union of finitely many transverse geodesic disks of radius <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-good position and such that <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\tau\leq\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\tau\leq\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof of the lemma is based on the following simple observation. For every <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a constant <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{\prime}=\delta^{\prime}(\delta)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{\prime}=\delta^{\prime}(\delta)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following is true for every Riemannian metric <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-10,10)\times B(0,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-10,10)\times B(0,10)</annotation></semantics></math> which is sufficiently <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a surface <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset(-10,10)\times B(0,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset(-10,10)\times B(0,10)</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of finitely many disjoint, transverse (with respect to the vector field <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>) geodesic disks of radius <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-good position. By this we mean that the flow line of the vector field <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> starting at any point in <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> can only meet <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> again after time at least <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and that the projections of the boundary circles of <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(0,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(0,10)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the properties in item (2) above. Then there exists a surface <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> by adding disjoint transverse geodesic disks of radius <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-good position and such that, for every point <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,z)\in(-5,5)\times B(0,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,z)\in(-5,5)\times B(0,5)</annotation></semantics></math>, the flow line of the vector field <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> starting at <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,z)</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> within time strictly less than <math id="S9.SS1.p14.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be small. Note that there exist a universal postive integer <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> independent of any of the other quantities involved, a finite set <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> of embeddings <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:(-10,10)\times B(0,10)\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:(-10,10)\times B(0,10)\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> defined as above for various choices of points <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> and trivializations <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, and an <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring of <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> such that the images of any two distinct embeddings of the same color are disjoint and such that the union over all <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> of the sets <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi((-5,5)\times B(0,5))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi((-5,5)\times B(0,5))</annotation></semantics></math> covers <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi=\Psi_{1}\sqcup\cdots\sqcup\Psi_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊔</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi=\Psi_{1}\sqcup\cdots\sqcup\Psi_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be the partition of <math id="S9.SS1.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the coloring.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}\coloneqq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}\coloneqq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq N</annotation></semantics></math>, recursively define <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}\coloneqq\delta_{i-1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}\coloneqq\delta_{i-1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i-1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i-1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> via the above observation. We claim that the assertion of the lemma holds with <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\coloneqq\delta_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\coloneqq\delta_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{0}\coloneqq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{0}\coloneqq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is clearly in <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-good position. Since the images of any two distinct embeddings in <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint, we can apply the above observation for each such embedding separately and obtain a surface <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of disjoint transverse geodesic disks of radius <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> which is in <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-good position and has the property that <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Sigma_{1}}(p)\leq\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Sigma_{1}}(p)\leq\rho</annotation></semantics></math> for all points <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in the union <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{\psi\in\Psi_{1}}\psi((-5,5)\times B(0,5))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{\psi\in\Psi_{1}}\psi((-5,5)\times B(0,5))</annotation></semantics></math>. We repeat the same step for all embeddings <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in\Psi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in\Psi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and obtain a surface <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-good position such that <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Sigma_{2}}(p)\leq\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Sigma_{2}}(p)\leq\rho</annotation></semantics></math> for all points <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{\psi\in\Psi_{1}\cup\Psi_{2}}\psi((-5,5)\times B(0,5))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{\psi\in\Psi_{1}\cup\Psi_{2}}\psi((-5,5)\times B(0,5))</annotation></semantics></math>. We iterate this step for all colors. Then the resulting surface <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\coloneqq\Sigma_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\coloneqq\Sigma_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-good position and satisfies <math id="S9.SS1.p16.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\tau\leq\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\tau\leq\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.2. </span>Stratification of thin products</h3>

<div id="S9.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold. Assume that <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> for some closed <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq s_{-}&lt;s_{+}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq s_{-}&lt;s_{+}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and assume that the difference <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\coloneqq s_{+}-s_{-}\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\coloneqq s_{+}-s_{-}\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math> is small. In order to prove Lemma <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 8.1. ‣ 8. Proof of ellipsoid embedding stability ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>, we need to construct a special stratification <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[s_{-},s_{+}]\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[s_{-},s_{+}]\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> whose top-dimensional strata are symplectic cuboids and perturbed symplectic frusta with cuts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Hamiltonian vector field induced by the Hamiltonian</p>
<table id="S9.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:M=[0,1]\times Y\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad H(s,p)\coloneqq-s." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:M=[0,1]\times Y\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\qquad H(s,p)\coloneqq-s.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be a compatible almost complex structure on <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J\partial_{s}=R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\partial_{s}=R</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the coordinate of the first factor of <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Riemannian metric induced by <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let us fix a constant <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> as in Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 9.1. ‣ 9.1. Stratifications compatible with a flow ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>. If <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small, we may apply Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 9.1. ‣ 9.1. Stratifications compatible with a flow ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a> with <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\coloneqq\sigma/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\coloneqq\sigma/10</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with the restrictions of <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the resulting exhaustive transverse surface in <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-good position. Moreover, let <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be the surface obtained from <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> by increasing the radii of all disk components from <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\varepsilon)\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\varepsilon)\rho</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\coloneqq\varepsilon(\delta)=\delta^{2}/100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\coloneqq\varepsilon(\delta)=\delta^{2}/100</annotation></semantics></math> as in Subsection <a href="#S9.SS1" title="9.1. Stratifications compatible with a flow ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>. We emphasize that <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed universal constants. In particular, they do not change if we shrink <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the stratifications of <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times Y</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, induced by the pair of surfaces <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,\Sigma^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,\Sigma^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that the sets of top-dimensional strata of <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> are in bijective correspondence. The set of top-dimensional strata of the stratification <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[s_{-},s_{+}]\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[s_{-},s_{+}]\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> we are about to construct will also be in bijective correspondence with these two sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a point <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> and a unitary trivialization</p>
<table id="S9.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta:\mathbb{C}\cong\langle\partial_{s},R_{(s_{-},p)}\rangle^{\perp}\subset T_{(\sigma_{-},p)}M." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta:\mathbb{C}\cong\langle\partial_{s},R_{(s_{-},p)}\rangle^{\perp}\subset T_{(\sigma_{-},p)}M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Given such a tuple <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>, we define an embedding</p>
<table id="S9.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\rightarrow[s_{-},s_{+}]\times Y" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\rightarrow[s_{-},s_{+}]\times Y</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">as follows: First, we define <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(0,0,z)\coloneqq(s_{-},\exp_{p}(\sigma\eta(z)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>exp</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(0,0,z)\coloneqq(s_{-},\exp_{p}(\sigma\eta(z)))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the exponential map of the restriction of the metric <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times Y\cong Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times Y\cong Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the hypersurface</p>
<table id="S9.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\coloneqq[s_{-},s_{+}]\times D(p,2\sigma)\subset[s_{-},s_{+}]\times Y," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\coloneqq[s_{-},s_{+}]\times D(p,2\sigma)\subset[s_{-},s_{+}]\times Y,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(p,2\sigma)\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(p,2\sigma)\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math> is the transverse geodesic disk defined via the same exponential map. The symplectic form <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> induces a characteristic foliation on <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. At the point <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{-},p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{-},p)</annotation></semantics></math>, this characteristic foliation is tangent to <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small, the characteristic folation is close to being tangent to <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> everywhere on <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the characteristic leaf starting at a point <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{-},q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{-},q)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in D(p,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in D(p,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is a small perturbation of the line segment <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[s_{-},s_{+}]\times\left\{q\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[s_{-},s_{+}]\times\left\{q\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(s,0,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(s,0,z)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the unique intersection point of the characteristic leaf starting at the point <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(0,0,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(0,0,z)</annotation></semantics></math> and the slice <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{s_{-}+s\sigma\right\}\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{s_{-}+s\sigma\right\}\times Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we define <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(s,t,z)\coloneqq\varphi^{\sigma t}(\psi(s,0,z))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(s,t,z)\coloneqq\varphi^{\sigma t}(\psi(s,0,z))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the flow induced by <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.2</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the standard area form on <math id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and let us define the area form <math id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> by</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega\coloneqq\sigma^{-2}\psi(0,0,\cdot)^{*}\omega." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>ψ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\coloneqq\sigma^{-2}\psi(0,0,\cdot)^{*}\omega.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then we have</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}\omega=\Omega+ds\wedge dt\qquad\text{and}\qquad\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\Omega,\Omega_{0})=O(\sigma)\quad(\sigma\rightarrow 0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:2.167em;" width="2.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}\omega=\Omega+ds\wedge dt\qquad\text{and}\qquad\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\Omega,\Omega_{0})=O(\sigma)\quad(\sigma\rightarrow 0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is immediate from the construction of <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> that the characteristic foliation on <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to the vector field <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that</span></p>
<table id="S9.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\psi^{*}\omega|_{[0,1]\times\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1)}=\psi(0,0,\cdot)^{*}\omega." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>ω</mi><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>ψ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}\omega|_{[0,1]\times\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1)}=\psi(0,0,\cdot)^{*}\omega.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly, it follows from the construction of <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> that the characteristic foliation on <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{s\right\}\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{s\right\}\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to the vector field <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude that</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\psi^{*}\omega|_{\left\{s\right\}\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)}=\psi(0,0,\cdot)^{*}\omega." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>ω</mi><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>ψ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}\omega|_{\left\{s\right\}\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)}=\psi(0,0,\cdot)^{*}\omega.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">This shows that we can write</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}\omega=\Omega+ds\wedge\alpha_{s}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}\omega=\Omega+ds\wedge\alpha_{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{s})_{s\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{s})_{s\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-forms on <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,1)\times B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,1)\times B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that (<a href="#S9.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 9.2. Stratification of thin products ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>) implies that the restriction of <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes. We have</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=d(\Omega+ds\wedge\alpha_{s})=ds\wedge d\alpha_{s}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=d(\Omega+ds\wedge\alpha_{s})=ds\wedge d\alpha_{s}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">This implies that <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-form. We can therefore write <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{s}=du_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{s}=du_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> for a function <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}:(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}:(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The function <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely determined after imposing the normalization <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}(0,0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}(0,0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that since the restriction of <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, we then have <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}(0,z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}(0,z)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the Hamiltonian vector field of the Hamiltonian <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-1}\psi^{*}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-1}\psi^{*}H</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the symplectic form <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\psi^{*}R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\psi^{*}R</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, note that</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\sigma^{-1}\psi^{*}H)(s,t,z)=-s-\sigma^{-1}\sigma_{-}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\sigma\psi^{*}R=\partial_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>σ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mtext>and</mtext><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma^{-1}\psi^{*}H)(s,t,z)=-s-\sigma^{-1}\sigma_{-}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\sigma\psi^{*}R=\partial_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p6.6.1" class="ltx_text">This implies that <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m27" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{s}(\partial_{t})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{s}(\partial_{t})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude that <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}(t,z)=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}(t,z)=t</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to prove that <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\Omega,\Omega_{0})=O(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\Omega,\Omega_{0})=O(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> tends to zero. Let us write <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> to indicate the dependence of <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;r\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;r\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, define the map <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{r}:B(0,1)\rightarrow B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{r}:B(0,1)\rightarrow B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{r}(z)\coloneqq rz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{r}(z)\coloneqq rz</annotation></semantics></math>. A simple computation shows that</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{r\sigma}=r^{-2}S_{r}^{*}\Omega_{\sigma}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{r\sigma}=r^{-2}S_{r}^{*}\Omega_{\sigma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> at the center of <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, this implies that <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\Omega_{r\sigma},\Omega_{0})=O(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\Omega_{r\sigma},\Omega_{0})=O(r)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> tends to zero.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.3</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi,\psi^{\prime}:[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\rightarrow[s_{-},s_{+}]\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi,\psi^{\prime}:[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\rightarrow[s_{-},s_{+}]\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the embeddings induced by two choices of points and unitary trivializations <math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p^{\prime},\eta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p^{\prime},\eta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Assume that the intersection <math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\coloneqq\operatorname{im}(\psi)\cap\operatorname{im}(\psi^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\coloneqq\operatorname{im}(\psi)\cap\operatorname{im}(\psi^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is non-empty and consider the transition map</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi\coloneqq\psi^{\prime-1}\circ\psi:\psi^{-1}(U)\rightarrow\psi^{\prime-1}(U)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi\coloneqq\psi^{\prime-1}\circ\psi:\psi^{-1}(U)\rightarrow\psi^{\prime-1}(U).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then there exist a translation <math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and an orientation preserving affine isometry <math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{R}^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{R}^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\chi,(\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{R}}\times f\times g)|_{\psi^{-1}(U)})=O(\sigma)\quad(\sigma\rightarrow 0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\chi,(\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{R}}\times f\times g)|_{\psi^{-1}(U)})=O(\sigma)\quad(\sigma\rightarrow 0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Riemannian metric on <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by restricting the standard metric on <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us show that <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}g,g_{0})=O(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}(\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}g,g_{0})=O(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> tends to zero. For <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;r\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;r\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the embedding associated to <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>, but with the interval <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[s_{-},s_{+}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[s_{-},s_{+}]</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[s_{-},s_{-}+r(s_{+}-s_{-})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[s_{-},s_{-}+r(s_{+}-s_{-})]</annotation></semantics></math>. Define a map</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{r}:[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\rightarrow[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\qquad S_{r}(s,t,z)\coloneqq(rs,rt,rz)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{r}:[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\rightarrow[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\qquad S_{r}(s,t,z)\coloneqq(rs,rt,rz).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">It is straightforward to check that <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{r}=\psi\circ S_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{r}=\psi\circ S_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence that</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\sigma)^{-2}\psi_{r}^{*}g=r^{-2}S_{r}^{*}(\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}g)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\sigma)^{-2}\psi_{r}^{*}g=r^{-2}S_{r}^{*}(\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}g).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">Note that <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}g</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> at the point <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that <math id="S9.SS2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}((r\sigma)^{-2}\psi_{r}^{*}g,g_{0})=O(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}_{C^{\infty}}((r\sigma)^{-2}\psi_{r}^{*}g,g_{0})=O(r)</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">We observe that the transition map <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> is an isometry with respect to the metric <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-2}\psi^{*}g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{-1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{-1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> and the metric <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-2}(\psi^{\prime})^{*}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-2}(\psi^{\prime})^{*}g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi^{\prime})^{-1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi^{\prime})^{-1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>. Both of these metrics have <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> distance <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by the above. Moreover, by definition of <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the coordinate of the first factor of <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and we have <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\chi_{*}\partial_{t}=\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{*}\partial_{t}=\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining these observations, we see that <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> must have <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> distance <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> from a map of the form <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{R}}\times f\times g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{R}}\times f\times g</annotation></semantics></math> for a translation <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and an affine isometry <math id="S9.SS2.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For each disk component <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times D(p,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times D(p,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, pick an arbitrary unitary trivialization <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta:\mathbb{C}\cong\langle\partial_{s},R_{(s_{-},p)}\rangle^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta:\mathbb{C}\cong\langle\partial_{s},R_{(s_{-},p)}\rangle^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{D}:[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\rightarrow[\sigma_{-},\sigma_{+}]\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{D}:[0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1)\rightarrow[\sigma_{-},\sigma_{+}]\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the embedding associated to <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{D}^{-1}(D)=\left\{0\right\}\times\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1/10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{D}^{-1}(D)=\left\{0\right\}\times\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1/10)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, it follows from Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 9.3. ‣ 9.2. Stratification of thin products ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.3</span></a> that, for every disk component <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> which intersects the image of <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist a point <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,z)\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,z)\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and a perturbation <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> of the disk <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{0\right\}\times\left\{t\right\}\times B(z,1/10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{0\right\}\times\left\{t\right\}\times B(z,1/10)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> size <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{D}^{-1}(D^{\prime})=E\cap([0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{D}^{-1}(D^{\prime})=E\cap([0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1))</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us assume that <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small such that the <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> size of the perturbation is much smaller than <math id="S9.SS2.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Let us define the auxiliary hypersurface <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{D}\coloneqq\psi_{D}([0,1]\times\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{D}\coloneqq\psi_{D}([0,1]\times\left\{0\right\}\times B(0,1))</annotation></semantics></math>. Again, it follows from Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 9.3. ‣ 9.2. Stratification of thin products ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.3</span></a> that, for each disk component <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{D^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{D^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects the image of <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,z)\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,z)\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and a perturbation <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\times\left\{t\right\}\times B(z,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\times\left\{t\right\}\times B(z,1)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> size <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{D}^{-1}(W_{D^{\prime}})=V\cap([0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{D}^{-1}(W_{D^{\prime}})=V\cap([0,1]\times(-1,1)\times B(0,1))</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small such that the <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> size of the perturbation is much smaller than <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. In the following, we perturb the hypersurfaces <math id="S9.SS2.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> while preserving this property.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a top-dimensional stratum <math id="S9.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of type (S<a href="#S9.I2.i1" title="item 1 ‣ 9.1. Stratifications compatible with a flow ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). A neighbourhood of the corresponding top-dimensional stratum <math id="S9.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S9.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{s_{-}\right\}\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> is depicted in Figure <a href="#S9.F3" title="Figure 9.3 ‣ 9.2. Stratification of thin products ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.3</span></a>. The closure <math id="S9.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{C}_{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{C}_{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects four disk components of <math id="S9.SS2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. We abbreviate them by <math id="S9.SS2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D,D_{1},D_{2},D_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D,D_{1},D_{2},D_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> as indicated in Figure <a href="#S9.F3" title="Figure 9.3 ‣ 9.2. Stratification of thin products ‣ 9. Proof of the stratification lemma ‣ Packing stability and the subleading asymptotics of symplectic Weyl laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
